  p1-l1  [137 797 458 807]  {NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal 9 i}Proceedings of the 61st Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics
  p1-l2  [226 808 369 819]  {NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal 9 i}Volume 2: Short Papers{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}, pages 352–365
  p1-l3  [179 819 417 829]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}July 9-14, 2023 ©2023 Association for Computational Linguistics
  p1-l4  [124 76 472 95]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 14.3 b}PLUE: Language Understanding Evaluation Benchmark
  p1-l5  [206 92 389 111]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 14.3 b}for Privacy Policies in English
  p1-l6  [120 131 197 146]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}Jianfeng Chi{CMR8 8}1{CMMI8 8 i},{CMR8 8}2
  p1-l7  [209 131 384 146]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}Wasi Uddin Ahmad{CMR8 8}3{CMSY8 8 i}∗{NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b} Yuan Tian{CMR8 8}3
  p1-l8  [397 131 480 146]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}Kai-Wei Chang{CMR8 8}3
  p1-l9  [123 145 475 159]  {CMR8 8}1{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 12}Meta AI,{CMR8 8} 2{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 12}University of Virginia,{CMR8 8} 3{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 12}University of California, Los Angeles
  p1-l10  [135 160 464 172]  {Inconsolatazi4-Regular 12}jianfengchi@meta.com,{wasiahmad,yuant,kwchang}@ucla.edu
  p1-l11  [158 212 202 228]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}Abstract
  p1-l12  [88 236 274 248]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}Privacy policies provide individuals with infor-
  p1-l13  [88 248 272 260]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}mation about their rights and how their personal
  p1-l14  [88 260 274 272]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}information is handled. Natural language un-
  p1-l15  [88 272 272 284]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}derstanding (NLU) technologies can support
  p1-l16  [88 284 274 296]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}individuals and practitioners to understand bet-
  p1-l17  [88 296 272 308]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}ter privacy practices described in lengthy and
  p1-l18  [88 308 274 320]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}complex documents. However, existing ef-
  p1-l19  [88 320 272 332]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}forts that use NLU technologies are limited
  p1-l20  [88 331 272 343]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}by processing the language in a way exclusive
  p1-l21  [88 343 272 355]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}to a single task focusing on certain privacy
  p1-l22  [88 355 274 367]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}practices. To this end, we introduce the Pri-
  p1-l23  [88 367 274 379]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}vacy Policy Language Understanding Evalua-
  p1-l24  [88 379 274 391]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}tion (PLUE) benchmark, a multi-task bench-
  p1-l25  [88 391 274 403]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}mark for evaluating the privacy policy lan-
  p1-l26  [88 403 272 415]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}guage understanding across various tasks. We
  p1-l27  [88 415 272 427]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}also collect a large corpus of privacy policies
  p1-l28  [88 427 274 439]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}to enable privacy policy domain-specific lan-
  p1-l29  [88 439 272 451]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}guage model pre-training. We evaluate several
  p1-l30  [88 451 274 463]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}generic pre-trained language models and con-
  p1-l31  [88 463 274 475]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}tinue pre-training them on the collected corpus.
  p1-l32  [87 475 272 487]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}We demonstrate that domain-specific continual
  p1-l33  [88 487 272 499]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}pre-training offers performance improvements
  p1-l34  [88 499 274 511]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}across all tasks. The code and models are re-
  p1-l35  [88 511 272 523]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}leased at{Inconsolatazi4-Regular 10} https://github.com/JFChi/PLUE{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}.
  p1-l36  [71 532 154 548]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}1 Introduction
  p1-l37  [71 554 289 567]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Privacy policies are documents that outline how a
  p1-l38  [71 568 289 581]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}company or organization collects, uses, shares, and
  p1-l39  [71 581 289 595]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}protects individuals’ personal information. Without
  p1-l40  [71 595 291 608]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}a clear understanding of privacy policies, individu-
  p1-l41  [71 608 289 622]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}als may not know how their personal information is
  p1-l42  [71 622 291 635]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}being used or who it is being shared with. The pri-
  p1-l43  [71 636 291 649]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}vacy violation might cause potential harm to them.
  p1-l44  [71 649 291 662]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}However, privacy policies are lengthy and complex,
  p1-l45  [71 663 289 676]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}prohibiting users from reading and understanding
  p1-l46  [71 676 289 689]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}them in detail (Commission et al., 2012; Gluck
  p1-l47  [71 690 233 703]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}et al., 2016; Marotta-Wurgler, 2015).
  p1-l48  [82 703 290 716]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Various natural language understanding (NLU)
  p1-l49  [71 717 291 730]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}technologies have recently been developed to un-
  p1-l50  [71 730 290 744]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}derstand privacy policies (Wilson et al., 2016a;
  p1-l51  [71 744 290 757]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Harkous et al., 2018; Ravichander et al., 2019;
  p1-l52  [87 763 191 774]  {CMSY6 6 i}∗{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}Work done while at UCLA.
  p1-l53  [306 213 524 227]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Ahmad et al., 2020; Parvez et al., 2022; Ahmad
  p1-l54  [306 227 524 240]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}et al., 2021; Bui et al., 2021). These tasks focus
  p1-l55  [306 241 526 254]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}on understanding specific privacy practices at dif-
  p1-l56  [306 254 526 267]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ferent syntax or semantics levels and require sig-
  p1-l57  [306 268 524 281]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}nificant effort for data annotations (e.g., domain
  p1-l58  [306 281 524 294]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}experts). It is hard to develop generic pre-trained
  p1-l59  [306 295 525 308]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}language models (e.g., BERT (Devlin et al., 2019))
  p1-l60  [306 308 526 321]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}with task-specific fine-tuning using limited anno-
  p1-l61  [306 322 525 335]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}tated data. Besides, the unique characteristics of
  p1-l62  [306 335 525 349]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}privacy policies, such as reasoning over ambiguity
  p1-l63  [306 349 524 362]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}and vagueness, modality, and document structure
  p1-l64  [306 363 524 376]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(Ravichander et al., 2021), make it challenging to
  p1-l65  [306 376 524 389]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}directly apply generic pre-trained language models
  p1-l66  [306 390 433 403]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}to the privacy policy domain.
  p1-l67  [317 403 526 416]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}To address these problems and encourage re-
  p1-l68  [306 417 526 430]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}search to develop NLU technologies in the pri-
  p1-l69  [306 430 526 444]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}vacy policy domain, we introduce the{NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b} P{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}rivacy Pol-
  p1-l70  [306 444 525 458]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}icy{NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b} L{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}anguage{NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b} U{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}nderstanding{NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b} E{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}valuation (PLUE)
  p1-l71  [306 458 524 471]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}benchmark, to evaluate the privacy policy language
  p1-l72  [306 471 526 484]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}understanding across six tasks, including text clas-
  p1-l73  [306 485 526 498]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}sification, question answering, semantic parsing,
  p1-l74  [306 498 524 511]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}and named-entity recognition. PLUE also includes
  p1-l75  [306 512 524 525]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}a pre-training privacy policy corpus that we crawl
  p1-l76  [306 525 526 538]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}from the websites to enable privacy policy domain-
  p1-l77  [306 539 524 552]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}specific language model pre-training. We use this
  p1-l78  [306 552 526 566]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}corpus to pre-train BERT (Devlin et al., 2019),
  p1-l79  [306 566 526 579]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}RoBERTa (Liu et al., 2019), Electra (Clark et al.,
  p1-l80  [306 579 526 593]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}2020), and SpanBERT (Joshi et al., 2020) and fine-
  p1-l81  [306 593 526 606]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}tune them on the downstream tasks. We demon-
  p1-l82  [306 607 524 620]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}strate that domain-specific continual pre-training
  p1-l83  [306 620 526 633]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}offers performance improvements across all tasks.
  p1-l84  [306 634 524 647]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}We will release the benchmark to assist natural
  p1-l85  [306 647 526 660]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}language processing (NLP) researchers and practi-
  p1-l86  [306 661 431 674]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}tioners in future exploration.
  p1-l87  [306 684 488 699]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}2 Policy Language Understanding
  p1-l88  [324 698 485 713]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}Evaluation (PLUE) Benchmark
  p1-l89  [306 720 526 733]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}PLUE is centered on six English privacy policy lan-
  p1-l90  [306 734 524 747]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}guage understanding tasks. The datasets and tasks
  p1-l91  [306 747 525 761]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}are selected based on the following principles: (1)
  p1-l92  [306 761 524 774]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}usefulness: the selected tasks can help practitioners
  p1-l93  [291 781 307 794]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}352
