  p2-l59  [198 197 397 209]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}Table 1: Statistics of the PLUE datasets and tasks.
  p2-l60  [71 225 289 238]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}in the domain quickly understand privacy practices
  p2-l61  [70 238 289 252]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}without reading the whole privacy policy; (2) task
  p2-l62  [71 252 291 265]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}diversity: the selected tasks focus on different se-
  p2-l63  [71 266 289 279]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}mantic levels, e.g., words (phrases), sentences, and
  p2-l64  [71 279 289 292]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}paragraphs; (3) task difficulty: the selected tasks
  p2-l65  [71 293 289 306]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}should be adequately challenging for more room
  p2-l66  [71 306 289 319]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}for improvement; (4) training efficiency: all tasks
  p2-l67  [71 320 290 333]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}can be trainable on a single moderate GPU (e.g.,
  p2-l68  [71 333 290 346]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}GeForce GTX 1080 Ti) for no more than ten hours;
  p2-l69  [70 347 289 360]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(5) accessibility: all datasets are publicly available
  p2-l70  [71 360 289 374]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}under licenses that allow usage and redistribution
  p2-l71  [71 374 167 387]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}for research purposes.
  p2-l72  [71 396 84 410]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}2.1
  p2-l73  [95 396 184 410]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}Datasets and Tasks
  p2-l74  [71 414 291 427]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}PLUE includes six tasks in four categories. Ta-
  p2-l75  [71 428 289 441]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ble 1 presents an overview of the datasets and tasks
  p2-l76  [70 441 289 454]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}within PLUE, and Table 4 in the Appendix gives
  p2-l77  [71 455 182 468]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}an example for each task.
  p2-l78  [71 471 114 485]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}OPP-115
  p2-l79  [124 471 289 484]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Wilson et al. (2016a) presented 115
  p2-l80  [71 484 289 498]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Online Privacy Policies (OPP-115). The dataset
  p2-l81  [71 498 291 511]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}comprises website privacy policies with text seg-
  p2-l82  [71 512 289 525]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ments annotated with one or more privacy practices
  p2-l83  [71 525 289 538]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}from ten categories (see Appendix A.1). We train a
  p2-l84  [71 539 289 552]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}multi-label classifier to predict the privacy practices
  p2-l85  [71 552 252 565]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}given a sentence from a policy document.
  p2-l86  [71 568 112 582]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}APP-350
  p2-l87  [123 569 291 582]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Zimmeck et al. (2019) presented APP-
  p2-l88  [71 582 291 595]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}350, a collection of mobile application privacy poli-
  p2-l89  [71 596 289 609]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}cies annotating what types of users’ data mobile
  p2-l90  [71 609 289 622]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}applications collect or share. Like OPP-115, each
  p2-l91  [71 623 289 636]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}text segment in a policy document is annotated
  p2-l92  [70 636 291 649]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}with zero or more privacy practices (listed in Ap-
  p2-l93  [71 650 289 663]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}pendix A.2). In total, there are 30 data-type-related
  p2-l94  [71 663 290 677]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}classes in APP-350, and we assign one more class,
  p2-l95  [71 677 289 690]  {Inconsolatazi4-Regular 10.9}No_Mention{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}, to those text segments that do not
  p2-l96  [71 690 179 704]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}pertain to such practices.
  p2-l97  [71 706 122 721]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}PrivacyQA
  p2-l98  [133 707 289 720]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Ravichander et al. (2019) proposed a
  p2-l99  [71 720 289 733]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}question-answering dataset, PrivacyQA, comprised
  p2-l100  [71 734 289 747]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}of 35 mobile application privacy policies. Given
  p2-l101  [71 747 289 761]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}a question from a mobile application user and a
  p2-l102  [71 761 289 774]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}sentence from a privacy policy, the task is to predict
  p2-l103  [306 225 526 238]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}whether the sentence is relevant to the question.
  p2-l104  [306 238 524 252]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}PrivacyQA includes unanswerable and subjective
  p2-l105  [306 252 525 265]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}questions and formulates the QA task as a binary
  p2-l106  [306 266 426 279]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}sentence classification task.
  p2-l107  [306 281 351 296]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}PolicyQA
  p2-l108  [362 282 526 295]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Ahmad et al. (2020) proposed a read-
  p2-l109  [306 295 524 309]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ing comprehension (Rajpurkar et al., 2016) style
  p2-l110  [306 309 524 322]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}dataset, PolicyQA. The dataset is derived from
  p2-l111  [306 322 526 336]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}OPP-115 annotations that include a set of fine-
  p2-l112  [306 336 526 349]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}grained attributes and evidence text spans that sup-
  p2-l113  [306 350 524 363]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}port the annotations. Considering the annotated
  p2-l114  [306 363 524 376]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}spans as the answer spans, PolicyQA generates
  p2-l115  [306 377 526 390]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}diverse questions relating to the corresponding pri-
  p2-l116  [306 390 524 403]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}vacy practices and attributes. The task is to predict
  p2-l117  [306 404 524 417]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}the answer text span given the corresponding text
  p2-l118  [306 417 403 430]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}segment and question.
  p2-l119  [306 433 345 447]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}PolicyIE
  p2-l120  [356 434 526 447]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Ahmad et al. (2021) proposed a seman-
  p2-l121  [306 447 524 460]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}tic parsing dataset composed of two tasks: intent
  p2-l122  [306 461 524 474]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}classification and slot filling. Given a sentence in a
  p2-l123  [306 474 524 487]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}privacy policy, the task is to predict the sentence’s
  p2-l124  [306 488 526 501]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}intent (i.e., privacy practice) and identify the seman-
  p2-l125  [306 501 526 515]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}tic concepts associated with the privacy practice.
  p2-l126  [306 515 526 528]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Based on the role of the slots in privacy practices,
  p2-l127  [306 528 526 542]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}PolicyIE groups them into type-I and type-II slots.
  p2-l128  [306 542 524 555]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}In total, there are four intent labels and 14 type-I
  p2-l129  [306 556 524 569]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}and four type-II slot labels. We individually train
  p2-l130  [306 569 524 582]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}a text classifier and sequence taggers to perform
  p2-l131  [306 583 516 596]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}intent classification and slot filling, respectively.
  p2-l132  [306 599 355 613]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}PI-Extract
  p2-l133  [366 599 526 612]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Bui et al. (2021) presented PI-Extract,
  p2-l134  [306 613 524 626]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}a named-entity recognition (NER) dataset. It aims
  p2-l135  [306 626 526 639]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}to identify what types of user data are (not) col-
  p2-l136  [306 640 526 653]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}lected or shared mentioned in the privacy policies.
  p2-l137  [306 653 526 666]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}It contains 4 types of named entities:{Inconsolatazi4-Regular 10.9} COLLECT{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9},
  p2-l138  [306 667 524 680]  {Inconsolatazi4-Regular 10.9}NOT_COLLECT{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9},{Inconsolatazi4-Regular 10.9} SHARE{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} and{Inconsolatazi4-Regular 10.9} NOT_SHARE{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}. Note that
  p2-l139  [306 680 526 693]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}the named entities of different types may overlap.
  p2-l140  [306 694 524 707]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Thus, we report the results for collection-related
  p2-l141  [306 707 475 720]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}and share-related entities, respectively.
  p2-l142  [306 729 320 744]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}2.2
  p2-l143  [331 729 475 744]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}Pre-training Corpus Collection
  p2-l144  [306 747 525 761]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}The existing pre-trained language models (PLMs)
  p2-l145  [306 761 526 774]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}mostly use data from BooksCorpus (Zhu et al.,
  p2-l146  [291 781 307 794]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}353
  == page 3  595 x 842  lines 105  pictures 0  drawings 2
  p3-l1  [71 72 289 85]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}2015) and English Wikipedia. Language models
  p3-l2  [71 85 289 98]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}pre-trained on text from those sources might not
  p3-l3  [71 99 291 112]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}perform well on the downstream privacy policy lan-
  p3-l4  [71 112 289 126]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}guage understanding tasks, as privacy policies are
  p3-l5  [71 126 290 139]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}composed of text written by domain experts (e.g.,
  p3-l6  [71 139 289 153]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}lawyers). Gururangan et al. (2020) suggested that
  p3-l7  [71 153 291 166]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}adapting to the domain’s unlabeled data (domain-
  p3-l8  [71 167 289 180]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}adaptive pre-training) improves the performance
  p3-l9  [71 180 289 193]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}of domain-specific tasks. Therefore, we collect
  p3-l10  [71 194 289 207]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}a large privacy policy corpus for language model
  p3-l11  [71 207 289 220]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}pre-training. In order to achieve broad coverage
  p3-l12  [71 221 289 234]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}across privacy practices written in privacy policies
  p3-l13  [70 234 289 247]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(William, 2020; Ahmad et al., 2021), we collect the
  p3-l14  [71 248 291 261]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}privacy policies from two sources: mobile applica-
  p3-l15  [71 261 291 275]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}tion privacy policies and website privacy policies.
  p3-l16  [70 275 289 288]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Appendix B provides more details about how we
  p3-l17  [71 289 256 302]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}collect these two types of privacy policies.
  p3-l18  [71 311 84 326]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}2.3
  p3-l19  [95 311 184 326]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}Models & Training
  p3-l20  [71 329 115 344]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}Baselines
  p3-l21  [126 330 289 343]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}We benchmark pre-trained language
  p3-l22  [71 343 290 357]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}models (PLMs), BERT (Devlin et al., 2019),
  p3-l23  [71 357 289 370]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}RoBERTa (Liu et al., 2019), SpanBERT (Joshi
  p3-l24  [71 370 289 384]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}et al., 2020), Electra (Clark et al., 2020), and
  p3-l25  [71 384 289 397]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}LEGAL-BERT (Chalkidis et al., 2020). We present
  p3-l26  [71 398 242 411]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}the details of the PLMs in Appendix C.
  p3-l27  [71 414 256 428]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}Domain-specific Continual Pre-training
  p3-l28  [267 414 291 427]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}In or-
  p3-l29  [71 428 290 441]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}der to adapt PLMs to the privacy policy domain,
  p3-l30  [70 441 290 454]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}we continue to train BERT, Electra, SpanBERT,
  p3-l31  [71 455 289 468]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}and RoBERTa on the pre-training corpus described
  p3-l32  [71 468 291 481]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}in Section 2.2. We refer to them as PP-BERT, PP-
  p3-l33  [71 482 291 495]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}RoBERTa, PP-SpanBERT, and PP-Electra, respec-
  p3-l34  [71 494 263 509]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}tively.{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 8}1{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} We present details in Appendix D.1.
  p3-l35  [71 512 191 526]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}Task-specific Fine-tuning
  p3-l36  [201 512 289 525]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}We fine-tune PLMs
  p3-l37  [71 525 289 539]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}for each PLUE task. We{NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal 10.9 i} only{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} tune the learning
  p3-l38  [71 539 290 552]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}rate for each task, as we found in the preliminary
  p3-l39  [71 553 290 566]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}experiments that model performances are highly
  p3-l40  [71 566 289 579]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}sensitive to the learning rate. We present more
  p3-l41  [71 580 178 593]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}details in Appendix D.2.
  p3-l42  [71 603 189 619]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}3 Experiment Results
  p3-l43  [71 626 291 639]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Tables 2 and 3 present the results for all the ex-
  p3-l44  [71 639 290 653]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}periment models for PLUE tasks. Rows 2-9 show
  p3-l45  [71 653 291 666]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}the results of the base PLMs and their correspond-
  p3-l46  [71 667 289 680]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ing variants with privacy policy domain-specific
  p3-l47  [71 680 289 693]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}continual pre-training. Similar to GLUE (Wang
  p3-l48  [71 694 289 707]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}et al., 2019), we also provide the average scores
  p3-l49  [71 707 291 720]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}of all PLUE tasks in the last column of Table 3.
  p3-l50  [70 721 290 734]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}We observe that the language models (PP-BERT,
  p3-l51  [84 742 289 754]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 6}1{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}We continually pre-train only the base models to mitigate
  p3-l52  [71 753 289 764]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}the environmental impact of our experiments, but our code
  p3-l53  [71 763 251 774]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}supports continual pre-training of large PLMs too.
  p3-l54  [306 72 524 85]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}PP-SpanBERT, PP-Electra, PP-RoBERTa) adapted
  p3-l55  [306 85 526 98]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}to the privacy policy domain outperform the gen-
  p3-l56  [306 99 526 112]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}eral language models consistently in all the tasks,
  p3-l57  [306 112 524 126]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}and PP-RoBERTa performs the best among all base
  p3-l58  [306 126 524 139]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}models in terms of the average scores of all PLUE
  p3-l59  [306 139 524 153]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}tasks. In particular, PP-RoBERTa performs the
  p3-l60  [306 152 526 166]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}best for OPP-115, APP-350, PrivacyQA,{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 8}2{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} and PI-
  p3-l61  [306 167 524 180]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Extract, among all base models. PP-BERT and
  p3-l62  [306 180 526 193]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}PP-RoBERTa perform the best for PolicyQA; PP-
  p3-l63  [306 194 526 207]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Electra and PP-RoBERTa achieve the best perfor-
  p3-l64  [306 207 525 220]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}mance for PolicyIE. In contrast, LEGAL-BERT
  p3-l65  [306 221 526 234]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(row 10) performs comparably or shows moder-
  p3-l66  [306 234 526 247]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ate improvements over BERT, indicating that pre-
  p3-l67  [306 248 526 261]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}training on the general legal corpus does not neces-
  p3-l68  [306 261 526 275]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}sarily help privacy policy language understanding.
  p3-l69  [317 275 524 289]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}It is interesting to see that continual pre-training
  p3-l70  [306 289 525 302]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}of the language models using the privacy policy
  p3-l71  [306 303 526 316]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}domain data benefits them differently. For exam-
  p3-l72  [306 316 524 329]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ple, in the text classification tasks (i.e., OPP-115
  p3-l73  [306 330 524 343]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}and APP-350), the performance difference between
  p3-l74  [306 343 526 356]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}SpanBERT and PP-SpanBERT are most significant,
  p3-l75  [306 357 525 370]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}while models using MLM (BERT and RoBERTa)
  p3-l76  [306 370 524 383]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}already shows relatively high performance before
  p3-l77  [306 384 524 397]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}continual pre-training and continual pre-training
  p3-l78  [306 397 511 411]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}brings moderate gains to BERT and RoBERTa.
  p3-l79  [317 411 524 424]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}We further investigate the improvement of large
  p3-l80  [306 425 524 438]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}variants of PLMs over base variants of PLMs on
  p3-l81  [306 438 524 452]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}PLUE tasks. Since PP-RoBERTa{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 7}BASE{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} performs the
  p3-l82  [306 452 526 465]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}best among all base models, we also continue pre-
  p3-l83  [306 466 524 479]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}train RoBERTa{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 7}LARGE{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} (PP-RoBERTa{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 7}LARGE{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}). As
  p3-l84  [306 479 526 492]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}shown in the last five rows in Tables 2 and 3,
  p3-l85  [306 493 526 506]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}the large pre-trained language models mostly out-
  p3-l86  [306 506 526 519]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}perform their base counterparts. Noticeably, PP-
  p3-l87  [306 520 524 533]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}RoBERTa{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 7}LARGE{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} is the best-performing model in
  p3-l88  [306 533 524 546]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}APP-350, PolicyQA, PI-Extract, and sub-tasks in
  p3-l89  [306 547 524 560]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}PolicyIE, and it also achieves the highest average
  p3-l90  [306 560 496 574]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}scores of all PLUE tasks among all models.
  p3-l91  [317 574 524 588]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Lastly, even though domain-specific pre-training
  p3-l92  [306 588 524 601]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}and large PLMs help boost the performance for all
  p3-l93  [306 601 524 615]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}tasks, the performance of some tasks and datasets
  p3-l94  [306 615 525 628]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(e.g., APP-350, PrivacyQA, slot filling in PolicyIE)
  p3-l95  [306 629 525 642]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}remains low, which indicates much potential for
  p3-l96  [306 642 526 655]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}further work on NLP for the privacy policy domain.
  p3-l97  [306 666 395 682]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}4 Related Work
  p3-l98  [306 689 431 703]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}Privacy Policy Benchmarks
  p3-l99  [442 689 525 703]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}The Usable Privacy
  p3-l100  [306 703 526 716]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Policy Project (Sadeh et al., 2013) is the most sig-
  p3-l101  [306 717 525 730]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}nificant effort to date, resulting in a large pool of
  p3-l102  [306 730 526 743]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}works (Wilson et al., 2016a,b; Sathyendra et al.,
  p3-l103  [319 752 525 764]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 6}2{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}Ravichander et al. (2019) reported 39.8% F1 score for
  p3-l104  [306 763 499 774]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}BERT model; however, we are able to achieve 36.3%.
