  p4-l254  [71 567 524 579]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}Table 3: Performance comparison of pre-trained models on intent classification and slot filling tasks (PolicyIE) and
  p4-l255  [71 579 524 591]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}average scores of all PLUE tasks. We fine-tune all the models three times with different seeds and report average
  p4-l256  [71 591 375 603]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}performances. Human performances are reported from the respective works.
  p4-l257  [71 619 289 632]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}2016; Mysore Sathyendra et al., 2017; Bhatia and
  p4-l258  [71 633 290 646]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Breaux, 2015; Bhatia et al., 2016; Hosseini et al.,
  p4-l259  [71 646 291 659]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}2016; Zimmeck et al., 2019) to facilitate the au-
  p4-l260  [71 660 289 673]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}tomation of privacy policy analysis. A wide range
  p4-l261  [71 673 290 686]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}of NLP techniques have been explored accordingly
  p4-l262  [70 687 289 700]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(Liu et al., 2014; Ramanath et al., 2014; Wilson
  p4-l263  [71 700 291 713]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}et al., 2016a; Harkous et al., 2018; Zimmeck et al.,
  p4-l264  [71 714 290 727]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}2019; Shvartzshanider et al., 2018; Harkous et al.,
  p4-l265  [71 727 290 741]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}2018; Ravichander et al., 2019; Ahmad et al., 2020;
  p4-l266  [71 741 233 754]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Bui et al., 2021; Ahmad et al., 2021).
  p4-l267  [306 619 449 633]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}Pre-trained Language Models
  p4-l268  [460 619 525 632]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}In the last few
  p4-l269  [306 633 524 646]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}years, NLP research has witnessed a radical change
  p4-l270  [306 646 526 659]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}with the advent of PLMs like ELMo (Peters et al.,
  p4-l271  [306 660 524 673]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}2018) and BERT (Devlin et al., 2019). PLMs
  p4-l272  [306 673 524 686]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}achieved state-of-the-art results in many language
  p4-l273  [306 687 524 700]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}understanding benchmarks. Consequently, PLMs
  p4-l274  [306 700 526 713]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}have been developed for a wide range of domains,
  p4-l275  [306 714 524 727]  {NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal 10.9 i}e.g.,{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} scientific (Beltagy et al., 2019), medical (Lee
  p4-l276  [306 727 526 741]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}et al., 2020; Rasmy et al., 2021; Alsentzer et al.,
  p4-l277  [306 741 526 754]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}2019), legal (Chalkidis et al., 2020), and cybersecu-
  p4-l278  [306 755 524 768]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}rity (Ranade et al., 2021; Bayer et al., 2022). This
  p4-l279  [291 781 307 794]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}355
  == page 5  595 x 842  lines 102  pictures 0  drawings 1
  p5-l1  [70 72 291 85]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}work investigates the adaptation of PLMs to facili-
  p5-l2  [71 85 279 98]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}tate NLP research in the privacy policy domain.
  p5-l3  [71 107 236 123]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}5 Conclusion and Future work
  p5-l4  [71 129 291 142]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Reliable aggregation of datasets and benchmark-
  p5-l5  [71 142 289 156]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ing foundation models on them facilitate future
  p5-l6  [71 156 289 169]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}research. This work presents PLUE, a benchmark
  p5-l7  [71 170 291 183]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}for training and evaluating new security and pri-
  p5-l8  [71 183 289 196]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}vacy policy models. PLUE will help researchers
  p5-l9  [71 197 289 210]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}benchmark policy language understanding under a
  p5-l10  [71 210 278 223]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}unified setup and facilitate reliable comparison.
  p5-l11  [82 224 291 237]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}PLUE also presents some challenges in lan-
  p5-l12  [71 237 291 250]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}guage understanding evaluation for privacy poli-
  p5-l13  [71 251 289 264]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}cies. For example, the imbalance data issue for
  p5-l14  [71 264 291 278]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}privacy practices is a major challenge in the Priva-
  p5-l15  [71 278 289 291]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}cyQA task (Parvez et al., 2022). Data efficiency is
  p5-l16  [71 292 289 305]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}also a challenge for continual pre-training as the
  p5-l17  [71 305 291 318]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}amount of unlabeled data is also small for this do-
  p5-l18  [71 319 289 332]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}main. Approaches such as (Qin et al., 2022) could
  p5-l19  [71 332 289 345]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}be investigated to continually adapt LMs for the
  p5-l20  [71 346 201 359]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}emerging data in this domain.
  p5-l21  [71 368 130 383]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}Limitations
  p5-l22  [71 389 289 403]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}The pre-training privacy policy corpus and the
  p5-l23  [71 403 289 416]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}downstream task datasets are unlikely to contain
  p5-l24  [71 416 289 430]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}toxic or biased content. Therefore, they should
  p5-l25  [71 430 289 443]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}not magnify toxicity or bias in the pre-trained
  p5-l26  [71 444 290 457]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}and fine-tuned models, although the models may
  p5-l27  [71 457 291 470]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}exhibit such behavior due to their original pre-
  p5-l28  [71 471 289 484]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}training. The pre-training and benchmark datasets
  p5-l29  [71 484 289 497]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}are formed based on privacy policies crawled in
  p5-l30  [71 498 291 511]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}the past; as a result, they could be outdated by now.
  p5-l31  [71 511 290 524]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}This work focuses on the English language only,
  p5-l32  [71 525 291 538]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}and the findings may not apply to other languages.
  p5-l33  [71 547 158 562]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}Ethics Statement
  p5-l34  [71 568 107 582]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}License
  p5-l35  [118 569 289 582]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}The OPP-115 and APP-350 datasets
  p5-l36  [71 582 289 595]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}are made available for research, teaching, and
  p5-l37  [71 596 289 609]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}scholarship purposes only, with further parameters
  p5-l38  [71 609 291 622]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}in the spirit of a Creative Commons Attribution-
  p5-l39  [71 623 291 636]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}NonCommercial License (CC BY-NC). The Pol-
  p5-l40  [71 636 289 649]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}icyQA and PI-Extract datasets are derived from
  p5-l41  [71 650 289 663]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}OPP-115 datasets. The PrivacyQA and PolicyIE
  p5-l42  [71 663 289 677]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}datasets are released under an MIT license. The
  p5-l43  [71 677 291 690]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}pre-training corpus, MAPS Policies Dataset, is re-
  p5-l44  [71 690 289 704]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}leased under CC BY-NC. We strictly adhere to
  p5-l45  [71 704 291 717]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}these licenses and will release the PLUE bench-
  p5-l46  [71 718 254 731]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}mark resources under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0.
  p5-l47  [71 733 154 748]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}Carbon Footprint
  p5-l48  [165 734 289 747]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}We only use RoBERTa large
  p5-l49  [71 747 291 761]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}models for continual training on the privacy pol-
  p5-l50  [71 761 289 774]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}icy domain to reduce the environmental impacts
  p5-l51  [306 72 424 85]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}of training large models.
  p5-l52  [436 72 526 85]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}The PP-BERT, PP-
  p5-l53  [306 85 524 98]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}SpanBERT, PP-Electra, and PP-RoBERTa models
  p5-l54  [306 99 524 112]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}were trained for 100k steps on Tesla V100 GPUs
  p5-l55  [306 112 524 126]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}that took 1-2 days. Therefore, the training would
  p5-l56  [306 125 524 139]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}emit only 9kg of carbon into the environment.{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 8}3
  p5-l57  [306 139 524 153]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}All fine-tuning experiments were very lightweight
  p5-l58  [306 153 524 166]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}due to the small size of the datasets, resulting in
  p5-l59  [306 167 482 180]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}approximately 12kg of carbon emission.
