  p9-l47  [71 724 282 740]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}B More Details of Pre-training Corpora
  p9-l48  [70 747 291 761]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}We use MAPS, the mobile application privacy pol-
  p9-l49  [71 761 291 774]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}icy corpus presented by Zimmeck et al. (2019).
  p9-l50  [306 104 526 118]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}MAPS consists of the URLs of 441K mobile ap-
  p9-l51  [306 118 524 131]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}plication privacy policies, which were collected
  p9-l52  [306 132 525 145]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}from April to May 2018 from the Google Play
  p9-l53  [306 145 524 158]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}store. We remove the duplicated URLs, crawl the
  p9-l54  [306 159 526 172]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}privacy policy documents in HTML/PDF format,
  p9-l55  [306 172 524 185]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}convert them to raw text format, and filter out the
  p9-l56  [306 186 526 199]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}documents with noise (e.g., empty documents re-
  p9-l57  [306 199 524 212]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}sulting from obsolete URLs). Finally, we ended
  p9-l58  [306 213 526 226]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}up with 64K privacy policy documents. For web-
  p9-l59  [306 226 524 240]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}site privacy policies, we use the Princeton-Leuven
  p9-l60  [306 240 524 253]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Longitudinal Corpus of Privacy Policies (Amos
  p9-l61  [306 252 524 267]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}et al., 2021).{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 8}4{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} The Princeton-Leuven Longitudinal
  p9-l62  [306 267 524 280]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Corpus of Privacy Policies contains 130K website
  p9-l63  [306 281 524 294]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}privacy policies spanning over two decades. We
  p9-l64  [306 294 526 307]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}use the documents with the latest date and con-
  p9-l65  [306 308 526 321]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}vert them (from markdown format) into text format.
  p9-l66  [306 321 526 334]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Combining these two corpora, we obtain our pre-
  p9-l67  [306 335 455 348]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}training corpus with 332M words.
  p9-l68  [306 358 409 373]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}C Baseline Models
  p9-l69  [306 381 524 394]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}We benchmark a few pre-trained language models
  p9-l70  [306 394 467 407]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}as baselines to facilitate future work.
  p9-l71  [306 410 335 424]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}BERT
  p9-l72  [346 411 525 424]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Devlin et al. (2019) proposed Transformer
  p9-l73  [306 424 526 437]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(Vaswani et al., 2017) based language model pre-
  p9-l74  [306 438 524 451]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}trained on BooksCorpus and English Wikipedia
  p9-l75  [306 451 524 464]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}data using masked language modeling (MLM) and
  p9-l76  [306 465 415 478]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}next sentence prediction.
  p9-l77  [306 481 340 495]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}Electra
  p9-l78  [351 481 525 494]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Clark et al. (2020) pre-trains a generator
  p9-l79  [306 495 526 508]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}and a discriminator on the same corpus as BERT,
  p9-l80  [306 508 524 522]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}where the generator takes a masked text as input
  p9-l81  [306 522 526 535]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}and is trained using the MLM objective. The dis-
  p9-l82  [306 536 525 549]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}criminator takes the predictions from the generator
  p9-l83  [306 549 526 562]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}and detects which tokens are replaced by the gener-
  p9-l84  [306 563 526 576]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ator. After pre-training, the generator is discarded,
  p9-l85  [306 576 524 589]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}and the discriminator is used as the language model
  p9-l86  [306 590 418 603]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}for the downstream tasks.
  p9-l87  [306 606 360 620]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}SpanBERT
  p9-l88  [371 606 524 619]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Joshi et al. (2020) shares the same
  p9-l89  [306 620 524 633]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}architecture and pre-training corpus as BERT but
  p9-l90  [306 633 524 646]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}differs in the pre-training objectives. It extends
  p9-l91  [306 647 526 660]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}BERT by masking contiguous spans instead of sin-
  p9-l92  [306 660 526 674]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}gle tokens and training the span boundary represen-
  p9-l93  [306 674 461 687]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}tations to predict the masked spans.
  p9-l94  [306 690 354 704]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}RoBERTa
  p9-l95  [365 690 526 704]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Liu et al. (2019) presented a replica-
  p9-l96  [306 704 524 717]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}tion study of BERT pretraining where they showed
  p9-l97  [306 717 526 731]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}that BERT was significantly undertrained and pro-
  p9-l98  [306 731 524 744]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}posed RoBERTa that tunes key hyperparameters
  p9-l99  [319 752 528 764]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 6}4{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}The corpus is publicly available at{Inconsolatazi4-Regular 9} https://github.
  p9-l100  [306 763 462 774]  {Inconsolatazi4-Regular 9}com/citp/privacy-policy-historical{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}.
