  '''
  acl2023-hardt-ellipsis.pdf  sha256 750d6b7b40b51436
  metadata: title='' authors=[] date='2023-07-08'
  == page 1  595 x 842  lines 89  pictures 0  drawings 0
  p1-l1  [137 797 458 807]  {NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal 9 i}Proceedings of the 61st Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics
  p1-l2  [230 808 365 819]  {NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal 9 i}Volume 2: Short Papers{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}, pages 39–47
  p1-l3  [179 819 417 829]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}July 9-14, 2023 ©2023 Association for Computational Linguistics
  p1-l4  [87 76 508 95]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 14.3 b}Ellipsis-Dependent Reasoning: a New Challenge for Large Language
  p1-l5  [275 92 320 111]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 14.3 b}Models
  p1-l6  [265 131 333 146]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}Daniel Hardt
  p1-l7  [228 145 370 159]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 12}Copenhagen Business School
  p1-l8  [249 160 349 173]  {NimbusMonL-Regu 12}dha.msc@cbs.dk
  p1-l9  [158 212 202 228]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}Abstract
  p1-l10  [88 233 274 245]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}We propose a novel challenge for large lan-
  p1-l11  [88 245 274 257]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}guage models: ellipsis-dependent reasoning.
  p1-l12  [87 257 274 269]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}We define several structures of paired exam-
  p1-l13  [88 269 272 281]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}ples, where an ellipsis example is matched to
  p1-l14  [88 281 272 293]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}its non-ellipsis counterpart, and a question is
  p1-l15  [88 293 274 305]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}posed which requires resolution of the ellipsis.
  p1-l16  [88 305 272 317]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}Test results show that the best models perform
  p1-l17  [88 317 272 329]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}well on non-elliptical examples but struggle
  p1-l18  [88 329 258 341]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}with all but the simplest ellipsis structures.
  p1-l19  [71 347 154 362]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}1 Introduction
  p1-l20  [71 369 291 382]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Ellipsis is a fundamental feature of human lan-
  p1-l21  [71 382 290 395]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}guage, occurring in all registers, where parts of
  p1-l22  [71 396 289 409]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}sentences are omitted, although the missing parts
  p1-l23  [71 409 289 422]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}are essential for understanding the meaning. The
  p1-l24  [71 423 289 436]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}following is an example of Verb Phrase Ellipsis
  p1-l25  [70 436 215 449]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(VPE)(Bos and Spenader, 2011):
  p1-l26  [71 459 84 472]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(1)
  p1-l27  [103 459 272 472]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}William went running. Harold did too.
  p1-l28  [71 481 291 494]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(1) is understood as asserting that Harold went run-
  p1-l29  [71 495 289 508]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ning; that is, the hearer or reader naturally fills
  p1-l30  [71 508 291 522]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}in the missing material. This is done by identify-
  p1-l31  [71 522 289 535]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ing the antecedent VP,{NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal 10.9 i} went running{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}, in the first
  p1-l32  [71 535 291 549]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}sentence. The following is the non-elliptical coun-
  p1-l33  [71 549 130 562]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}terpart of (1):
  p1-l34  [71 572 84 585]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(2)
  p1-l35  [103 572 291 585]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}William went running. Harold went run-
  p1-l36  [103 585 142 598]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ning too.
  p1-l37  [70 608 289 621]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}With such examples, we can test understanding
  p1-l38  [71 621 289 634]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}of ellipsis by targeting the ellipsis phrase with a
  p1-l39  [71 635 178 648]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}simple Yes/No question:
  p1-l40  [71 657 84 670]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(3)
  p1-l41  [103 657 208 670]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Did Harold go running?
  p1-l42  [71 680 291 693]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}If a system answers the question incorrectly for (1),
  p1-l43  [71 693 291 706]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}the ellipsis example, but answers correctly for (2),
  p1-l44  [71 707 289 720]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}the non-elliptical counterpart of (1), we can ascribe
  p1-l45  [71 720 290 733]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}the result specifically to the challenge of ellipsis,
  p1-l46  [71 734 257 747]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}since the examples are otherwise identical.
  p1-l47  [82 747 291 761]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}As with pronominal anaphora and other dis-
  p1-l48  [71 761 289 774]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}course processes, there is great flexibility in the
  p1-l49  [306 213 525 227]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}way ellipsis can occur in discourse. Example (1)
  p1-l50  [306 227 524 240]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}involves two simple adjacent sentences. It is also
  p1-l51  [306 241 524 254]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}possible for ellipsis or the antecedent to occur in
  p1-l52  [306 254 525 267]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}embedded clauses. Furthermore, ellipsis can occur
  p1-l53  [306 268 524 281]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}either before or after the antecedent. Finally, an
  p1-l54  [306 281 524 294]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}arbitrary amount of material can intervene between
  p1-l55  [306 295 490 308]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}the antecedent and the ellipsis occurrence.
  p1-l56  [317 309 525 322]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}In this paper, we propose the challenge of
  p1-l57  [306 322 526 336]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ellipsis-dependent reasoning. This challenge con-
  p1-l58  [306 336 524 349]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}sists of examples involving an ellipsis clause, the
  p1-l59  [306 349 526 363]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}target. Each ellipsis example is paired with its non-
  p1-l60  [306 363 524 376]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}elliptical counterpart, where the target clause is
  p1-l61  [306 377 524 390]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}overt rather than elliptical. We then pose a question
  p1-l62  [306 390 525 403]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}whose answer is dependent on the target clause. A
  p1-l63  [306 404 526 417]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}key aspect of the challenge is that ellipsis occur-
  p1-l64  [306 417 524 430]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}rences are possible in a variety of diverse structural
  p1-l65  [306 431 524 444]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}configurations. We test a series of GPT-3 models
  p1-l66  [306 444 485 457]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(GPT) on several such ellipsis structures.
  p1-l67  [306 468 395 484]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}2 Related Work
  p1-l68  [306 492 526 505]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}There is a large literature concerning the prob-
  p1-l69  [306 506 526 519]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ing of language models from a variety of perspec-
  p1-l70  [306 519 525 532]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}tives. Furthermore, there has been substantial work
  p1-l71  [306 533 526 546]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}specifically addressing ellipsis in NLP. In this pa-
  p1-l72  [306 546 526 559]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}per, we are proposing the challenge of ellipsis-
  p1-l73  [306 560 526 573]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}dependent reasoning. This proposal builds on var-
  p1-l74  [306 573 525 586]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ious strands of prior research; below we consider
  p1-l75  [306 587 526 600]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}some particularly relevant aspects of this literature.
  p1-l76  [306 611 320 625]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}2.1
  p1-l77  [331 611 476 625]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}Probing Models for Knowledge
  p1-l78  [306 630 525 643]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}The Winograd Schema (Kocijan et al. (2022);
  p1-l79  [306 643 524 656]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Levesque et al. (2012)) involves test examples that
  p1-l80  [306 657 524 670]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}use the linguistic problem of pronoun resolution
  p1-l81  [306 670 524 683]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}to gain insight into the commonsense reasoning
  p1-l82  [306 684 524 697]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}abilities of an AI system. To do this, the Winograd
  p1-l83  [306 697 525 710]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Schema requires pairs of examples that differ only
  p1-l84  [306 711 463 724]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}in one specific, small way, as in (4):
  p1-l85  [306 734 319 747]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(4)
  p1-l86  [339 734 526 747]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}The city councilmen refused the demonstra-
  p1-l87  [339 747 524 761]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}tors a permit because they feared/advocated
  p1-l88  [338 761 378 774]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}violence.
  p1-l89  [293 781 304 794]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}39
  == page 2  595 x 842  lines 103  pictures 0  drawings 0
  p2-l1  [70 72 290 85]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}With “feared", the pronoun “they" refers to the city
  p2-l2  [71 85 289 98]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}councilmen, while with “advocated", it refers to the
  p2-l3  [71 99 289 112]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}demonstrators. Humans understand this because
  p2-l4  [71 112 289 126]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}of general, commonsense knowledge about what
  p2-l5  [70 126 289 139]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}would reasonably explain the refusal of a permit
  p2-l6  [71 139 289 153]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}in the two cases. It is difficult to ensure that such
  p2-l7  [71 153 291 166]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}examples can{NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal 10.9 i} only{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} be solved through such sophis-
  p2-l8  [71 167 291 180]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ticated reasoning, and, according to Kocijan et al.
  p2-l9  [70 180 289 193]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(2022)[p. 8], “Solving Winograd schemas is not
  p2-l10  [71 194 291 207]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}a surrogate for the ability to do commonsense rea-
  p2-l11  [71 207 107 220]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}soning".
  p2-l12  [82 221 291 234]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}A different approach is exemplified by Lin et al.
  p2-l13  [70 234 289 247]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(2019): here, examples are constructed which test
  p2-l14  [71 248 291 261]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}specific aspects of linguistic knowledge of a sys-
  p2-l15  [71 261 289 275]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}tem, namely, whether BERT embeddings “encode
  p2-l16  [71 275 289 288]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}hierarchical information". For example, a task is
  p2-l17  [71 289 289 302]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}defined to identify the main auxiliary verb in a
  p2-l18  [71 302 290 315]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}sentence, even in cases where the main auxiliary
  p2-l19  [71 316 289 329]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}is not the first auxiliary verb to appear. Training
  p2-l20  [71 329 291 342]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}and testing datasets are automatically generated us-
  p2-l21  [71 343 289 356]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ing a context-free grammar for several such tasks
  p2-l22  [71 356 265 369]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}involving hierarchical syntactic information.
  p2-l23  [71 378 84 392]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}2.2
  p2-l24  [95 378 261 392]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}Anaphora and Question Answering
  p2-l25  [71 396 289 409]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Quoref (Dasigi et al. (2019); Zhang and Zhao
  p2-l26  [70 410 289 423]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(2022)) is a question-answer dataset designed so
  p2-l27  [71 423 291 436]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}that correct answers cannot be given unless a coref-
  p2-l28  [71 437 290 450]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}erence relationship is correctly identified; that is,
  p2-l29  [71 450 289 463]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}the reasoning involved in question answering is
  p2-l30  [71 464 289 477]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}dependent on resolving coreference. This is, in a
  p2-l31  [71 477 289 490]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}sense, the inverse of the Winograd schema, where
  p2-l32  [71 491 291 504]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}resolving coreference is dependent upon reasoning.
  p2-l33  [71 504 289 518]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Just as with the Winograd schema, it is difficult to
  p2-l34  [71 518 289 531]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ensure that resolving this dependency is required
  p2-l35  [71 532 289 545]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}for system success. (Dasigi et al., 2019)[p. 1] note
  p2-l36  [71 545 289 558]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}that this is “challenging, because it is hard to avoid
  p2-l37  [71 559 289 572]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}lexical cues that shortcut complex reasoning", and
  p2-l38  [71 572 289 585]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}based on a random sample, found that coreference
  p2-l39  [71 586 270 599]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}resolution was required for 78% of questions.
  p2-l40  [71 608 84 622]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}2.3
  p2-l41  [95 608 175 622]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}Ellipsis as a Task
  p2-l42  [71 625 289 639]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}There has been substantial work on ellipsis as a
  p2-l43  [71 639 291 652]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}discrete NLP task (Khullar (2020), Zhang et al.
  p2-l44  [70 653 291 666]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(2019); Kenyon-Dean et al. (2016); Bos and Spe-
  p2-l45  [71 666 289 679]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}nader (2011)). Vanderlyn et al. (2022) surveys a
  p2-l46  [71 680 291 693]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}variety of forms of what they call “implicit ref-
  p2-l47  [71 693 291 706]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}erence", which includes ellipsis and related phe-
  p2-l48  [71 707 289 720]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}nomena. Aralikatte et al. (2021) frame ellipsis as
  p2-l49  [71 720 289 733]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}a question-answering task, i.e., a task of locating
  p2-l50  [71 734 289 747]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}an antecedent, understood as a span of tokens in
  p2-l51  [71 747 290 761]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}context. Aralikatte et al. (2021) report token F1
  p2-l52  [71 761 291 774]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}scores of 78.66 for VPE and 86.01 for sluicing, an-
  p2-l53  [306 72 524 85]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}other form of ellipsis. It’s important to note that the
  p2-l54  [306 85 524 98]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}task here, of antecedent identification, is a sub-part
  p2-l55  [306 99 524 112]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}of the ellipsis challenge. Before the antecedent is
  p2-l56  [306 112 526 126]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}identified, an ellipsis occurrence must be identified,
  p2-l57  [306 126 524 139]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}and after the antecedent is identified, it must be
  p2-l58  [306 139 522 153]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}interpreted, or “reconstructed", at the ellipsis site.
  p2-l59  [306 164 320 178]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}2.4
  p2-l60  [331 164 485 178]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}Relevance for Ellipsis-Dependent
  p2-l61  [331 177 379 191]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}Reasoning
  p2-l62  [306 196 525 209]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}The specific task of ellipsis is addressed in work
  p2-l63  [306 210 525 223]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}like that of Aralikatte et al. (2021), but the key
  p2-l64  [306 223 526 237]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}difference here is that we are probing for a com-
  p2-l65  [306 237 524 250]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}plete solution to the ellipsis problem. The proposed
  p2-l66  [306 251 526 264]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ellipsis-dependent reasoning task involves a ques-
  p2-l67  [306 264 526 277]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}tion that can only be answered correctly if the el-
  p2-l68  [306 278 524 291]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}lipsis is properly identified and interpreted. This
  p2-l69  [306 291 524 304]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}combines aspects of the preceding works in a novel
  p2-l70  [306 305 524 318]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}way: like the Winograd schema and the syntactic
  p2-l71  [306 318 524 331]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}work by Lin et al. (2019), it probes for what we
  p2-l72  [306 332 524 345]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}see as a specific type of psychologically-defined
  p2-l73  [306 345 524 359]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}knowledge: namely, a representation of context
  p2-l74  [306 359 524 372]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}that supports the resolution of ellipsis. Similarly to
  p2-l75  [306 372 524 386]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}the work on Quoref, we use targeted questions to
  p2-l76  [306 386 477 399]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}probe for discourse-related knowledge.
  p2-l77  [317 400 524 413]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}There is an extensive literature on the contextual
  p2-l78  [306 414 524 427]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}interpretation of natural language, resting on the
  p2-l79  [306 427 525 440]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}idea of a dynamic, ongoing model of discourse. For
  p2-l80  [306 441 526 454]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}example, Discourse Representation Theory (Kamp,
  p2-l81  [305 454 526 468]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}1981) describes a semantic model supporting dis-
  p2-l82  [306 468 526 481]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}course phenomena such as pronominal and tempo-
  p2-l83  [306 482 524 495]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ral anaphora, and Sag and Hankamer (1984) argue
  p2-l84  [306 495 524 508]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}explicitly that ellipsis and other such phenomena
  p2-l85  [306 509 524 522]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}are interpreted with respect to a discourse model
  p2-l86  [306 522 526 535]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(Garnham, 2010). As one study puts it, “Inter-
  p2-l87  [306 536 524 549]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}preting a verb-phrase ellipsis (VP ellipsis) requires
  p2-l88  [306 549 526 562]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}accessing an antecedent in memory, and then in-
  p2-l89  [306 563 524 576]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}tegrating a representation of this antecedent into
  p2-l90  [306 576 524 590]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}the local context" (Martin and McElree, 2008). In
  p2-l91  [306 590 524 603]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}this paper, we seek to determine whether a large
  p2-l92  [306 603 524 617]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}language model is capable of such an interpretive
  p2-l93  [306 617 342 630]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}process.
  p2-l94  [306 642 349 657]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}3 Data
  p2-l95  [306 665 524 679]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}There is a great deal of variety in the structural
  p2-l96  [306 679 526 692]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}configurations in which ellipsis can occur. In ta-
  p2-l97  [306 693 524 706]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}bles 1 and 2 we define structures for ellipsis and
  p2-l98  [306 706 410 719]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}antecedent occurrences.
  p2-l99  [317 720 526 733]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}In all structures, there is an ellipsis occurrence,
