  p3-l29  [101 317 259 329]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}Table 1: Structures for Positive Answer
  p3-l30  [71 351 289 364]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}arate Sentence, and Conjoined Sentence, involve
  p3-l31  [71 365 289 378]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}two adjacent main clauses. This is followed by two
  p3-l32  [71 378 291 391]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}structures in which either the VPE or antecedent oc-
  p3-l33  [71 392 289 405]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}cur in a subordinate clause. There is a Backwards
  p3-l34  [71 405 290 418]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}structure where the VPE precedes the antecedent;
  p3-l35  [71 419 289 432]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}here the VPE is in a subordinate clause. Finally, we
  p3-l36  [71 432 289 445]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}have a Two Actions structure; that is, two ellipsis
  p3-l37  [71 446 289 459]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}occurrences each with their respective antecedent
  p3-l38  [70 459 289 473]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}VPs. We have two versions: one in which the target
  p3-l39  [71 473 289 486]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}question has a correct answer of “Yes", shown in
  p3-l40  [71 486 289 500]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}table 1, and another where the target question has
  p3-l41  [71 500 259 513]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}a correct answer of “No", shown in table 2.
  p3-l42  [82 514 289 527]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}We generate large numbers of examples of each
  p3-l43  [71 527 289 540]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}structure by performing random substitutions for
  p3-l44  [71 541 289 554]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}both the subject and verb. The substitution lists are
  p3-l45  [71 554 289 568]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}given in the appendix, along with samples of each
  p3-l46  [71 567 261 581]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}structure and the size of the resulting sets.{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 8} 1
  p3-l47  [71 591 110 606]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}4 Test
  p3-l48  [71 614 291 627]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}For each instantiation of a given structure, we pro-
  p3-l49  [71 627 289 640]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}duce paired ellipsis and non-ellipsis examples, with
  p3-l50  [71 641 289 654]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}an associated Yes/No question. We randomly select
  p3-l51  [70 654 289 667]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}1000 examples for each structure, including 500
  p3-l52  [71 668 289 681]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ellipsis examples and 500 examples which are their
  p3-l53  [71 681 291 694]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}non-elliptical counterparts. Each example is pre-
  p3-l54  [71 695 289 708]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}sented to the system, preceded by the text, “Please
  p3-l55  [71 708 291 722]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}give a Yes or No answer:". We test five GPT-3 mod-
  p3-l56  [71 722 291 735]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}els on these structures: Davinci-003, Davinci-002,
