  p5-l1  [71 246 291 258]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}Figure 5: Ellipsis Accuracy – babbage-001 (1000 exam-
  p5-l2  [71 258 145 270]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}ples per structure.)
  p5-l3  [71 479 289 491]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}Figure 6: Ellipsis Accuracy – ada-001 (1000 examples
  p5-l4  [71 491 127 503]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}per structure.)
  p5-l5  [71 547 291 560]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}models, and is able to perform in a way that sug-
  p5-l6  [71 560 291 573]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}gests human-level understanding. Because of this,
  p5-l7  [71 574 289 587]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}it is important to explore areas in which it might
  p5-l8  [71 587 289 601]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}still differ from human language understanding. In
  p5-l9  [71 601 289 614]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}this paper we have argued that ellipsis is one such
  p5-l10  [71 615 289 628]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}area. For many simple ellipsis structures, the most
  p5-l11  [71 628 289 641]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}powerful GPT-3 models struggle, with accuracies
  p5-l12  [71 642 289 655]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}far lower on ellipsis examples than on non-elliptical
  p5-l13  [71 655 128 668]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}counterparts.
  p5-l14  [82 680 289 693]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}In many ways, GPT-3 appears to understand
  p5-l15  [71 693 289 706]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}the texts that it processes, often being able to
  p5-l16  [71 707 291 720]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}answer questions that appear to rely on sophisti-
  p5-l17  [71 720 291 733]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}cated reasoning. However, the challenge of ellipsis-
  p5-l18  [71 734 289 747]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}dependent reasoning provides evidence that GPT-3
  p5-l19  [71 747 290 761]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}is not able to understand in anything like the way
  p5-l20  [71 761 121 774]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}humans do.
  p5-l21  [306 253 526 265]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}Figure 7: Ellipsis case – Errors by model. False Pos-
  p5-l22  [306 265 524 277]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}itives (Incorrect “Yes") vs. False Negatives (Incorrect
  p5-l23  [305 277 329 289]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}“No")
  p5-l24  [306 485 524 497]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}Figure 8: Non-ellipsis case – Errors by model. False
  p5-l25  [306 497 526 509]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}Positives (Incorrect “Yes") vs. False Negatives (Incor-
  p5-l26  [306 509 348 521]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}rect “No")
  p5-l27  [306 543 383 559]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}8 Limitations
  p5-l28  [306 568 526 581]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}This paper argues that the proposed task of ellipsis-
  p5-l29  [306 582 525 595]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}dependent reasoning is a difficult challenge for
  p5-l30  [306 595 526 609]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}GPT-3 models, which are among the most power-
  p5-l31  [306 609 524 622]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ful current language models. The data constructed
  p5-l32  [306 622 526 636]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}here is restricted to English, and furthermore is re-
  p5-l33  [306 636 524 649]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}stricted to a single form of ellipsis, namely verb
  p5-l34  [306 650 525 663]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}phrase ellipsis. It may well be that other forms of
  p5-l35  [306 663 524 676]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ellipsis may give rise to different effects, and it is
  p5-l36  [306 677 525 690]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}also important to test the claims made here on other
  p5-l37  [306 690 352 703]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}languages.
