  p1-l1  [137 798 458 806]  {NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal 9 i}Proceedings of the 61st Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics
  p1-l2  [230 809 367 818]  {NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal 9 i}Volume 2: Short Papers,{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9} pages 57–67
  p1-l3  [179 821 417 829]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}July 9-14, 2023 ©2023 Association for Computational Linguistics
  p1-l4  [136 72 459 85]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 14.3 b}A Fast Algorithm for Computing Prefix Probabilities
  p1-l5  [212 96 281 107]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}Franz Nowak
  p1-l6  [311 96 386 107]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}Ryan Cotterell
  p1-l7  [177 110 421 121]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 12}{{Inconsolatazi4-Regular 12}franz.nowak, ryan.cotterell{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 12}}{Inconsolatazi4-Regular 12}@inf.ethz.ch
  p1-l8  [158 159 202 169]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}Abstract
  p1-l9  [88 182 272 191]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}Multiple algorithms are known for efficiently
  p1-l10  [88 194 272 203]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}calculating the prefix probability of a string
  p1-l11  [88 206 272 215]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}under a probabilistic context-free grammar
  p1-l12  [88 218 272 227]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}(PCFG). Good algorithms for the problem have
  p1-l13  [88 230 274 239]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}a runtime cubic in the length of the input string.
  p1-l14  [88 242 274 251]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}However, some proposed algorithms are subop-
  p1-l15  [88 254 274 263]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}timal with respect to the size of the grammar.
  p1-l16  [88 266 274 275]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}This paper proposes a novel speed-up of Je-
  p1-l17  [88 278 272 287]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}linek and Lafferty’s (1991) algorithm, whose
  p1-l18  [88 288 272 299]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}original runtime is{CMSY10 10 i} O{CMR10 10}({CMMI10 10 i}N{CMR7 7}3{CMSY10 10 i} |N |{CMR7 7}3{CMR10 10} +{CMSY10 10 i} |N |{CMR7 7}4{CMR10 10} ){NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}, where
  p1-l19  [88 301 273 311]  {CMMI10 10 i}N{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10} is the input length and{CMSY10 10 i} |N |{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10} is the number of
  p1-l20  [88 314 272 323]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}non-terminals in the grammar. In contrast, our
  p1-l21  [88 324 253 335]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}speed-up runs in{CMSY10 10 i} O{CMR10 10}({CMMI10 10 i}N{CMR7 7}2{CMSY10 10 i} |N |{CMR7 7}3{CMR10 10} +{CMMI10 10 i} N{CMR7 7}3{CMSY10 10 i} |N |{CMR7 7}2{CMR10 10} ){NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10}.
  p1-l22  [110 346 245 354]  {Inconsolatazi4-Regular 10}https://github.com/rycolab/
  p1-l23  [110 358 181 366]  {Inconsolatazi4-Regular 10}prefix-parsing
  p1-l24  [71 377 154 388]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}1 Introduction
  p1-l25  [71 399 289 409]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Probabilistic context-free grammars (PCFGs) are
  p1-l26  [71 413 291 423]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}an important formalism in NLP (Eisenstein, 2019,
  p1-l27  [71 426 291 436]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Chapter 10). One common use of PCFGs is to con-
  p1-l28  [71 440 289 450]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}struct a language model. For instance, PCFGs form
  p1-l29  [71 454 291 463]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}the backbone of many neural language models, e.g.,
  p1-l30  [71 467 289 477]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}recurrent neural network grammars (RNNGs; Dyer
  p1-l31  [71 481 291 490]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}et al., 2016; Dyer, 2017; Kim et al., 2019). How-
  p1-l32  [71 494 290 504]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ever, in order to use a PCFG as a language model,
  p1-l33  [71 508 291 518]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}one needs to be able to compute prefix probabilities,
  p1-l34  [71 521 289 531]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}i.e., the probability that the yield of a derivation
  p1-l35  [71 535 289 545]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}starts with the given string. In notation, given a
  p1-l36  [71 548 290 559]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}string{CMMIB10 10.9 i} w{CMR10 10.9} ={CMMI10 10.9 i} w{CMR8 8}1{CMSY10 10.9 i} · · ·{CMMI10 10.9 i} w{CMMI8 8 i}N{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} , we seek the probability
  p1-l37  [71 557 289 572]  {CMMI10 10.9 i}p{CMR10 10.9}(S{CMSY8 8 i} ∗{CMSY10 10.9 i}⇒{CMMIB10 10.9 i} w{CMSY10 10.9 i} · · ·{CMR10 10.9}){NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} where{CMR10 10.9} S{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} is the distinguished start
  p1-l38  [71 572 289 586]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}symbol of the grammar and{CMSY8 8 i} ∗{CMSY10 10.9 i}⇒{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} is the closure over
  p1-l39  [71 588 289 600]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}applications of derivation rules of the grammar.{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 8}1
  p1-l40  [71 604 289 614]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Our paper gives a more efficient algorithm for the
  p1-l41  [71 617 291 627]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}simultaneous computation of the prefix probabil-
  p1-l42  [71 631 279 641]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ities of{NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal 10.9 i} all{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} prefixes of a string{CMMIB10 10.9 i} w{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} under a PCFG.
  p1-l43  [82 644 289 654]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}The authors are aware of two existing efficient
  p1-l44  [71 658 289 668]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}algorithms to compute prefix probabilities under
  p1-l45  [71 670 290 681]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}a PCFG.{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 8}2{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} The first is Jelinek and Lafferty’s (1991)
  p1-l46  [84 690 289 702]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 6}1{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9} Specifically,{CMMIB9 9 b i} α{CMSY6 6 i} ∗{CMSY9 9 i}⇒{CMMIB9 9 b i} β{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9} means that there exists an{CMMI9 9 i} n{CMSY9 9 i} ≥{CMR9 9} 0
  p1-l47  [71 703 148 712]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}such that{CMMIB9 9 b i} α{CMSY9 9 i} ⇒ · · · ⇒
  p1-l48  [114 711 148 719]  {CMEX9 9}| {z }
  p1-l49  [121 718 140 724]  {CMMI6 6 i}n{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 6} times
  p1-l50  [150 703 281 712]  {CMMIB9 9 b i}β{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}, where{CMSY9 9 i} ⇒{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9} marks a derivation step.
  p1-l51  [84 723 289 733]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 6}2{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}Upon publication of this work, the authors were made
  p1-l52  [71 735 289 743]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}aware of two other algorithms for finding prefix probabilities
  p1-l53  [71 745 291 753]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}in the special case of idempotent semirings (Corazza et al.
  p1-l54  [70 755 289 763]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}1994; Sánchez and Benedí 1997). See App. B for a discussion
  p1-l55  [71 765 195 773]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}of prefix parsing under a semiring.
  p1-l56  [306 159 526 169]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}algorithm which is derived from CKY (Kasami,
  p1-l57  [305 173 525 183]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}1965; Younger, 1967; Cocke and Schwartz, 1970)
  p1-l58  [306 187 525 196]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}and, thus, requires the grammar to be in Chomsky
  p1-l59  [306 200 524 210]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}normal form (CNF). Jelinek–Lafferty runs in
  p1-l60  [306 212 524 224]  {CMSY10 10.9 i}O{CMR10 10.9}({CMMI10 10.9 i}N{CMR8 8}3{CMSY10 10.9 i} |N |{CMR8 8}3{CMR10 10.9} +{CMSY10 10.9 i} |N |{CMR8 8}4{CMR10 10.9} ){NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} time, where{CMMI10 10.9 i} N{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} is the length
  p1-l61  [306 226 524 237]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}of the input and{CMSY10 10.9 i} N{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} is the number of non-terminals
  p1-l62  [306 239 526 251]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}of the grammar, slower than the{CMSY10 10.9 i} O{CMR10 10.9}({CMMI10 10.9 i}N{CMR8 8}3{CMSY10 10.9 i} |N |{CMR8 8}3{CMR10 10.9} )
  p1-l63  [306 254 525 264]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}required for parsing with CKY, when the number
  p1-l64  [306 267 493 278]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}of non-terminals{CMSY10 10.9 i} |N |{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} is taken into account.
  p1-l65  [317 282 524 292]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}The second, due to Stolcke (1995), is derived
  p1-l66  [306 295 524 305]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}from Earley parsing (Earley, 1970) and can parse
  p1-l67  [306 307 526 319]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}arbitrary PCFGs,{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 8}3{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} with a runtime of{CMSY10 10.9 i} O{CMR10 10.9}({CMMI10 10.9 i}N{CMR8 8}3{CMSY10 10.9 i} |N |{CMR8 8}3{CMR10 10.9} ){NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}.
  p1-l68  [306 322 524 332]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Many previous authors have improved the runtime
  p1-l69  [306 336 524 346]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}of Earley’s (Graham et al., 1980; Leermakers
  p1-l70  [306 350 524 359]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}et al., 1992; Moore, 2000,{NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal 10.9 i} inter alia{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}), and Opedal
  p1-l71  [306 363 524 373]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}et al. (2023) successfully applied this speed-up
  p1-l72  [306 377 524 386]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}to computing prefix probabilities, achieving a
  p1-l73  [306 388 525 400]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}runtime of{CMSY10 10.9 i} O{CMR10 10.9}({CMMI10 10.9 i}N{CMR8 8}3{CMSY10 10.9 i} |G|{CMR10 10.9}){NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}, where{CMSY10 10.9 i} |G|{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} is the size of
  p1-l74  [306 404 525 414]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}the grammar, that is, the sum of the number of
  p1-l75  [306 417 444 427]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}symbols in all production rules.
  p1-l76  [317 431 525 441]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Our paper provides a more efficient version of
  p1-l77  [306 445 525 455]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Jelinek and Lafferty (1991) for the computation of
  p1-l78  [306 458 526 468]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}prefix probabilities under a PCFG in CNF. Specif-
  p1-l79  [306 470 526 482]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ically, we give an{CMSY10 10.9 i} O{CMR10 10.9}({CMMI10 10.9 i}N{CMR8 8}2{CMSY10 10.9 i} |N |{CMR8 8}3{CMR10 10.9} +{CMMI10 10.9 i} N{CMR8 8}3{CMSY10 10.9 i} |N |{CMR8 8}2{CMR10 10.9} ){NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} time al-
  p1-l80  [306 485 526 495]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}gorithm, which is the fastest attested in the liter-
  p1-l81  [306 497 524 509]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}ature for dense grammars in CNF,{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 8}4{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} matching the
  p1-l82  [306 513 524 522]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}complexity of CKY adapted for dense grammars
  p1-l83  [306 524 524 536]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}by Eisner and Blatz (2007).{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 8}5{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} We provide a full
  p1-l84  [306 540 524 549]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}derivation and proof of correctness, as well as an
  p1-l85  [306 553 524 563]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}open-source implementation on GitHub. We also
  p1-l86  [306 567 524 577]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}briefly discuss how our improved algorithm can be
  p1-l87  [306 580 513 590]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}extended to work for semiring-weighted CFGs.
  p1-l88  [306 605 393 616]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}2 Preliminaries
  p1-l89  [306 628 524 637]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}We start by introducing the necessary background
  p1-l90  [306 641 479 651]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}on probabilistic context-free grammars.
  p1-l91  [319 662 524 672]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 6}3{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}Note that Earley’s and, by extension, Stolcke’s algorithms
  p1-l92  [306 673 524 682]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}also implicitly binarize the grammar during execution by using
  p1-l93  [306 683 450 691]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}dotted rules as additional non-terminals.
  p1-l94  [319 693 524 702]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 6}4{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}A PCFG in CNF is dense if for every{CMR9 9} X{CMMI9 9 i},{CMR9 9} Y{CMMI9 9 i},{CMR9 9} Z{CMSY9 9 i} ∈ N{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9} , we
  p1-l95  [306 704 445 712]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}have a production rule{CMR9 9} X{CMSY9 9 i} →{CMR9 9} Y Z{CMSY9 9 i} ∈ R{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}.
  p1-l96  [319 713 524 723]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 6}5{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}Note that there exist approximate parsing algorithms with
  p1-l97  [306 725 526 733]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}lower complexity bounds (Cohen et al., 2013). Moreover,
  p1-l98  [306 735 526 743]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}there are parsing algorithms that asymptotically run in sub-
  p1-l99  [306 745 524 753]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}cubic time in the input length using fast matrix multiplication
  p1-l100  [306 755 524 763]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}(Valiant, 1975; Benedí and Sánchez, 2007). However, they are
  p1-l101  [306 765 435 773]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}of limited practical use (Lee, 1997).
  p1-l102  [292 783 303 793]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}57
  p1-i1  [269 126 332 136]  picture
  p1-i2  [88 346 100 358]  picture
