  p4-l83  [306 404 396 415]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 12 b}4 Our Speed-up
  p4-l84  [306 426 526 436]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}We now turn to our development of a faster dy-
  p4-l85  [306 439 526 449]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}namic program to compute all prefix probabilities.
  p4-l86  [306 453 524 463]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}The speed-up comes from a different way to
  p4-l87  [306 466 526 477]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}factorize{CMMI10 10.9 i} π{CMR10 10.9}({CMMI10 10.9 i}i,{CMR10 10.9} X{CMMI10 10.9 i}, k{CMR10 10.9}){NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}, which allows additional mem-
  p4-l88  [306 480 525 490]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}oization. Starting with the definition of the prefix
  p4-l89  [306 493 526 504]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}probability in Eq. (16a), we first expand{CMMI10 10.9 i} ξ{CMR10 10.9}(Y Z{CMSY10 10.9 i} |{CMR10 10.9} X)
  p4-l90  [306 507 525 517]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}by Eq. (9), as seen in Eq. (16b). Then, we factor
  p4-l91  [306 521 526 531]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}out all terms that depend on the left-corner non-
  p4-l92  [306 534 524 544]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}terminal{CMR10 10.9} Y{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} in Eq. (16c), which we store in a chart
  p4-l93  [306 548 524 558]  {CMMI10 10.9 i}γ{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}, see Eq. (16e). We then do the same for all terms
  p4-l94  [306 559 525 571]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}depending on{CMR10 10.9} X{CMSY8 8 i}′{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} , factoring them out in Eq. (16d)
  p4-l95  [306 575 523 585]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}and storing them in another chart{CMMI10 10.9 i} δ{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}, see Eq. (16f).
  p4-l96  [317 589 525 599]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Our improved algorithm for computing all prefix
  p4-l97  [306 602 446 612]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}probabilities is shown in Alg. 3.
  p4-l98  [306 620 525 630]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}Proposition 3.{NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal 10.9 i} The complexity of our improved
  p4-l99  [306 632 468 644]  {NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal 10.9 i}algorithm is{CMSY10 10.9 i} O{CMR10 10.9}({CMMI10 10.9 i}N{CMR8 8}2{CMSY10 10.9 i} |N |{CMR8 8}3{CMR10 10.9} +{CMMI10 10.9 i} N{CMR8 8}3{CMSY10 10.9 i} |N |{CMR8 8}2{CMR10 10.9} ){NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal 10.9 i}.
  p4-l100  [306 655 524 665]  {NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal 10.9 i}Proof.{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} As before, Alg. 3 starts by precomputing
  p4-l101  [306 669 527 679]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}and assigning the left-corner expectations in lines 1–
  p4-l102  [306 680 526 692]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}3, which takes{CMSY10 10.9 i} O{CMR10 10.9}({CMSY10 10.9 i}|N |{CMR8 8}3{CMR10 10.9} ){NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} and{CMSY10 10.9 i} O{CMR10 10.9}({CMSY10 10.9 i}|N |{CMR8 8}2{CMR10 10.9} ){NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}, respectively.
  p4-l103  [306 696 526 706]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}We then initialize the prefix probabilities and com-
  p4-l104  [306 709 524 719]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}pute the inside probabilities in lines 5–8, taking
  p4-l105  [306 721 524 733]  {CMSY10 10.9 i}O{CMR10 10.9}({CMMI10 10.9 i}N{CMR8 8}2{CMSY10 10.9 i} |N |{CMR10 10.9}){NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}. As Eisner and Blatz (2007) show, one
  p4-l106  [306 734 526 747]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}can compute{CMMI10 10.9 i} β{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} (line 9) in{CMSY10 10.9 i} O{CMR10 10.9}({CMMI10 10.9 i}N{CMR8 8}2{CMSY10 10.9 i} |N |{CMR8 8}3{CMR10 10.9} +{CMMI10 10.9 i} N{CMR8 8}3{CMSY10 10.9 i} |N |{CMR8 8}2{CMR10 10.9} ){NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9},
  p4-l107  [306 750 524 760]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}thus improving the runtime of Alg. 1 for dense
