  p10-l13  [71 185 525 195]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}It is then straightforward to show that matrix addition is associative and commutative, while matrix
  p10-l14  [71 199 524 208]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}multiplication is associative and distributive over matrix addition. Hence, the set of square matrices
  p10-l15  [71 212 526 222]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}of dimension{CMMI10 10.9 i} d{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} over a semiring, with addition and multiplication as defined above, is itself a semiring.
  p10-l16  [71 226 458 235]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}Furthermore, by the element-wise definition of its addition operation, it is also complete.
  p10-l17  [71 249 86 259]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}B.2
  p10-l18  [97 249 264 259]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}Semiring-weighted prefix algorithm
  p10-l19  [70 267 524 278]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}We now consider a semiring-weighted CFG{CMSY10 10.9 i} G{CMR10 10.9} ={CMSY10 10.9 i} ⟨N{CMMI10 10.9 i} ,{CMR10 10.9} Σ{CMMI10 10.9 i},{CMR10 10.9} S{CMMI10 10.9 i},{CMSY10 10.9 i} R{CMMI10 10.9 i}, p,{CMSY10 10.9 i} W⟩{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}, where{CMSY10 10.9 i} N{CMMI10 10.9 i} ,{CMR10 10.9} Σ{CMMI10 10.9 i},{CMR10 10.9} S{CMMI10 10.9 i},{CMSY10 10.9 i} R{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} are defined as
  p10-l20  [71 280 524 291]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}before but the weighting function{CMMI10 10.9 i} p{CMR10 10.9}:{CMSY10 10.9 i} R → W{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} now maps rules to elements of a commutative semiring
  p10-l21  [71 293 524 305]  {CMSY10 10.9 i}W{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}.{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 8} 9{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} Note that we no longer require the rule weights to sum to one. As before, we define the matrix{CMMI10 10.9 i} P
  p10-l22  [71 307 524 318]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}as the square matrix of dimension{CMSY10 10.9 i} |N |{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} whose rows and columns are indexed by the non-terminals{CMSY10 10.9 i} N{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} in
  p10-l23  [71 322 524 333]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}some fixed order so that the entry{CMMI10 10.9 i} P{CMMI8 8 i}ij{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} corresponds to the weight of getting the non-terminal{CMR10 10.9} X{CMR8 8}j{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} on the left
  p10-l24  [71 335 524 346]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}after one rule application to{CMR10 10.9} X{CMR8 8}i{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} . Since the rule weights are no longer locally normalized, however, we
  p10-l25  [71 347 476 359]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}need to include the treesum under the right non-terminal of each rule as an additional term:{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 8}10
  p10-l26  [172 373 276 387]  {CMMI10 10.9 i}P{CMMI8 8 i}ij{CMR10 10.9} ={CMMI10 10.9 i} p{CMR10 10.9}(X{CMMI8 8 i}i{CMSY10 10.9 i} −→{CMR10 10.9} X{CMMI8 8 i}j{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} _{CMR10 10.9}){NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 6} def
  p10-l27  [267 372 297 388]  {CMR10 10.9}={CMEX10 10.9} M
  p10-l28  [279 390 299 398]  {CMR8 8}Y{CMSY8 8 i}∈N
  p10-l29  [301 375 423 387]  {CMMI10 10.9 i}p{CMR10 10.9}(X{CMMI8 8 i}i{CMSY10 10.9 i} −→{CMR10 10.9} X{CMMI8 8 i}j{CMR10 10.9}Y){CMSY10 10.9 i} ·{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} treesum{CMR10 10.9}(Y)
  p10-l30  [507 376 525 386]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(26)
  p10-l31  [70 411 524 422]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}We can then calculate the weight of getting{CMR10 10.9} X{CMMI8 8 i}j{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} from{CMR10 10.9} X{CMMI8 8 i}i{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} at the leftmost non-terminal after exactly{CMMI10 10.9 i} k
  p10-l32  [71 424 249 438]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}derivation steps as{CMR10 10.9} ({CMMI10 10.9 i}P{CMMI8 8 i} k{CMR10 10.9} ){CMMI8 8 i}ij{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} , where{CMMI10 10.9 i} P{CMMI8 8 i} k{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 6} def
  p10-l33  [241 426 286 436]  {CMR10 10.9}={CMMI10 10.9 i} P{CMSY10 10.9 i} · · ·{CMMI10 10.9 i} P
  p10-l34  [253 435 287 445]  {CMEX10 10.9}| {z }
  p10-l35  [258 443 282 451]  {CMMI8 8 i}k{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 8} times
  p10-l36  [287 427 524 437]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}. Finally, to get the left-corner expectations, we then
  p10-l37  [71 450 524 465]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}need to calculate the Kleene closure over the matrix{CMMI10 10.9 i} P{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9},{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 8} 11{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} that is, we want to find{CMMI10 10.9 i} P{CMSY8 8 i} ∗{CMR10 10.9} ={CMEX10 10.9} P{CMSY8 8 i}∞{CMMI8 8 i} k{CMR8 8}=0{CMMI10 10.9 i} P{CMMI8 8 i} k{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} . To
  p10-l38  [71 467 524 477]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}compute the Kleene closure over the transition matrix we can use an efficient algorithm by Lehmann
  p10-l39  [71 480 524 490]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(1977) which is a generalization of the well-known shortest-path algorithm usually attributed to Floyd
  p10-l40  [71 492 524 504]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}(1962) and Warshall (1962), but introduced previously by Roy (1959).{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 8}12{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} The algorithm works under the
  p10-l41  [71 507 525 517]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}condition that the Kleene closure of all individual matrix entries from semiring{CMSY10 10.9 i} W{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} exists, which is true for
  p10-l42  [71 520 422 531]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}our case since we assumed{CMSY10 10.9 i} W{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} to be complete. The algorithm is shown in Alg. 4.
  p10-l43  [70 546 477 556]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b}Algorithm 4{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} Lehmann’s algorithm for computing the Kleene closure over a transition matrix
  p10-l44  [77 563 170 573]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}1:{NimbusRomNo9L-Medi 10.9 b} def{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9} Lehmann({CMMI10 10.9 i}M{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}):
