  p10-l69  [84 691 524 701]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 6}9{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}We require that{CMSY9 9 i} W{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9} be{NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal 9 i} commutative{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9} because the order of rule applications does not affect string weight in a weighted CFG.
  p10-l70  [81 702 524 711]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 6}10{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}For locally normalized semirings, the treesum of any non-terminal is{CMBX9 9 b} 1{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}. In the general case when the weights are not
  p10-l71  [71 713 524 721]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}normalized, the treesum of a semiring-weighted WCFG can be computed through fixed point iteration in a similar way to
  p10-l72  [71 723 215 731]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}Newton’s method (Esparza et al., 2007).
  p10-l73  [81 732 462 742]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 6}11{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}Note that the Kleene closure exists since matrices with elements from a complete semiring are complete.
  p10-l74  [81 743 524 753]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 6}12{NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}The generalization is by way of choosing the appropriate semiring for the given problem. By the same token, Lehmann’s
  p10-l75  [71 755 525 763]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}algorithm can also be seen as a generalization of Kleene’s (1956) algorithm for converting finite-state automata to regular
  p10-l76  [71 765 501 773]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 9}expressions and the Gauss–Jordan algorithm for computing matrix inversion (see e.g. Althoen and McLaughlin (1987)).
  p10-l77  [292 783 303 793]  {NimbusRomNo9L-Regu 10.9}66
