  metadata: title='' authors=[] date='2024-05-03'
  == page 1  595 x 842  lines 27  pictures 0  drawings 1
  p1-l1  [83 118 514 136]  {SFBX2074 20.7}Qualia and the Formal Structure of Meaning
  p1-l2  [231 166 371 181]  {SFRM1440 14.3}Xerxes D. Arsiwalla{CMR10 10}1{CMMI10 10 i},{CMR10 10}2{CMMI10 10 i},{SFRM1000 10}∗
  p1-l3  [193 204 405 216]  {CMR8 8}1{SFTI1095 10.9 i}Pompeu Fabra University, Barcelona, Spain
  p1-l4  [220 222 377 234]  {CMR8 8}2{SFTI1095 10.9 i}Wolfram Institute, Illinois, USA
  p1-l5  [275 292 323 302]  {SFBX1095 10.9}Abstract
  p1-l6  [98 321 516 331]  {SFRM1095 10.9}This work explores the hypothesis that subjectively attributed meaning constitutes the
  p1-l7  [81 335 516 345]  {SFRM1095 10.9}phenomenal content of conscious experience. That is, phenomenal content is semantic. This
  p1-l8  [81 348 516 358]  {SFRM1095 10.9}form of subjective meaning manifests as an intrinsic and non-representational character of
  p1-l9  [81 362 516 372]  {SFRM1095 10.9}qualia. Empirically, subjective meaning is ubiquitous in conscious experiences. We point
  p1-l10  [81 376 516 385]  {SFRM1095 10.9}to phenomenological studies that lend evidence to support this. Furthermore, this notion
  p1-l11  [81 389 516 399]  {SFRM1095 10.9}of meaning closely relates to what Frege refers to as "sense", in metaphysics and philoso-
  p1-l12  [81 403 516 413]  {SFRM1095 10.9}phy of language. It also aligns with Peirce’s "interpretant", in semiotics. We discuss how
  p1-l13  [81 416 516 426]  {SFRM1095 10.9}Frege’s sense can also be extended to the raw feels of consciousness. Sense and reference
  p1-l14  [81 430 516 440]  {SFRM1095 10.9}both play a role in phenomenal experience. Moreover, within the context of the mind-matter
  p1-l15  [81 443 516 453]  {SFRM1095 10.9}relation, we provide a formalization of subjective meaning associated to one’s mental repre-
  p1-l16  [81 457 516 467]  {SFRM1095 10.9}sentations. Identifying the precise maps between the physical and mental domains, we argue
  p1-l17  [81 470 516 480]  {SFRM1095 10.9}that syntactic and semantic structures transcend language, and are realized within each of
  p1-l18  [81 484 516 494]  {SFRM1095 10.9}these domains. Formally, meaning is a relational attribute, realized via a map that interprets
  p1-l19  [81 498 516 507]  {SFRM1095 10.9}syntactic structures of a formal system within an appropriate semantic space. The image of
  p1-l20  [81 511 516 521]  {SFRM1095 10.9}this map within the mental domain is what is relevant for experience, and thus comprises
  p1-l21  [81 525 516 535]  {SFRM1095 10.9}the phenomenal content of qualia. We conclude with possible implications this may have for
  p1-l22  [81 538 284 548]  {SFRM1095 10.9}experience-based theories of consciousness.
  p1-l23  [89 598 545 609]  {SFTI1200 12 i}Keywords{SFRM1200 12}: Consciousness Science; Qualia; Mind-Matter Problem; Formal Structures; Lan-
  p1-l24  [52 612 191 623]  {SFRM1200 12}guage; Meaning; Semiotics.
  p1-l25  [66 730 190 740]  {SFRM0700 7}∗{SFTT1000 10}x.d.arsiwalla@gmail.com
  p1-l26  [296 759 302 770]  {SFRM1200 12}1
