  == page 2  595 x 842  lines 39  pictures 0  drawings 0
  p2-l1  [52 72 125 87]  {SFBX1728 17.2}Contents
  p2-l2  [52 112 144 123]  {SFBX1200 12}1 Introduction
  p2-l3  [539 112 545 123]  {SFBX1200 12}2
  p2-l4  [52 149 384 159]  {SFBX1200 12}2 Subjective Meaning in Phenomenology of Experience
  p2-l5  [538 149 545 159]  {SFBX1200 12}4
  p2-l6  [52 185 311 196]  {SFBX1200 12}3 Do Raw Feels Have Subjective Meaning?
  p2-l7  [538 185 545 196]  {SFBX1200 12}9
  p2-l8  [52 221 269 232]  {SFBX1200 12}4 The Formal Structure of Meaning
  p2-l9  [532 222 545 232]  {SFBX1200 12}11
  p2-l10  [52 258 131 269]  {SFBX1200 12}5 Discussion
  p2-l11  [532 258 545 269]  {SFBX1200 12}18
  p2-l12  [52 304 61 319]  {SFBX1728 17.2}1
  p2-l13  [80 304 182 319]  {SFBX1728 17.2}Introduction
  p2-l14  [52 344 545 355]  {SFRM1200 12}How should one formulate a mathematical or metaphysical framework that admits a formalization
  p2-l15  [52 358 545 369]  {SFRM1200 12}of the phenomenal contents of conscious experience? The phenomenal contents or the so-called
  p2-l16  [52 373 545 384]  {SFRM1200 12}qualia of consciousness are inherently subjective attributes of experience, and by most accounts,
  p2-l17  [52 387 545 399]  {SFRM1200 12}deemed non-representational [127]. They refer to the raw, immediate and phenomenal aspects
  p2-l18  [52 402 545 412]  {SFRM1200 12}of perception, sensation or mental states. Phenomenal content, by its very nature, is subjective;
  p2-l19  [52 416 545 427]  {SFRM1200 12}that is, available only through one’s first-person perspective. Qualia are at the heart of the "what
  p2-l20  [52 430 545 442]  {SFRM1200 12}it’s like to be" character of conscious experience, as elucidated by Thomas Nagel in 1974 [97]. If
  p2-l21  [52 445 545 456]  {SFRM1200 12}it were possible to provide a complete third-person description of phenomenal states, that would
  p2-l22  [52 459 545 470]  {SFRM1200 12}resolve the "what it’s like to be" problem of consciousness. Qualia cannot be fully described, com-
  p2-l23  [52 474 545 485]  {SFRM1200 12}municated or understood through any objective third-person account or computational routine.
  p2-l24  [52 488 545 499]  {SFRM1200 12}For instance, attempts to fully convey the subjective experience of tasting chocolate or perceiving
  p2-l25  [52 503 545 514]  {SFRM1200 12}the color red through objective descriptions or computational algorithms fall short of capturing
  p2-l26  [52 517 545 528]  {SFRM1200 12}the full qualitative nature of those experiences. These observations collectively indicate that cer-
  p2-l27  [52 532 545 542]  {SFRM1200 12}tain aspects of conscious experiences pose challenges for all attempts that seek to fully explain
  p2-l28  [52 546 545 557]  {SFRM1200 12}or replicate qualia through either exclusively computational, or objective descriptions, that is,
  p2-l29  [52 560 545 571]  {SFRM1200 12}potential third-person descriptions. Besides potential implications for philosophy of mind, this
  p2-l30  [52 575 545 586]  {SFRM1200 12}problem also suggests new frontiers for the philosophy of science itself; namely, how does one ex-
  p2-l31  [52 589 545 600]  {SFRM1200 12}tend the existing scientific paradigm based on third-person perspective to account for phenomena
  p2-l32  [52 604 545 615]  {SFRM1200 12}that are subjective and non-representational - that is, amenable only via first-person perspective?
  p2-l33  [89 628 545 639]  {SFRM1200 12}For these very reasons, a scientific description or theory elucidating the nature of qualia,
  p2-l34  [52 643 545 654]  {SFRM1200 12}or one explaining the phenomenology of conscious experiences continues to be an extremely chal-
  p2-l35  [52 657 545 669]  {SFRM1200 12}lenging problem at the heart of modern science. Attempts at any such theoretical description (of
  p2-l36  [52 671 300 683]  {SFRM1200 12}qualia) call for at least two essential ingredients:
  p2-l37  [52 700 545 712]  {SFSL1200 12 i}(i) A whole new scientific paradigm of subjectivity, one which describes phenomena from the
  p2-l38  [52 715 325 726]  {SFSL1200 12 i}intrinsic point-of-view, or "the view from the inside".
  p2-l39  [296 759 302 770]  {SFRM1200 12}2
  == page 3  595 x 842  lines 44  pictures 0  drawings 1
  p3-l1  [52 75 545 87]  {SFSL1200 12 i}(ii) A metaphysical (and possibly mathematical) formalization of non-representational entities
  p3-l2  [52 89 378 101]  {SFSL1200 12 i}(potentially in the form of abstract objects and their relations).
  p3-l3  [89 114 545 126]  {SFRM1200 12}Currently, in the absence of a complete description of (or difficulty conceptualizing) the
  p3-l4  [52 129 545 140]  {SFRM1200 12}above two ingredients, one often relies on constructs using information, computation or causality
  p3-l5  [52 143 545 155]  {SFRM1200 12}[43, 99, 124]. These are certainly useful to quantify computational processes or identify states of
  p3-l6  [52 157 545 169]  {SFRM1200 12}complexity, but that is still rather far from capturing the very phenomenal character of qualia{SFRM0800 8}1{SFRM1200 12} .
  p3-l7  [52 172 545 183]  {SFRM1200 12}Possibly, a metaphysical formalization of phenomenal properties is what is needed. Even though
  p3-l8  [52 187 545 198]  {SFRM1200 12}a satisfactory solution to this problem does not exist yet, here we put forth a proposal that
  p3-l9  [52 201 545 212]  {SFRM1200 12}identifies subjectively attributed meaning with the phenomenal content of conscious experience.
  p3-l10  [52 216 545 227]  {SFRM1200 12}We then discuss how subjective meaning of this kind can be formalized within the setting of the
  p3-l11  [52 230 163 241]  {SFRM1200 12}mind-matter relation.
  p3-l12  [89 254 545 266]  {SFRM1200 12}More broadly, within the cognitive sciences (inclusive of approaches focussing on cognition,
  p3-l13  [52 269 545 281]  {SFRM1200 12}consciousness, computation, and clinical practice), meaning has been discussed in one of two
  p3-l14  [52 283 545 295]  {SFRM1200 12}ways: either as a by-product of explicit neuronal or computational mechanisms [45], or as an
  p3-l15  [52 298 545 310]  {SFRM1200 12}intentionality relation between mental representations and what they represent [24, 89]. These
  p3-l16  [52 313 545 324]  {SFRM1200 12}two approaches differ in metaphysical ontology. The former assumes a purely physicalistic stance,
  p3-l17  [52 327 545 338]  {SFRM1200 12}whereas the latter presupposes a fundamental relationality between the physical and the mental.
  p3-l18  [89 352 545 363]  {SFRM1200 12}For instance, in the former scenario, the meaning associated to one’s experience is believed
  p3-l19  [52 366 545 378]  {SFRM1200 12}to arise from various cognitive processes and associations thereof [45, 58, 71]. Such cognitive
  p3-l20  [52 381 545 392]  {SFRM1200 12}processes may then serve as accessories to conscious experience. In such a view, when an individual
  p3-l21  [52 395 545 406]  {SFRM1200 12}experiences a scene, it triggers associations, memories, emotions and cognitive mechanisms that
  p3-l22  [52 410 545 421]  {SFRM1200 12}provide cognitive significance to that particular experience. For instance, the color red might
  p3-l23  [52 424 545 435]  {SFRM1200 12}evoke emotions, cultural associations or memories linked to the color. Here, meaning is not
  p3-l24  [52 439 545 450]  {SFRM1200 12}considered an inherent feature of qualia, but something that is constructed and attributed by
  p3-l25  [52 453 545 464]  {SFRM1200 12}an individual based upon their cognitive deliberations and psychological states. The significance
  p3-l26  [52 467 545 478]  {SFRM1200 12}and interpretation of any experience, is then shaped by one’s perceptions, memories, beliefs and
  p3-l27  [52 482 545 493]  {SFRM1200 12}also cultural influences. In other words, meaning construction is discussed through the lens of
  p3-l28  [52 496 545 508]  {SFRM1200 12}cognitive (and metacognitive) mechanistic explanations [45]; that is, as an interpretative layer
  p3-l29  [52 511 545 522]  {SFRM1200 12}imposed on top of experience, rather than an inherent feature of raw subjective experience itself.
  p3-l30  [89 536 545 546]  {SFRM1200 12}In contrast to meaning as a cognitive by-product, the second approach mentioned above,
  p3-l31  [52 550 545 561]  {SFRM1200 12}decrees a more prominent role for meaning with regard to consciousness. Here, meaning is dis-
  p3-l32  [52 564 545 576]  {SFRM1200 12}cussed in the sense of Brentano’s notion of "intentionality" [33]. In the present work, our discus-
  p3-l33  [52 579 545 590]  {SFRM1200 12}sions align more with the stance that meaning is indeed fundamental to consciousness. Further-
  p3-l34  [52 593 545 604]  {SFRM1200 12}more, we propose that subjective meaning constitutes the phenomenal content of all conscious
  p3-l35  [52 608 545 619]  {SFRM1200 12}experience. Compiling evidence from phenomenological studies, we demonstrate that this form
  p3-l36  [52 622 545 633]  {SFRM1200 12}of meaning is ubiquitous to the qualia of consciousness. As mentioned, it is also important to
  p3-l37  [52 637 545 648]  {SFRM1200 12}formalize the mathematical or metaphysical structure of this kind of meaning, if one is to make
  p3-l38  [52 651 545 662]  {SFRM1200 12}progress on the problem of qualia. Fortunately, seminal works in the philosophy of mind and
  p3-l39  [52 665 545 677]  {SFRM1200 12}language [52] (see also [48, 135]), as well as mathematical advances in natural language seman-
  p3-l40  [66 688 545 698]  {SFRM0700 7}1{SFRM1000 10}Roger Penrose has been a vociferous proponent of the view that consciousness necessarily involves a funda-
  p3-l41  [52 701 545 711]  {SFRM1000 10}mentally non-computational process [105] (see also [15] for a recent perspective on this point). An early remnant
  p3-l42  [52 713 545 722]  {SFRM1000 10}of this view, though not explicitly expressed in terms of consciousness, may be traced back to Alan Turing’s PhD
  p3-l43  [52 725 332 735]  {SFRM1000 10}thesis, where he contrasts between intuition and ingenuity [126].
  p3-l44  [296 759 302 770]  {SFRM1200 12}3
  == page 4  595 x 842  lines 36  pictures 0  drawings 5
  p4-l1  [52 75 545 87]  {SFRM1200 12}tics [40] equip us with the right conceptual groundwork for formalizing subjective meaning with
  p4-l2  [52 90 253 101]  {SFRM1200 12}regard to the problem of consciousness.
  p4-l3  [89 115 545 126]  {SFRM1200 12}The outline of this paper is as follows: in section 2, we provide phenomenological evidence
  p4-l4  [52 129 545 140]  {SFRM1200 12}to support the claim that meaning is omnipresent in qualia; in section 3, we discuss how Frege’s
  p4-l5  [52 143 545 154]  {SFRM1200 12}notion of sense extends to the raw feels of conscious experience; in section 4, we formalize sub-
  p4-l6  [52 158 545 169]  {SFRM1200 12}jective meaning of mental representations in the context of the mind-matter relation; lastly, we
  p4-l7  [52 172 263 183]  {SFRM1200 12}conclude in section 5 with final thoughts.
  p4-l8  [52 219 61 234]  {SFBX1728 17.2}2
  p4-l9  [80 219 513 235]  {SFBX1728 17.2}Subjective Meaning in Phenomenology of Experience
  p4-l10  [56 278 548 289]  {SFBX1200 12}Proposition 2.1.{SFTI1200 12 i} We hypothesize a notion of subjectively attributed meaning or subjective mean-
  p4-l11  [56 292 548 303]  {SFTI1200 12 i}ing as an inherent feature of the qualia of consciousness. This subjective meaning constitutes the
  p4-l12  [56 307 304 318]  {SFTI1200 12 i}phenomenal content of all conscious experiences.
  p4-l13  [89 356 545 368]  {SFRM1200 12}Meaning is not merely about language{SFRM0800 8}2{SFRM1200 12} , nor is it merely a construct about the world "as
  p4-l14  [52 372 545 383]  {SFRM1200 12}is". The notion of meaning has been extensively discussed by several philosophers in the context of
  p4-l15  [52 386 545 398]  {SFRM1200 12}language, mind, metaphysics and epistemology [52,63,68,74]. How cognitive and artificial agents
  p4-l16  [52 401 545 412]  {SFRM1200 12}learn to make associations about things in the world, is deeply relevant to our understanding of
  p4-l17  [52 415 233 426]  {SFRM1200 12}biological and artificial intelligence.
  p4-l18  [89 440 545 451]  {SFRM1200 12}Discussions about meaning-making or meaning-generation broadly involve some kind of
  p4-l19  [52 454 545 465]  {SFRM1200 12}attribution of either significance, interpretation or semantic content to an agent’s actions, per-
  p4-l20  [52 468 545 480]  {SFRM1200 12}ceptions and thoughts [45,58,71]. Some of these attributions may be representable using compu-
  p4-l21  [52 483 545 494]  {SFRM1200 12}tational principles, and hence be realized as specific cognitive functions. Alternatively, there may
  p4-l22  [52 498 545 509]  {SFRM1200 12}also exist attributions that are non-representational. These, one might refer to as phenomenal,
  p4-l23  [52 512 307 523]  {SFRM1200 12}and hence intrinsic to the qualia of consciousness.
  p4-l24  [52 541 545 552]  {SFSS1200 12}Note that it is this latter class, of non-representational significance attributions, that we will refer
  p4-l25  [52 555 345 566]  {SFSS1200 12}to as subjectively attributed meaning or subjective meaning.
  p4-l26  [52 584 545 595]  {SFRM1200 12}Let us briefly compare the above two kinds of significance attributions. Computationally repre-
  p4-l27  [52 599 545 610]  {SFRM1200 12}sentable attributions are the ones that are particularly well-aligned with an embodied cognition
  p4-l28  [52 613 545 624]  {SFRM1200 12}perspective, where an agent has to learn the affordances of its environment for planning its ac-
  p4-l29  [52 627 545 639]  {SFRM1200 12}tions and directing itself towards its goals [95, 118]. By some accounts, this process also requires
  p4-l30  [52 642 545 653]  {SFRM1200 12}the agent to actively infer or at least conceptualize its own actions and perceptions, as well as
  p4-l31  [52 656 545 668]  {SFRM1200 12}that of other agents in the world [57, 87]. Meaning (in this context of cognitive functions) per-
  p4-l32  [52 671 545 682]  {SFRM1200 12}tains to the knowledge or significance that an experience acquires via mechanistic controls or
  p4-l33  [52 685 545 696]  {SFRM1200 12}computational processes, over and above its raw phenomenal content. In that sense, this is a
  p4-l34  [52 700 545 711]  {SFRM1200 12}deliberative meaning-making process. For example, meaning associated to perceptual processes
  p4-l35  [66 721 492 732]  {SFRM0700 7}2{SFRM1000 10} It was David Mumford who first pointed out that "Grammar isn’t merely part of language" [96].
  p4-l36  [296 759 302 770]  {SFRM1200 12}4
  == page 5  595 x 842  lines 42  pictures 0  drawings 1
  p5-l1  [52 75 545 87]  {SFRM1200 12}involve the interpretation and understanding (often deliberative) of sensory stimuli, such as recog-
  p5-l2  [52 90 545 101]  {SFRM1200 12}nizing various representations of shapes, colors and patterns, or perceiving objects in the external
  p5-l3  [52 104 545 115]  {SFRM1200 12}world. In language and cognitive semiotics, meaning involves the understanding of language-
  p5-l4  [52 119 545 130]  {SFRM1200 12}based representations, concepts and symbols, when cognitive agents engage in understanding and
  p5-l5  [52 133 545 145]  {SFRM1200 12}communicating through language, signs and gestures [140]. Similarly, cognitive states involving
  p5-l6  [52 147 545 159]  {SFRM1200 12}beliefs, biases or intentions, involve some kind of value attribution by the individual (based on
  p5-l7  [52 162 238 174]  {SFRM1200 12}psychological conditioning) [73, 119].
  p5-l8  [89 187 545 198]  {SFRM1200 12}On the other hand, non-representational significance attributions are of the type that are
  p5-l9  [52 201 545 213]  {SFRM1200 12}immediate (as opposed to deliberative) and of a phenomenal character. They distinctly mean
  p5-l10  [52 216 545 227]  {SFRM1200 12}something to the experiencing subject. Examples include moments of intuitive insights, profound
  p5-l11  [52 230 545 242]  {SFRM1200 12}personal revelations, states of transcendence [28, 86, 113] (and more such examples discussed in
  p5-l12  [52 244 545 256]  {SFRM1200 12}Table 1 below). While the phenomenology of these experiences can certainly be investigated qual-
  p5-l13  [52 259 545 270]  {SFRM1200 12}itatively using introspective methods, the full scope of the meaning experienced in all of these
  p5-l14  [52 274 354 284]  {SFRM1200 12}instances is only available through first-person perspective.
  p5-l15  [52 302 545 313]  {SFSS1200 12}For these reasons, such subjectively attributed meaning is an observer-dependent construct: it lit-
  p5-l16  [52 317 248 328]  {SFSS1200 12}erally holds in the mind of the beholder!
  p5-l17  [52 344 545 357]  {SFRM1200 12}Meaning of this form is a genuinely subjective quality{SFRM0800 8}3{SFRM1200 12} . In the discussion that follows, we will
  p5-l18  [52 360 545 371]  {SFRM1200 12}elaborate how this form of meaning might be directly tied to the qualia of consciousness, and
  p5-l19  [52 375 545 386]  {SFRM1200 12}how it may in fact manifest as the phenomenal content of all experience. We also point to
  p5-l20  [52 389 299 400]  {SFRM1200 12}phenomenological evidence in favor of this view.
  p5-l21  [52 414 545 424]  {SFBX1200 12}Remark 2.1.{SFTI1200 12 i} Earlier, we mentioned about meaning as discussed in the sense of Brentano’s notion
  p5-l22  [52 427 545 439]  {SFTI1200 12 i}of "intentionality" [33]. There, meaning is conceived as a relation between the physical and the
  p5-l23  [52 442 545 454]  {SFTI1200 12 i}mental (see also [23, 24]). Later, in section 4, we will indeed formalize such a relation between
  p5-l24  [52 457 545 468]  {SFTI1200 12 i}the physical and mental domains. How does this relational notion of meaning corroborate with
  p5-l25  [52 471 313 482]  {SFTI1200 12 i}the idea of subjective meaning being proposed here?
  p5-l26  [89 496 545 507]  {SFTI1200 12 i}Notice that, meaning, when conceptualized as a relation between the physical and the men-
  p5-l27  [52 511 545 521]  {SFTI1200 12 i}tal, is necessarily an extrinsically defined notion. Namely, extrinsic to any physical and mental
  p5-l28  [52 525 545 536]  {SFTI1200 12 i}observer. On the other hand, one may ask, from the intrinsic point of view of an observer within
  p5-l29  [52 539 545 550]  {SFTI1200 12 i}the mental domain, how does this physical to mental relation manifest? In other words, what
  p5-l30  [52 554 545 565]  {SFTI1200 12 i}does the mind experience of this relation? If one thinks of this relation as a formal map from a
  p5-l31  [52 568 545 580]  {SFTI1200 12 i}domain (physical) to a codomain (mental), then the answer is: the image of this map within the
  p5-l32  [52 583 545 594]  {SFTI1200 12 i}codomain. Subjective meaning is thus the mental image of Brentano’s intentionality. This is the
  p5-l33  [52 597 545 608]  {SFTI1200 12 i}facet of meaning that a conscious agent can experience with their mind. For this reason, we could
  p5-l34  [52 612 545 623]  {SFTI1200 12 i}have also referred to this notion of meaning as intrinsic meaning. The full relational map is only
  p5-l35  [52 626 545 637]  {SFTI1200 12 i}accessible to a metaphysical observer. Working from such an intrinsic point of view is of direct
  p5-l36  [52 641 348 651]  {SFTI1200 12 i}relevance to any experience-based theory of consciousness.
  p5-l37  [52 665 545 676]  {SFBX1200 12}Remark 2.2.{SFTI1200 12 i} Let us make a brief comment about qualia in more general modalities. Even though
  p5-l38  [52 679 545 691]  {SFTI1200 12 i}a sizable fraction of works discussing qualia often anchor around sensory experiences ("the redness
  p5-l39  [66 702 545 712]  {SFRM0700 7}3{SFRM1000 10}As such, the "objective" use of meaning in language and social communication is only a matter of standard-
  p5-l40  [52 715 545 724]  {SFRM1000 10}ization of what symbols may refer to, or formation of social norms and conventions within and between groups of
  p5-l41  [52 726 143 736]  {SFRM1000 10}agents [3, 55, 79, 110].
  p5-l42  [296 759 302 770]  {SFRM1200 12}5
  == page 6  595 x 842  lines 40  pictures 0  drawings 4
  p6-l1  [52 75 545 87]  {SFTI1200 12 i}of red", for instance), it is to be noted that qualia also refer to aspects of conscious experiences
  p6-l2  [52 89 545 101]  {SFTI1200 12 i}beyond the purely sensory [37, 88, 127]. Examples include; (a) qualia associated to subjective
  p6-l3  [52 104 545 115]  {SFTI1200 12 i}feelings and emotions, like the feeling of joy, sadness, or fear, which might not be directly tied
  p6-l4  [52 118 545 130]  {SFTI1200 12 i}(though indirectly related) to sensory input; (b) qualia associated to thought processes, such as
  p6-l5  [52 133 545 145]  {SFTI1200 12 i}the immediate awareness of having a thought, a mental image or an idea; (c) qualia associated to
  p6-l6  [52 148 545 159]  {SFTI1200 12 i}introspective states, such as the subjective sense of self-awareness, reflective thought, or experience
  p6-l7  [52 162 545 174]  {SFTI1200 12 i}of one’s own mental states; to name a few (see [37, 88, 127] and references therein). The above
  p6-l8  [52 177 545 188]  {SFTI1200 12 i}and other examples of qualia not exclusively tied to sensory perceptions will be relevant for us
  p6-l9  [52 191 79 202]  {SFTI1200 12 i}later.
  p6-l10  [89 223 545 234]  {SFRM1200 12}Empirically, how could subjective meaning manifest as the phenomenal content of qualia
  p6-l11  [52 237 545 249]  {SFRM1200 12}(in the broader sense of qualia mentioned in the remark above)? For instance, consider the case
  p6-l12  [52 251 545 262]  {SFRM1200 12}of qualia associated to emotions: one may ask whether emotional feelings might come endowed
  p6-l13  [52 266 545 277]  {SFRM1200 12}with some form of subjective meaning that is immediate to the experience? Now, emotions
  p6-l14  [52 280 545 291]  {SFRM1200 12}and feelings as intricate mental states certainly do carry a personal form of significance for the
  p6-l15  [52 295 545 306]  {SFRM1200 12}experiencing subject. Furthermore, this form of significance is highly subjective, contextual to
  p6-l16  [52 309 545 320]  {SFRM1200 12}the individual’s history, and intimately tied to their internal states. But significance of this kind
  p6-l17  [52 323 545 335]  {SFRM1200 12}is in fact a semantic quality, in that, it means something to the subject (in the next section
  p6-l18  [52 338 545 350]  {SFRM1200 12}we compare this notion of subjective meaning to Frege’s notion of sense). It is this aspect of
  p6-l19  [52 352 545 364]  {SFRM1200 12}meaning that we claim is raw and immediate to the emotion (as opposed to a form of deliberative
  p6-l20  [52 367 545 379]  {SFRM1200 12}interpretation of the emotion). Now, discussions about phenomenal properties usually refer to the
  p6-l21  [52 381 545 392]  {SFRM1200 12}immediate, intrinsic and non-representational qualities of conscious experiences that constitute
  p6-l22  [52 395 545 407]  {SFRM1200 12}the "what it is like" aspect of those experiences [97]. The form of meaning that we are referring
  p6-l23  [52 410 545 422]  {SFRM1200 12}to above, is intrinsically subjective (involving personal significance and value attribution) and
  p6-l24  [52 425 545 436]  {SFRM1200 12}non-representational in the same way that sensory qualia are. One may report one’s emotional
  p6-l25  [52 439 545 450]  {SFRM1200 12}experiences categorically, but there is no way to convey an objectively third-person perspective as
  p6-l26  [52 454 545 465]  {SFRM1200 12}to what that emotion means to the experiencing subject. This meaning or significance is closely
  p6-l27  [52 468 292 479]  {SFRM1200 12}associated to the raw feel of the emotion itself.
  p6-l28  [89 493 545 504]  {SFRM1200 12}First, let us discuss why meaning of the above kind may be closely entwined with the
  p6-l29  [52 507 178 518]  {SFRM1200 12}subject’s own self-states.
  p6-l30  [52 529 545 540]  {SFBX1200 12}Remark 2.3.{SFTI1200 12 i} Let us note that issues related to personal significance or personal value attribution
  p6-l31  [52 543 521 554]  {SFTI1200 12 i}within the realm of a subject’s experiences are features closely associated to their self-model.
  p6-l32  [52 575 104 586]  {SFRM1200 12}Therefore:
  p6-l33  [56 608 548 618]  {SFBX1200 12}Proposition 2.2.{SFTI1200 12 i} We posit that subjective meaning is phenomenologically relevant to all qualia
  p6-l34  [56 622 548 633]  {SFTI1200 12 i}associated with the subject’s self-states, or at least those involving any relation between self-states
  p6-l35  [56 637 328 647]  {SFTI1200 12 i}to other states within the subject’s experiential realm.
  p6-l36  [89 686 545 697]  {SFRM1200 12}From a systems theory perspective, a self-model is a construct that encompasses an in-
  p6-l37  [52 701 545 712]  {SFRM1200 12}dividual’s interpretations, value attributions and representations concerning their own identity,
  p6-l38  [52 715 545 727]  {SFRM1200 12}internal states, belief systems, emotional states and behaviors [89, 91]. This internal model con-
  p6-l39  [52 730 545 741]  {SFRM1200 12}tributes to the recognition of one’s own goals and value systems, and consequently, it influences
  p6-l40  [296 759 302 770]  {SFRM1200 12}6
  == page 7  595 x 842  lines 51  pictures 0  drawings 62
  p7-l1  [52 75 545 87]  {SFRM1200 12}all external models of the subject (such as those pertaining to the world, or models of other
  p7-l2  [52 89 545 101]  {SFRM1200 12}agents that the subject interacts with). In the more specific context of consciousness studies, the
  p7-l3  [52 104 545 115]  {SFRM1200 12}relevant kind of self-models are what are referred to as "phenomenal self-models". These models
  p7-l4  [52 118 545 130]  {SFRM1200 12}were proposed by Thomas Metzinger in 2004 [89]. They are internal dynamical representations
  p7-l5  [52 133 545 144]  {SFRM1200 12}of the agent, and carry phenomenal content. A feature of these self-models is that they not only
  p7-l6  [52 148 545 159]  {SFRM1200 12}constitute a representation of the agent, but they also co-represent the representational relation
  p7-l7  [52 162 545 174]  {SFRM1200 12}(between the agent and the world) itself [89, 91]. In other words, they are equipped with a phe-
  p7-l8  [52 175 545 188]  {SFRM1200 12}nomenal model of the intentionality relation{SFRM0800 8}4{SFRM1200 12} . According to Metzinger, phenomenal self-models
  p7-l9  [52 191 545 202]  {SFRM1200 12}are characterized by phenomenal properties pertaining to ownership, perspectivalness and self-
  p7-l10  [52 205 545 217]  {SFRM1200 12}hood; all of which are essential for creating a first-person perspective [89, 91]. Presumably, these
  p7-l11  [52 220 545 231]  {SFRM1200 12}phenomenal properties realize what is referred to as sense of ownership, sense of situatedness
  p7-l12  [52 234 545 245]  {SFRM1200 12}and sense of self respectively. The "sense" in the above terms is indeed a form of subjective
  p7-l13  [52 248 545 260]  {SFRM1200 12}significance or knowingness, and hence a form of meaning (the link between sense and meaning is
  p7-l14  [52 263 545 275]  {SFRM1200 12}further discussed in the next section). For that reason, an experience of self, necessarily features
  p7-l15  [52 278 323 289]  {SFRM1200 12}subjective meaning of the kind we have noted above.
  p7-l16  [89 302 545 313]  {SFRM1200 12}Furthermore, one may extrapolate that the above stated properties of a phenomenal self-
  p7-l17  [52 317 545 328]  {SFRM1200 12}model would also ascribe subjective significance to the very manner in which an agent interacts
  p7-l18  [52 331 545 343]  {SFRM1200 12}with and perceives the world. In other words, the subjective meaning attached to (or in part,
  p7-l19  [52 345 545 357]  {SFRM1200 12}defining) one’s self attributes, will influence the way a subject experiences the world from their
  p7-l20  [52 360 545 371]  {SFRM1200 12}first-person perspective. Their meaning-attributes of self affect their meaning-attributes of the
  p7-l21  [52 374 383 386]  {SFRM1200 12}world (and vice-versa, owing to feedback from the environment).
  p7-l22  [89 399 545 410]  {SFRM1200 12}Let us now discuss literature examples which demonstrate the above. What we are sug-
  p7-l23  [52 414 545 425]  {SFRM1200 12}gesting here that subjective meaning, as the phenomenal content of experience, may well be
  p7-l24  [52 428 545 439]  {SFRM1200 12}ubiquitous to consciousness. Table 1 summarizes various examples of conscious experiences, typ-
  p7-l25  [52 443 545 454]  {SFRM1200 12}ically those besides the purely sensory ones, which explicitly evoke a personal form of meaning
  p7-l26  [52 457 449 468]  {SFRM1200 12}for the experiencing subject. The text thereafter, further elaborates on these.
  p7-l27  [174 484 183 495]  {SFRM1200 12}1.
  p7-l28  [198 484 427 495]  {SFRM1200 12}Spiritual realization and personal revelations
  p7-l29  [174 501 183 512]  {SFRM1200 12}2.
  p7-l30  [214 501 411 512]  {SFRM1200 12}Eureka moments and intuitive insights
  p7-l31  [174 519 183 530]  {SFRM1200 12}3.
  p7-l32  [217 519 407 530]  {SFRM1200 12}Experiences of aesthetic appreciation
  p7-l33  [174 536 183 547]  {SFRM1200 12}4.
  p7-l34  [238 536 386 547]  {SFRM1200 12}Feelings of love and empathy
  p7-l35  [174 554 183 565]  {SFRM1200 12}5.
  p7-l36  [203 554 421 565]  {SFRM1200 12}States of enlightenment and transcendence
  p7-l37  [174 571 183 582]  {SFRM1200 12}6.
  p7-l38  [234 571 391 582]  {SFRM1200 12}Altered states of consciousness
  p7-l39  [174 588 183 599]  {SFRM1200 12}7.
  p7-l40  [250 588 375 599]  {SFRM1200 12}Feelings of synchronicity
  p7-l41  [174 606 183 617]  {SFRM1200 12}8.
  p7-l42  [250 606 375 617]  {SFRM1200 12}Out-of-body experiences
  p7-l43  [174 623 183 634]  {SFRM1200 12}9.
  p7-l44  [253 623 371 634]  {SFRM1200 12}Near-death experiences
  p7-l45  [171 641 186 652]  {SFRM1200 12}10.
  p7-l46  [278 641 347 652]  {SFRM1200 12}States of flow
  p7-l47  [92 667 500 678]  {SFRM1200 12}Table 1: Examples of conscious experiences that constitute subjective meaning.
  p7-l48  [66 692 545 703]  {SFRM0700 7}4{SFRM1000 10}According to Metzinger, a system of "third-order embodiment" possess phenomenal self-models [91]. Such a
  p7-l49  [52 706 545 715]  {SFRM1000 10}system explicitly models itself as an embodied being, and maps some of the representational content generated in
  p7-l50  [52 718 526 727]  {SFRM1000 10}this process directly onto conscious experience. In other words, it consciously experiences itself as embodied.
  p7-l51  [296 759 302 770]  {SFRM1200 12}7
  == page 8  595 x 842  lines 41  pictures 0  drawings 0
  p8-l1  [66 75 545 86]  {SFRM1200 12}1. Moments of personal revelation, existential realization or sudden transformative life experi-
  p8-l2  [81 90 545 101]  {SFRM1200 12}ences like a spiritual realization, a meditative revelation or an epiphany have been discussed
  p8-l3  [81 104 545 116]  {SFRM1200 12}in [26, 61, 86, 93, 128]. These experiences have been reported to involve a deep and immedi-
  p8-l4  [81 119 545 130]  {SFRM1200 12}ate personal significance to the experiencing subject. For that reason, the aforementioned
  p8-l5  [81 133 545 144]  {SFRM1200 12}studies investigating these experiences lean towards consciousness, rather than seeking ex-
  p8-l6  [81 148 381 159]  {SFRM1200 12}planations based on purely cognitive control or processing.
  p8-l7  [66 172 545 183]  {SFRM1200 12}2. Sudden realizations such as "eureka" moments, intuitive insights, instinctive or gut feel-
  p8-l8  [81 187 545 197]  {SFRM1200 12}ings, that provide an immediate sense or perceived understanding to complex problems also
  p8-l9  [81 201 545 212]  {SFRM1200 12}possess subjective meaning within conscious experiences (see [113,131]). Likewise, for intro-
  p8-l10  [81 215 545 226]  {SFRM1200 12}spective awareness; that is, the immediate awareness of one’s own mental states, thoughts
  p8-l11  [81 229 242 241]  {SFRM1200 12}or introspective processes [129].
  p8-l12  [66 254 545 265]  {SFRM1200 12}3. Profound experiences of artistic or aesthetic appreciation, such as being deeply moved by
  p8-l13  [81 269 545 280]  {SFRM1200 12}a piece of music, art or even nature. Feelings of awe, wonder or personal connection when
  p8-l14  [81 283 545 294]  {SFRM1200 12}experiencing aesthetic perceptions. All of these constitute examples of conscious experiences
  p8-l15  [81 297 545 309]  {SFRM1200 12}that invoke an immediate kind of meaning in the form of personal significance [39, 49, 111].
  p8-l16  [66 322 545 333]  {SFRM1200 12}4. Intensely felt connections in personal relationships, profound moments of empathy or ex-
  p8-l17  [81 336 545 347]  {SFRM1200 12}periences of deep understanding and resonance with others, including feelings of love, are
  p8-l18  [81 350 544 362]  {SFRM1200 12}examples of experiences deeply tied to a purely subjective notion of meaning [35, 122, 138].
  p8-l19  [66 375 545 387]  {SFRM1200 12}5. Moments of transcendence (either through meditation, contemplation or religious practices),
  p8-l20  [81 390 545 401]  {SFRM1200 12}perceived states of enlightenment, metaphysical awareness, spiritual awakening, mystical
  p8-l21  [81 404 545 415]  {SFRM1200 12}encounters eliciting immediate, ineffable feelings of unity and connectedness either with a
  p8-l22  [81 418 545 430]  {SFRM1200 12}higher state of being or a divine presence [28,51,64,85,120]. These too, hold deep personal
  p8-l23  [81 433 221 444]  {SFRM1200 12}significance for the subject.
  p8-l24  [66 457 545 468]  {SFRM1200 12}6. Experiences involving altered states of consciousness, induced by meditation, trance or
  p8-l25  [81 472 545 483]  {SFRM1200 12}altered perceptions; such as those during lucid dreaming, mindfulness, heightened self-
  p8-l26  [81 486 376 498]  {SFRM1200 12}awareness, or those induced by hallucinogens [29, 94, 123].
  p8-l27  [66 511 545 522]  {SFRM1200 12}7. Following Carl Jung, there are several literature studies involving instances of synchronicity
  p8-l28  [81 525 545 536]  {SFRM1200 12}or meaningful coincidences that evoke immediate, ineffable feelings of interconnectedness
  p8-l29  [81 539 304 551]  {SFRM1200 12}for the experiencing subject [41, 46, 72, 101].
  p8-l30  [66 564 545 575]  {SFRM1200 12}8. Instances of transpersonal experiences, such as out-of-body experiences, which involve a
  p8-l31  [81 579 545 590]  {SFRM1200 12}sensation of being detached from one’s physical body and perceiving the world from a
  p8-l32  [81 593 545 604]  {SFRM1200 12}location outside the physical body [31, 32, 90]. These experiences are intimately concerned
  p8-l33  [81 607 171 618]  {SFRM1200 12}with sense of self.
  p8-l34  [66 632 545 643]  {SFRM1200 12}9. Also, near-death experiences such as experiences of vivid and panoramic life reviews, where
  p8-l35  [81 646 545 657]  {SFRM1200 12}subjects relive or review significant events or memories from their lives in a comprehensive
  p8-l36  [81 660 545 672]  {SFRM1200 12}manner (these are sometimes reported with feelings of peace or transcendence while seeming
  p8-l37  [81 675 413 687]  {SFRM1200 12}to enter into a different realm during the experience) [30, 31, 130]
  p8-l38  [61 700 545 711]  {SFRM1200 12}10. Lastly, experiences of being in a state of flow, where immediate, ineffable insights and
  p8-l39  [81 714 545 725]  {SFRM1200 12}intense focus arise during engaging activities like creating art, playing music or solving
  p8-l40  [81 728 237 740]  {SFRM1200 12}complex problems [70, 98, 133].
  p8-l41  [296 759 302 770]  {SFRM1200 12}8
