  p9-l1  [89 75 545 87]  {SFRM1200 12}The above are all examples of conscious experiences (although not of a typically sensory
  p9-l2  [52 89 545 101]  {SFRM1200 12}type) that constitute some form of personal meaning for the experiencing subject. We claim
  p9-l3  [52 104 545 115]  {SFRM1200 12}that the phenomenal content of these experiences is of a semantic type, that is, it is subjective
  p9-l4  [52 119 98 130]  {SFRM1200 12}meaning.
  p9-l5  [52 166 61 181]  {SFBX1728 17.2}3
  p9-l6  [80 165 414 181]  {SFBX1728 17.2}Do Raw Feels Have Subjective Meaning?
  p9-l7  [52 206 545 217]  {SFRM1200 12}The experiential phenomena enumerated in the section above, while predominantly of a non-
  p9-l8  [52 220 545 232]  {SFRM1200 12}sensory nature, have nonetheless been extensively studied in the consciousness literature (see
  p9-l9  [52 234 545 246]  {SFRM1200 12}references cited above). The important point of the above discussion was that a personal notion
  p9-l10  [52 248 545 260]  {SFRM1200 12}of meaning is ubiquitous to a wide variety of conscious experiences (particularly, those referring
  p9-l11  [52 263 545 275]  {SFRM1200 12}to non-sensory modalities). What about the raw feels of consciousness? Are they associated to
  p9-l12  [52 278 101 289]  {SFRM1200 12}meaning?
  p9-l13  [89 302 545 314]  {SFRM1200 12}Expositions concerning sensory qualia are often discussed in terms of raw feels [127]. In
  p9-l14  [52 317 545 328]  {SFRM1200 12}order to make the case that raw feels too, cannot be devoid of meaning that is intrinsic to the
  p9-l15  [52 331 545 342]  {SFRM1200 12}experiencing subject, let us consider a bridging concept between feels and meaning: namely, that
  p9-l16  [52 346 545 357]  {SFRM1200 12}of "sense". In philosophy of mind, the term sense has multiple connotations: it may refer to a
  p9-l17  [52 360 545 372]  {SFRM1200 12}subjective perception, an understanding or significance of something, or a form of knowledge [25,
  p9-l18  [52 374 545 386]  {SFRM1200 12}27,52]. Sense, perfectly well, applies also to experiences beyond those involving the purely sensory
  p9-l19  [52 389 545 401]  {SFRM1200 12}modalities (for instance, those involving internal states). Here we will anchor our discussion
  p9-l20  [52 403 301 415]  {SFRM1200 12}mainly on Frege’s use of the notion of sense [52].
  p9-l21  [52 428 545 439]  {SFBX1200 12}Remark 3.1.{SFTI1200 12 i} In his seminal 1892 paper, "On Sense and Reference", Gottlob Frege developed
  p9-l22  [52 442 545 454]  {SFTI1200 12 i}his ideas on sense and reference [52]. This laid the groundwork for the metaphysics of thought,
  p9-l23  [52 456 545 468]  {SFTI1200 12 i}theories of meaning in philosophy of mind, as well as discussions on semantics and language{SFRM0800 8} 5{SFTI1200 12 i} .
  p9-l24  [52 472 545 482]  {SFTI1200 12 i}Frege elaborates on the distinction between sense and reference, and suggests that both, sense and
  p9-l25  [52 485 492 497]  {SFTI1200 12 i}reference contribute to meaning (with respect to both, expressions as well as thoughts).
  p9-l26  [89 509 546 522]  {SFTI1200 12 i}According to Frege, reference{SFRM0800 8}6{SFTI1200 12 i} is concerned with naming objects or entities. It refers to, or
  p9-l27  [52 525 545 536]  {SFTI1200 12 i}is about, something. Sense, on the other hand, characterizes the mode in which something presents
  p9-l28  [52 539 544 551]  {SFTI1200 12 i}itself. In terms of expressions, this mode accounts for what the expression signifies (to the subject).
  p9-l29  [52 554 545 565]  {SFTI1200 12 i}It is the way by which one understands the expression via internal conceptual relations. Given the
  p9-l30  [52 568 545 580]  {SFTI1200 12 i}reference of words (as names that refer to something), the reference of a sentence as a whole can
  p9-l31  [52 583 545 594]  {SFTI1200 12 i}be described in terms of the references of the individual words and the way in which those words are
  p9-l32  [52 597 545 608]  {SFTI1200 12 i}arranged in the sentence. Based on this Frege identifies the reference of a sentence as a truth value
  p9-l33  [52 611 545 623]  {SFTI1200 12 i}(see also [48,135] for an interpretation of Frege to the philosophy of language). Correspondingly,
  p9-l34  [52 626 545 638]  {SFTI1200 12 i}the sense of a sentence is a "thought" ( [53] discusses the metaphysics of thought, as well as
  p9-l35  [52 640 545 652]  {SFTI1200 12 i}its linguistic realization). While the reference of a sentence can assume one of two truth values,
  p9-l36  [52 655 545 666]  {SFTI1200 12 i}the sense of a sentence can be acquired from a potentially infinite possibility of thoughts. Thus,
  p9-l37  [52 670 545 680]  {SFTI1200 12 i}sense and reference are both, compositional and hierarchical, existing at each level of the structure
  p9-l38  [66 691 545 702]  {SFRM0700 7}5{SFRM1000 10}Following Frege’s seminal essay, other works that have extensively addressed the relation between language,
  p9-l39  [52 704 545 713]  {SFRM1000 10}meaning and mind include those of Bertrand Russell, Ludwig Wittgenstein, Hilary Putnam and Saul Kripke, to
  p9-l40  [52 716 175 726]  {SFRM1000 10}name a few [75, 81, 109, 114].
  p9-l41  [66 727 361 738]  {SFRM0700 7}6{SFRM1000 10}Some authors also use the term "denotation" for "reference" [135].
  p9-l42  [296 759 302 770]  {SFRM1200 12}9
  == page 10  595 x 842  lines 41  pictures 0  drawings 5
  p10-l1  [52 75 545 87]  {SFTI1200 12 i}of the expression. Two syntactically distinct expressions, may have identical references (at each
  p10-l2  [52 89 353 101]  {SFTI1200 12 i}corresponding level of their structures), but distinct senses.
  p10-l3  [89 125 545 136]  {SFRM1200 12}The relevant question then is: Do raw feels have a subjective "sense" for that what is
  p10-l4  [52 139 545 150]  {SFRM1200 12}being experienced? Is this a form of meaning, after all? Let us now answer these questions. In
  p10-l5  [52 153 335 164]  {SFRM1200 12}doing so, we will make use of the following proposition:
  p10-l6  [56 189 548 200]  {SFBX1200 12}Proposition 3.1.{SFTI1200 12 i} Mental representations are to the mind, what symbolic expressions are to lan-
  p10-l7  [56 203 87 214]  {SFTI1200 12 i}guage.
  p10-l8  [89 252 545 263]  {SFRM1200 12}Building up on remark 3.1 above, let us analyze Frege’s notion of sense in the context of
  p10-l9  [52 266 545 277]  {SFRM1200 12}mind. In Frege’s view, the "sense of an expression" is a semantic attribute based on personal
  p10-l10  [52 281 545 291]  {SFRM1200 12}significance to the subject. This sense conveys a meaning of the expression to the subject. In
  p10-l11  [52 295 545 306]  {SFRM1200 12}this context, Frege even described thought as a semantic construct. In order to systematically
  p10-l12  [52 309 545 320]  {SFRM1200 12}lift this notion from the philosophy of language to the philosophy of mind; first, notice that just
  p10-l13  [52 324 545 335]  {SFRM1200 12}as an expression is a syntactic structure based on rules of composition and an architectural hi-
  p10-l14  [52 338 545 350]  {SFRM1200 12}erarchy; so is a mental representation of objects in the world (or of internal states), a syntactic
  p10-l15  [52 351 545 364]  {SFRM1200 12}construct based on rules of composition and structural hierarchy{SFRM0800 8}7{SFRM1200 12} (in the following section, we
  p10-l16  [52 367 545 379]  {SFRM1200 12}elaborate further on how grammar extends beyond spoken language; see also [96, 139]). Hence,
  p10-l17  [52 382 545 393]  {SFRM1200 12}our proposition above: that mental representations are to the mind, what symbolic expressions
  p10-l18  [52 396 545 407]  {SFRM1200 12}are to language. By themselves, they are both syntactic constructs based on admissible structural
  p10-l19  [52 411 545 421]  {SFRM1200 12}compositions of linguistic and mental primitives respectively. Extrapolating Frege’s philosophy
  p10-l20  [52 425 545 436]  {SFRM1200 12}to all syntactic constructs, mental representations also have a reference and a sense. Their ref-
  p10-l21  [52 439 545 450]  {SFRM1200 12}erence is literally what they are about, or what they represent; whereas, the sense of a mental
  p10-l22  [52 454 545 465]  {SFRM1200 12}representation is simply its significance to the subject. The latter is thus a semantic attribute of
  p10-l23  [52 468 545 480]  {SFRM1200 12}the representation. Mental representations thus have a structural (syntactic) aspect, as well as a
  p10-l24  [52 483 545 494]  {SFRM1200 12}semantic aspect. In the phenomenology of consciousness, when one speaks about awareness, or
  p10-l25  [52 496 545 508]  {SFRM1200 12}being aware about "something", it is certainly not the "thing" itself{SFRM0800 8}8{SFRM1200 12} that the subject is, or even
  p10-l26  [52 510 545 523]  {SFRM1200 12}possibly could be aware of{SFRM0800 8}9{SFRM1200 12} . Furthermore, the fact that qualia are transparent, implies that one
  p10-l27  [52 526 545 537]  {SFRM1200 12}does not experience their mental representations as representations either. Rather, it is only cer-
  p10-l28  [52 540 545 552]  {SFRM1200 12}tain attributes of one’s mental representation (of the "thing" being "observed") that the subject
  p10-l29  [52 555 527 566]  {SFRM1200 12}gains awareness of. And, a prominent attribute of a representation is its subjective meaning.
  p10-l30  [52 584 545 595]  {SFSS1200 12}Based on the above, we surmise that it is subjective meaning of one’s mental representation of the
  p10-l31  [52 598 545 610]  {SFSS1200 12}"thing" being observed (either external or internal) that the subject is aware of during a conscious
  p10-l32  [52 612 545 624]  {SFSS1200 12}experience. The raw feel of a conscious experience, therefore, has a sense to it, coming from the
  p10-l33  [52 627 327 638]  {SFSS1200 12}mental representation of that what is being experienced.
  p10-l34  [66 649 545 659]  {SFRM0700 7}7{SFRM1000 10}Psychophysical studies involving preverbal human infants have demonstrated compelling evidence of the use
  p10-l35  [52 662 545 671]  {SFRM1000 10}of logical reasoning and probabilistic inference in the way humans learn and construct mental representations of
  p10-l36  [52 674 545 684]  {SFRM1000 10}objects in the world [36, 121]. These early representations are constructed using logical primitives and rules of
  p10-l37  [52 686 131 696]  {SFRM1000 10}composition [106].
  p10-l38  [66 697 545 707]  {SFRM0700 7}8{SFRM1000 10}See [47] for discussions on mathematical foundations of "what is a thing?"; and [134] for a discussion on the
  p10-l39  [52 710 250 719]  {SFRM1000 10}metaphysics of abstract and concrete objects.
  p10-l40  [66 721 483 731]  {SFRM0700 7}9{SFRM1000 10}Several philosophers including Husserl and Merleau-Ponty have emphasized this point [68, 88] .
  p10-l41  [293 759 305 770]  {SFRM1200 12}10
  == page 11  595 x 842  lines 42  pictures 0  drawings 0
  p11-l1  [52 75 210 86]  {SFRM1200 12}Two remarks are in order here:
  p11-l2  [52 100 545 111]  {SFBX1200 12}Remark 3.2.{SFTI1200 12 i} There is of course room to argue that there may be other attributes of mental rep-
  p11-l3  [52 114 545 125]  {SFTI1200 12 i}resentations such as its symbolic, computational or informational features that also partake in the
  p11-l4  [52 129 545 140]  {SFTI1200 12 i}realm of awareness. However, unlike meaning, these other features are not non-representational.
  p11-l5  [52 143 545 154]  {SFTI1200 12 i}Which is why, they do in fact admit a third-person description. However, qualia, having a phe-
  p11-l6  [52 158 545 169]  {SFTI1200 12 i}nomenal character, are deemed non-representational. For that reason, we maintain that subjective
  p11-l7  [52 172 521 183]  {SFTI1200 12 i}meaning is the more compelling candidate that captures the phenomenal character of qualia.
  p11-l8  [52 197 546 207]  {SFBX1200 12}Remark 3.3.{SFTI1200 12 i} Let us also briefly comment on empirical studies that also invoke the notion of
  p11-l9  [52 211 545 222]  {SFTI1200 12 i}sense in consciousness research. In empirical and clinical literature on consciousness, one often
  p11-l10  [52 225 545 236]  {SFTI1200 12 i}encounters sense via terms such as, "sense of agency", "sense of self", "sense of ownership",
  p11-l11  [52 239 545 251]  {SFTI1200 12 i}"sense of time", and so on [42,67,80,84,132]. In all of these contexts, sense is often described as
  p11-l12  [52 254 545 265]  {SFTI1200 12 i}a subjective perception or comprehension of something. Notice that such a description is precisely a
  p11-l13  [52 269 545 280]  {SFTI1200 12 i}semantic attribute based on personal significance to the experiencing subject. The same can be said
  p11-l14  [52 283 545 294]  {SFTI1200 12 i}for other examples of sense such as an intense sensation of pain, an abrupt sense of discomfort,
  p11-l15  [52 298 545 309]  {SFTI1200 12 i}or a sudden surge of pleasure. In fact, sense and feeling are sometimes used interchangeably in
  p11-l16  [52 312 124 323]  {SFTI1200 12 i}this literature.
  p11-l17  [52 359 61 374]  {SFBX1728 17.2}4
  p11-l18  [80 359 355 374]  {SFBX1728 17.2}The Formal Structure of Meaning
  p11-l19  [52 399 545 410]  {SFRM1200 12}So far, we have put forth, mostly on phenomenological grounds, that meaning, generated intrin-
  p11-l20  [52 413 545 424]  {SFRM1200 12}sically by the experiencing self, is an integral and inseparable characteristic of qualia, across the
  p11-l21  [52 428 545 439]  {SFRM1200 12}board. Furthermore, the subjectivity, non-representational and as well, the transparency of, what
  p11-l22  [52 442 545 453]  {SFRM1200 12}has been referred to as subjective meaning, suggests why it may be directly linked to the phenom-
  p11-l23  [52 457 545 468]  {SFRM1200 12}enal contents of conscious experience. However, to simply label meaning as a mental property,
  p11-l24  [52 471 545 482]  {SFRM1200 12}does not do full justice to the important role that meaning plays with regard to the mind-matter
  p11-l25  [52 485 545 497]  {SFRM1200 12}relation. This view has also been proposed in [23, 24], albeit, based on different motivations. To
  p11-l26  [52 500 545 511]  {SFRM1200 12}elucidate the character of meaning with respect to the nature of qualia, and also its relevance to
  p11-l27  [52 514 503 525]  {SFRM1200 12}the mind-matter relation, we first need to discuss the formal structure of meaning itself.
  p11-l28  [89 539 545 550]  {SFRM1200 12}Of course, the concept of meaning is not exclusive to the philosophy of mind. Rather,
  p11-l29  [52 554 545 565]  {SFRM1200 12}it has a shared history across disciplines, including linguistics, semiotics, programming language
  p11-l30  [52 568 545 579]  {SFRM1200 12}theory, type theory, category theory and metamathematics, to name a few. Based on the specific
  p11-l31  [52 582 545 593]  {SFRM1200 12}domain, one considers or constructs appropriate semantic frameworks for given syntactic systems
  p11-l32  [52 596 545 608]  {SFRM1200 12}(both, verbal or non-verbal). A semantic framework provides an interpretation space for syntactic
  p11-l33  [52 611 545 622]  {SFRM1200 12}expressions. For instance, a symbolic expression such as a sentence or a logical proof, by itself,
  p11-l34  [52 626 545 637]  {SFRM1200 12}is a purely syntactic construct. It is its semantics that elucidates what the sentence or proof
  p11-l35  [52 640 545 651]  {SFRM1200 12}refers to or how it can be interpreted. For this reason, one works with a formal interpretation
  p11-l36  [52 655 545 666]  {SFRM1200 12}space within which syntactic expressions may be evaluated. These evaluations may either yield
  p11-l37  [52 669 545 681]  {SFRM1200 12}truth values or, more generally, meaning vectors (see distributional models of meaning [40]).
  p11-l38  [52 684 545 695]  {SFRM1200 12}Semantic models thus enable value or categorical evaluations of the syntax. However, what is
  p11-l39  [52 698 545 709]  {SFRM1200 12}important are not the specific categories or values that a syntactic structure may acquire within
  p11-l40  [52 712 545 724]  {SFRM1200 12}a model (which often depend on model constraints); rather, it is that these evaluations establish
  p11-l41  [52 727 545 738]  {SFRM1200 12}relations between syntactic expressions. For example, relations of similarity. It is such relations
  p11-l42  [293 759 305 770]  {SFRM1200 12}11
  == page 12  595 x 842  lines 44  pictures 0  drawings 1
  p12-l1  [52 75 545 86]  {SFRM1200 12}that are hypothesized to give words and sentences their meanings, which is how the so-called
  p12-l2  [52 89 374 101]  {SFRM1200 12}"compositional distributional models of meaning" operate [38].
  p12-l3  [89 115 545 126]  {SFRM1200 12}Let us consider the case of natural language semantics. Here, one may want to study
  p12-l4  [52 129 545 140]  {SFRM1200 12}lexical semantics which refers to word meanings, and how meanings between similar words might
  p12-l5  [52 143 545 154]  {SFRM1200 12}relate. In that case, an often used dictum due to J. R. Firth goes as, "You shall know a word
  p12-l6  [52 158 545 169]  {SFRM1200 12}by the company it keeps" [50]. That is, words with similar linguistic distributional properties
  p12-l7  [52 172 545 184]  {SFRM1200 12}(in large corpus data) have similar meanings. This is known as the distributional hypothesis of
  p12-l8  [52 186 545 198]  {SFRM1200 12}meaning [62]. Additionally, besides word meanings, one may also want to investigate meanings
  p12-l9  [52 201 545 213]  {SFRM1200 12}of phrases and sentences (given the meanings of the words contained therein). In this case, one
  p12-l10  [52 216 545 227]  {SFRM1200 12}resorts to the "principle of semantic compositionality", which is also known as Frege’s principle
  p12-l11  [52 230 545 242]  {SFRM1200 12}[104]. According to this, the meaning of a complex expression is derived from the meanings of its
  p12-l12  [52 243 545 256]  {SFRM1200 12}constituent expressions and the syntactic rules used to combine them{SFRM0800 8}10{SFRM1200 12}. Hence, the meaning of a
  p12-l13  [52 259 545 270]  {SFRM1200 12}sentence is then obtained by putting together these two principles, which yields what is known as
  p12-l14  [52 273 545 285]  {SFRM1200 12}the "Compositional Distributional Semantics" framework [40]. The mathematical formalization of
  p12-l15  [52 288 545 299]  {SFRM1200 12}this framework in terms of monoidal categories of vector spaces and Lambek’s pregroup grammar
  p12-l16  [52 302 545 313]  {SFRM1200 12}enables one to compute the meaning of well-typed sentences from the meaning of its constituent
  p12-l17  [52 317 545 328]  {SFRM1200 12}words, by inducing the type reduction mechanisms of the pregroup grammar to the whole category.
  p12-l18  [52 331 545 343]  {SFRM1200 12}These sentence meanings live in a single space, realized via monoidal (tensor) products of the
  p12-l19  [52 346 545 357]  {SFRM1200 12}category. Meaning is thus evaluated via a category-theoretic functor from a syntactic space
  p12-l20  [52 360 545 371]  {SFRM1200 12}governed by rules of grammar, to a semantic space, functioning as an interpretation space for
  p12-l21  [52 375 157 386]  {SFRM1200 12}syntactic constructs.
  p12-l22  [89 399 545 411]  {SFRM1200 12}Interestingly, such a functorial perspective (as elucidated above) turns out to be much
  p12-l23  [52 414 545 425]  {SFRM1200 12}more general than its particular instantiation in linguistics alone. In fact, in mathematical logic
  p12-l24  [52 428 545 440]  {SFRM1200 12}and abstract algebra this goes by the name of "Functorial Semantics" [78]. For example, an
  p12-l25  [52 443 448 454]  {SFRM1200 12}association map from a given syntactic system to a semantic system, such as:
  p12-l26  [130 473 447 485]  {CMBX12 12 b}Association Map{CMR12 12} :{CMBX12 12 b} Syntactic Space{CMSY10 12 i} →{CMBX12 12 b} Semantic Space
  p12-l27  [530 473 545 485]  {SFRM1200 12}(1)
  p12-l28  [52 509 545 520]  {SFRM1200 12}may realize how abstract algebraic systems yield a geometric representation within an appropriate
  p12-l29  [52 523 545 535]  {SFRM1200 12}space (for instance, the representation theory of groups). In this case, the "intuition" or physical
  p12-l30  [52 538 344 549]  {SFRM1200 12}interpretation of the algebraic system is a geometric one.
  p12-l31  [52 561 545 573]  {SFBX1200 12}Remark 4.1.{SFTI1200 12 i} It is interesting to see how eq.(1) compares to Charles S. Peirce’s Theory of Signs,
  p12-l32  [52 575 545 587]  {SFTI1200 12 i}or Semiotics [102, 103]. Semiotics refers to a formal theory of signification of entities (across
  p12-l33  [52 590 545 602]  {SFTI1200 12 i}science and philosophy), their representation, in terms of suitable signs, and the meaning of those
  p12-l34  [52 604 545 616]  {SFTI1200 12 i}signs [22]. The essential elements of Peirce’s theory include a triad consisting of a sign{CMMI12 12 i} S{SFTI1200 12 i}, the
  p12-l35  [52 619 545 630]  {SFTI1200 12 i}object{CMMI12 12 i} O{SFTI1200 12 i}, and the interpretant{CMMI12 12 i} I{SFTI1200 12 i}. The sign can be thought of as a symbol or a signifier of the thing
  p12-l36  [52 633 545 645]  {SFTI1200 12 i}(the object) being signified. Whereas, the interpretant is the understanding (sometimes described
  p12-l37  [52 648 479 660]  {SFTI1200 12 i}as a "translation") that the user of the sign has of the object-sign relation [22, 117].
  p12-l38  [62 670 545 680]  {SFRM0700 7}10{SFRM1000 10}Of course, compositionality is not the only guiding principle for semantics of complex expressions or conceptual
  p12-l39  [52 683 545 692]  {SFRM1000 10}combinations. One also has to take into account contributions from non-compositional semantics, as illustrated
  p12-l40  [52 695 545 705]  {SFRM1000 10}in [34]. These authors emphasize that the latter is markedly distinct from the former in terms of contextual
  p12-l41  [52 707 545 716]  {SFRM1000 10}dependence. This additional contribution is necessary for a more comprehensive treatment of natural language
  p12-l42  [52 719 545 728]  {SFRM1000 10}semantics and pragmatics. However, for our purposes here, compositional models will suffice for what follows.
  p12-l43  [52 731 327 740]  {SFRM1000 10}Additional contextual contributions can be included thereafter.
  p12-l44  [293 759 305 770]  {SFRM1200 12}12
  == page 13  595 x 842  lines 37  pictures 0  drawings 8
  p13-l1  [89 76 545 86]  {SFTI1200 12 i}The object determines the sign, and in turn, the sign, via the specific representation of the
  p13-l2  [52 90 463 101]  {SFTI1200 12 i}object, influences and shapes the interpretant. The order of these relations is so:
  p13-l3  [269 112 329 123]  {CMMI12 12 i}O{CMSY10 12 i} →{CMMI12 12 i} S{CMSY10 12 i} →{CMMI12 12 i} I
  p13-l4  [52 134 545 145]  {SFTI1200 12 i}One can think of the relation{CMMI12 12 i} O{CMSY10 12 i} →{CMMI12 12 i} S{SFTI1200 12 i} as a{CMMI12 12 i} Representation{SFTI1200 12 i} of the object, while the relation{CMMI12 12 i} S{CMSY10 12 i} →{CMMI12 12 i} I
  p13-l5  [52 148 546 160]  {SFTI1200 12 i}can be viewed as the{CMMI12 12 i} Interpretation{SFTI1200 12 i} of the sign (or an{CMMI12 12 i} Association{SFTI1200 12 i} that maps signs to what they
  p13-l6  [52 163 546 175]  {SFTI1200 12 i}refer to). The meaning of the sign, then manifests via the interpretation of the representation of
  p13-l7  [52 178 179 189]  {SFTI1200 12 i}the object being signified.
  p13-l8  [89 202 545 214]  {SFTI1200 12 i}The comparison to eq.(1) is now straightforward: the sign{CMMI12 12 i} S{SFTI1200 12 i} belongs to the syntactic do-
  p13-l9  [52 216 545 228]  {SFTI1200 12 i}main, whereas the interpretant{CMMI12 12 i} I{SFTI1200 12 i} is semantic. The interpretation map{CMMI12 12 i} S{CMSY10 12 i} →{CMMI12 12 i} I{SFTI1200 12 i}, then corresponds
  p13-l10  [52 231 218 243]  {SFTI1200 12 i}to the association map in eq.(1).
  p13-l11  [89 262 545 273]  {SFRM1200 12}Now, let us take the above formal perspective of meaning, as a map from syntax to
  p13-l12  [52 276 545 288]  {SFRM1200 12}semantics (eq.(1)), more seriously. How does one apply this to the mind-matter problem? To do
  p13-l13  [52 291 508 302]  {SFRM1200 12}so, we propose the following maps of relations between the physical and mental domains:
  p13-l14  [180 336 205 347]  {CMMI12 12 i}Obj{CMMI8 8 i}P
  p13-l15  [394 336 418 347]  {CMMI12 12 i}Rel{CMMI8 8 i}P
