  p14-l3  [89 115 305 126]  {SFRM1200 12}Furthermore, the two representation maps
  p14-l4  [160 141 267 153]  {CMSY10 12 i}R{CMMI8 8 i}Obj{CMR12 12} :{CMMI12 12 i} Obj{CMMI8 8 i}P{CMSY10 12 i} →{CMMI12 12 i} Obj{CMMI8 8 i}M
  p14-l5  [291 141 310 152]  {SFRM1200 12}and
  p14-l6  [333 141 437 153]  {CMSY10 12 i}R{CMMI8 8 i}Rel{CMR12 12} :{CMMI12 12 i} Rel{CMMI8 8 i}P{CMSY10 12 i} →{CMMI12 12 i} Rel{CMMI8 8 i}M
  p14-l7  [530 141 545 153]  {SFRM1200 12}(3)
  p14-l8  [52 167 545 178]  {SFRM1200 12}take objects and relations in the physical domain and construct models of these objects and
  p14-l9  [52 182 545 193]  {SFRM1200 12}relations respectively within the mental domain. At times, one interchangeably uses the term
  p14-l10  [52 195 545 207]  {SFRM1200 12}"mental representation" to refer to these models, rather than the maps themselves{SFRM0800 8}11{SFRM1200 12}. While
  p14-l11  [52 210 545 222]  {SFRM1200 12}mental models may well supervene upon the brain’s neuronal circuitry (and feedback loops with
  p14-l12  [52 225 545 237]  {SFRM1200 12}the environment, through the body), strictly speaking, these models are directly accessible only
  p14-l13  [52 239 545 251]  {SFRM1200 12}to the cognitive / conscious agent. For that reason, these models lie solely within the mental
  p14-l14  [52 253 545 266]  {SFRM1200 12}domain{SFRM0800 8}12{SFRM1200 12}, while the representations{CMSY10 12 i} R{CMMI8 8 i}Obj{SFRM1200 12} and{CMSY10 12 i} R{CMMI8 8 i}Rel{SFRM1200 12} themselves, are maps from the physical to the
  p14-l15  [52 269 132 280]  {SFRM1200 12}mental domain.
  p14-l16  [89 293 545 305]  {SFRM1200 12}Now, how does one realize meaning (in the sense of eq.(1)) within the framework of eq.(2)?
  p14-l17  [52 308 545 320]  {SFRM1200 12}In order to answer this, first let us note that the spaces of relations{CMMI12 12 i} Rel{CMMI8 8 i}P{SFRM1200 12} and{CMMI12 12 i} Rel{CMMI8 8 i}M{SFRM1200 12} above, precisely
  p14-l18  [52 322 545 333]  {SFRM1200 12}function as semantic spaces. In linguistics, and more specifically in natural language semantics,
  p14-l19  [52 336 545 348]  {SFRM1200 12}words (and sentences) that are deemed to carry similar meanings, are hypothesized to occur in
  p14-l20  [52 351 545 363]  {SFRM1200 12}similar linguistic distributions [62]. Semantic spaces are then constructed as vector spaces (or more
  p14-l21  [52 365 545 377]  {SFRM1200 12}generally, as metric spaces) endowed with a similarity metric, such that words or sentences with
  p14-l22  [52 380 545 391]  {SFRM1200 12}similar linguistic distributions are clustered together, and thus exhibit similar meanings. In other
  p14-l23  [52 394 545 405]  {SFRM1200 12}words, the meaning of a syntactic construct is a relational attribute that depends on how that
  p14-l24  [52 408 545 420]  {SFRM1200 12}construct is related (with respect to the similarity metric) to other constructs within that space.
  p14-l25  [52 423 545 434]  {SFRM1200 12}Meanings of concepts can be extracted from its network of relations with other concepts. Examples
  p14-l26  [52 437 545 449]  {SFRM1200 12}of such models include word embedding models in computational linguistics [92], and Gardenfors’
  p14-l27  [52 452 545 464]  {SFRM1200 12}"conceptual spaces" in cognitive science [59]. Furthermore, there is compelling evidence from
  p14-l28  [52 467 545 478]  {SFRM1200 12}neurophysiological studies demonstrating how semantic maps are encoded in the human brain as
  p14-l29  [52 481 545 493]  {SFRM1200 12}a network of (encoded) concepts, via similarity distance in encoding space [69, 100, 108]. Despite
  p14-l30  [52 496 545 506]  {SFRM1200 12}different choices in implementation, all the examples above suggest that relational maps between
  p14-l31  [52 510 339 521]  {SFRM1200 12}concepts provide the building blocks of semantic spaces.
  p14-l32  [89 535 545 546]  {SFRM1200 12}What about the space of syntax? Where does that appear within our framework? Syn-
  p14-l33  [52 549 545 560]  {SFRM1200 12}tactic structures refer to admissible compositions of symbols of a formal language, that generate
  p14-l34  [52 564 545 575]  {SFRM1200 12}the words or sentences of that language. What determines admissibility of symbolic compositions
  p14-l35  [52 578 545 589]  {SFRM1200 12}is the formal grammar specifying the language. In recent years, what one means by language
  p14-l36  [52 592 545 603]  {SFRM1200 12}and grammar has vastly generalized beyond traditional forms of written language. Examples
  p14-l37  [52 606 545 618]  {SFRM1200 12}include graph grammars in graphical languages [5–7, 83], pregroup grammars in diagrammatic
  p14-l38  [52 621 545 633]  {SFRM1200 12}languages involving string diagrams [40, 77], visual grammars corresponding to compositions in-
  p14-l39  [52 635 545 647]  {SFRM1200 12}volving visual scenes and actions [96,139], and hypergraph / operator algebras that extend graph
  p14-l40  [52 650 545 662]  {SFRM1200 12}grammars [4, 136, 137], just to name a few. In our case here, the collections of objects in both,
  p14-l41  [52 665 545 677]  {CMMI12 12 i}Obj{CMMI8 8 i}P{SFRM1200 12} and{CMMI12 12 i} Obj{CMMI8 8 i}M{SFRM1200 12} are not arbitrary collections. How objects interact and compose in the physical
  p14-l42  [52 679 545 690]  {SFRM1200 12}world, and the ensuing physical properties of these compositions, is highly constrained by the laws
  p14-l43  [62 701 545 711]  {SFRM0700 7}11{SFRM1000 10}More generally, mental representations may also encompass models of internal states, such as one’s self model
  p14-l44  [52 714 169 724]  {SFRM1000 10}(see [107] for an overview).
  p14-l45  [62 725 507 735]  {SFRM0700 7}12{SFRM1000 10}The neuroscientist experimenting upon the agent, is merely inferring what the model may be about.
  p14-l46  [293 759 305 770]  {SFRM1200 12}14
  == page 15  595 x 842  lines 45  pictures 0  drawings 1
  p15-l1  [52 75 545 86]  {SFRM1200 12}of physics; including laws of dynamical evolution, order effects such as causal ordering, kinemat-
  p15-l2  [52 90 545 101]  {SFRM1200 12}ical constraints such as conservation laws, and geometrical constraints due to the spaces within
  p15-l3  [52 104 545 115]  {SFRM1200 12}which these objects live. Furthermore, hierarchical structures of physical systems have their own
  p15-l4  [52 119 545 130]  {SFRM1200 12}macro-level laws in the form of statistical mechanics and thermodynamics. Consequently, rep-
  p15-l5  [52 133 545 145]  {SFRM1200 12}resentations of the physical world in{CMMI12 12 i} Obj{CMMI8 8 i}M{SFRM1200 12} are also modeled accordingly. The visual grammar
  p15-l6  [52 147 545 159]  {SFRM1200 12}discussed in [139], the "physics engine" in [76], and the "impossible figures" in [82], all lend strong
  p15-l7  [52 161 545 173]  {SFRM1200 12}evidence towards a grammar underlying mental representations{SFRM0800 8}13{SFRM1200 12}. In other words, structures that
  p15-l8  [52 177 545 188]  {SFRM1200 12}"live" in{CMMI12 12 i} Obj{CMMI8 8 i}P{SFRM1200 12} and{CMMI12 12 i} Obj{CMMI8 8 i}M{SFRM1200 12}, realize syntactically well-defined structures following a broader notion
  p15-l9  [52 191 377 202]  {SFRM1200 12}of grammar, as indicated in the examples and references above.
  p15-l10  [89 215 545 228]  {SFRM1200 12}Therefore, on one hand, we have rules constraining the space{CMMI12 12 i} Obj{CMMI8 8 i}P{SFRM1200 12} (and its representation
  p15-l11  [52 230 545 242]  {SFRM1200 12}in{CMMI12 12 i} Obj{CMMI8 8 i}M{SFRM1200 12}), that allow for only very specific compositions of physical (respectively, mental) objects
  p15-l12  [52 244 545 256]  {SFRM1200 12}corresponding to existing constructs in the world (mind). On the other hand, we have an associ-
  p15-l13  [52 259 545 271]  {SFRM1200 12}ation map{CMSY10 12 i} A{CMMI8 8 i}P{SFRM1200 12} , that defines relations between physically permissible objects, leading to the space
  p15-l14  [52 274 545 285]  {SFRM1200 12}of physical relations{CMMI12 12 i} Rel{CMMI8 8 i}P{SFRM1200 12} , which in turn, functions as a semantic space for the physical domain.
  p15-l15  [52 288 545 300]  {SFRM1200 12}Correspondingly, we have the association map{CMSY10 12 i} A{CMMI8 8 i}M{SFRM1200 12}, defining relations between permissible mental
  p15-l16  [52 302 545 314]  {SFRM1200 12}objects (obtained as representations of physical objects or of internal states of the agent), and
  p15-l17  [52 317 545 329]  {SFRM1200 12}leading to the space of mental relations{CMMI12 12 i} Rel{CMMI8 8 i}M{SFRM1200 12}, which now functions as a semantic space for the
  p15-l18  [52 331 545 343]  {SFRM1200 12}mental domain. As per eq.(1), in the system in eq.(2) we now have two possible levels at which
  p15-l19  [52 346 545 357]  {SFRM1200 12}meaning may manifest as a distributional and compositional attribute of these mappings from
  p15-l20  [52 360 545 371]  {SFRM1200 12}syntactic to semantic spaces: the physical level, as well as the mental level. However, these levels
  p15-l21  [52 375 488 386]  {SFRM1200 12}are not independent, and neither is the kind of meaning associated to these domains.
  p15-l22  [89 399 545 410]  {SFRM1200 12}Our central question is how does subjectively attributed meaning arise in the mind, and
  p15-l23  [52 413 545 426]  {SFRM1200 12}how does that associate to the broader mind-matter relation? Our map{CMSY10 12 i} A{CMMI8 8 i}P{SFRM1200 12} suggests that a
  p15-l24  [52 428 545 440]  {SFRM1200 12}formal notion of meaning also ought to exist within the physical domain (in terms of relations
  p15-l25  [52 442 545 454]  {SFRM1200 12}and interactions between physical structures, constrained by causal and statistical laws). However,
  p15-l26  [52 456 545 468]  {SFRM1200 12}there needs to be something to read-off this meaning associated to the physical world{SFRM0800 8}14{SFRM1200 12}. Now this
  p15-l27  [52 472 545 483]  {SFRM1200 12}is where mental states come into the picture. They serve as a means to read-off associations in
  p15-l28  [52 486 545 498]  {SFRM1200 12}the world. This is how that may be so: through the representation maps{CMSY10 12 i} R{CMMI8 8 i}Obj{SFRM1200 12} and{CMSY10 12 i} R{CMMI8 8 i}Rel{SFRM1200 12}, models
  p15-l29  [52 500 545 511]  {SFRM1200 12}of physical objects and their relations are constructed within the mental domain. Then, following
  p15-l30  [52 515 545 527]  {SFRM1200 12}simply the commutativity of the diagram in eq.(2), the association map{CMSY10 12 i} A{CMMI8 8 i}P{SFRM1200 12} (within the physical
  p15-l31  [52 529 545 541]  {SFRM1200 12}domain) induces the association map{CMSY10 12 i} A{CMMI8 8 i}M{SFRM1200 12} (within the mental domain). Thus, meanings about
  p15-l32  [52 544 545 555]  {SFRM1200 12}things in the physical world can be read-off via mental states, following this relation between the
  p15-l33  [52 558 204 569]  {SFRM1200 12}physical and mental domains.
  p15-l34  [52 582 545 593]  {SFBX1200 12}Remark 4.2.{SFTI1200 12 i} Once again, notice the parallels to Peirce’s object-sign-interpretant triad in semi-
  p15-l35  [52 596 545 608]  {SFTI1200 12 i}otics. The{CMMI12 12 i} O{CMSY10 12 i} →{CMMI12 12 i} S{CMSY10 12 i} →{CMMI12 12 i} I{SFTI1200 12 i} relations in Remark 4.1 are precisely captured by the following sequence
  p15-l36  [52 611 143 623]  {SFTI1200 12 i}of maps in eq.(2):
  p15-l37  [239 625 358 637]  {CMMI12 12 i}Obj{CMMI8 8 i}P{CMSY10 12 i} →{CMMI12 12 i} Obj{CMMI8 8 i}M{CMSY10 12 i} →{CMMI12 12 i} Rel{CMMI8 8 i}M
  p15-l38  [52 646 545 658]  {SFTI1200 12 i}where elements of{CMMI12 12 i} Obj{CMMI8 8 i}M{SFTI1200 12 i} play the role of signs with respect to the mind-matter relation, and{CMMI12 12 i} Rel{CMMI8 8 i}M
  p15-l39  [52 661 545 671]  {SFTI1200 12 i}give rise to Peirce’s interpretant, corresponding to mental objects. Of course, the above is only
  p15-l40  [62 682 545 692]  {SFRM0700 7}13{SFRM1000 10}Needless to say, there have been numerous debates in the literature as to whether grammar, in some form,
  p15-l41  [52 695 221 705]  {SFRM1000 10}may be innate to cognitive agents [60].
  p15-l42  [62 706 545 716]  {SFRM0700 7}14{SFRM1000 10}One may well call such an entity an "Observer". Though this is by no means a sufficient condition to define an
  p15-l43  [52 719 545 728]  {SFRM1000 10}observer. Hence, to read-off any kind of meaning, an observer is imperative. A discussion about observer theory
  p15-l44  [52 731 361 741]  {SFRM1000 10}from the perspective of second-order cybernetics can be found in [112].
  p15-l45  [293 759 305 770]  {SFRM1200 12}15
  == page 16  595 x 842  lines 34  pictures 1  drawings 1
  p16-l1  [52 76 545 86]  {SFTI1200 12 i}a particular application of Peirce’s theory of signs, which otherwise, was intended for broader
  p16-l2  [52 90 215 101]  {SFTI1200 12 i}applications across the sciences.
  p16-l3  [89 121 545 132]  {SFRM1200 12}Returning to our discussion about the meaning of things in the physical world, the astute
  p16-l4  [52 135 545 146]  {SFRM1200 12}reader will immediately notice that this is not the only instantiation of meaning available to one’s
  p16-l5  [52 150 545 161]  {SFRM1200 12}mental faculties. That is indeed so. And this is where internal states, as well as perceptual
  p16-l6  [52 164 352 175]  {SFRM1200 12}limitations of the subject, matter. Let us discuss this now:
  p16-l7  [52 189 545 201]  {SFRM1200 12}(i) First, as has been well documented in the literature, mental representations of objects and
  p16-l8  [52 204 545 215]  {SFRM1200 12}relations in the physical world, need not always be complete or accurate descriptions of those
  p16-l9  [52 218 545 230]  {SFRM1200 12}physical entities [107]. In practice, the cognitive / conscious agent has to build models of both,
  p16-l10  [52 232 545 243]  {SFRM1200 12}the world as well as that of other agents from incomplete social, behavioral and sensorimotor data
  p16-l11  [52 246 545 258]  {SFRM1200 12}(either due to stimulus uncertainty, hidden states of other agents, or the agent’s own limitations
  p16-l12  [52 261 545 273]  {SFRM1200 12}in processing and parsing all that information), about objects and agents in its environment,
  p16-l13  [52 275 284 287]  {SFRM1200 12}obtained in real-time [8–10, 16, 44, 54, 56, 115].
  p16-l14  [52 300 545 312]  {SFRM1200 12}(ii) Second, and more importantly, minds can also model their own internal states. The self-model
  p16-l15  [52 315 545 326]  {SFRM1200 12}being a classic example. And these internal models within the mental domain also influence each
  p16-l16  [52 329 545 340]  {SFRM1200 12}other. Again, the example being how the self-model modulates the way the agent probes its
  p16-l17  [52 344 545 355]  {SFRM1200 12}environment to construct a world model, and how through its self-model, the agent perceives its
  p16-l18  [52 358 545 369]  {SFRM1200 12}situated and embodied self, immersed within the world. What this means is that there is a self-
  p16-l19  [52 373 545 384]  {SFRM1200 12}referential aspect within the design of mental models, which is arguably a peculiar feature of the
  p16-l20  [52 387 545 398]  {SFRM1200 12}mental domain. This self-referential feature, or circular feedback, can be modeled via self-loops
  p16-l21  [52 400 372 413]  {SFRM1200 12}within the mental domain. This gives us the following maps{SFRM0800 8}15{SFRM1200 12}:
  p16-l22  [160 440 267 453]  {CMSY10 12 i}S{CMMI8 8 i}Obj{CMR12 12} :{CMMI12 12 i} Obj{CMMI8 8 i}M{CMSY10 12 i} →{CMMI12 12 i} Obj{CMMI8 8 i}M
  p16-l23  [291 441 310 452]  {SFRM1200 12}and
  p16-l24  [333 440 437 453]  {CMSY10 12 i}S{CMMI8 8 i}Rel{CMR12 12} :{CMMI12 12 i} Rel{CMMI8 8 i}M{CMSY10 12 i} →{CMMI12 12 i} Rel{CMMI8 8 i}M
  p16-l25  [52 470 252 482]  {SFRM1200 12}which extend the diagram in eq.(2) by:
  p16-l26  [530 588 545 600]  {SFRM1200 12}(4)
  p16-l27  [89 637 545 649]  {SFRM1200 12}In other words, in{CMMI12 12 i} Obj{CMMI8 8 i}M{SFRM1200 12} we have additional syntactic rules (compared to those in the
  p16-l28  [52 651 545 663]  {SFRM1200 12}purely physical domain) governing how mental models interact with each other. For instance,
  p16-l29  [52 666 545 678]  {SFRM1200 12}how the self-model (which includes models of the agent’s psychological as well as bodily faculties)
  p16-l30  [52 680 545 691]  {SFRM1200 12}may interact with or influence parameters within the subject’s world-model in order to generate
  p16-l31  [52 694 545 706]  {SFRM1200 12}anticipatory and adaptive control responses to a planned action [12, 65, 66, 91]. Correspondingly,
  p16-l32  [52 709 503 721]  {SFRM1200 12}the semantic space{CMMI12 12 i} Rel{CMMI8 8 i}M{SFRM1200 12} also captures relations within and between all internal models.
  p16-l33  [62 730 493 740]  {SFRM0700 7}15{SFRM1000 10}Note that these are not identity morphisms, but non-trivial mappings within the mental domain.
  p16-l34  [293 759 305 770]  {SFRM1200 12}16
