  p17-l1  [52 75 545 87]  {SFBX1200 12}Remark 4.3.{SFTI1200 12 i} The maps, in eqns.(2) and (4) above, demonstrate how meaning involves relations
  p17-l2  [52 90 545 101]  {SFTI1200 12 i}between the physical and mental domains. The mind-matter relation thus plays a central role in
  p17-l3  [52 104 545 115]  {SFTI1200 12 i}realizing subjective meaning within mental states. How does one realize sense and reference within
  p17-l4  [52 119 134 130]  {SFTI1200 12 i}this framework?
  p17-l5  [89 142 545 155]  {SFTI1200 12 i}The map from{CMMI12 12 i} Obj{CMMI8 8 i}P{SFTI1200 12 i} to{CMMI12 12 i} Rel{CMMI8 8 i}M{SFTI1200 12 i} following the composition{SFRM0800 8}16{CMSY10 12 i} A{CMMI8 8 i}P{CMSY10 12 i} ◦R{CMMI8 8 i}Rel{SFTI1200 12 i} suggests how reference
  p17-l6  [52 158 545 169]  {SFTI1200 12 i}(as a relation between the physical and the mental) about things in the world, as perceived by an
  p17-l7  [52 172 545 183]  {SFTI1200 12 i}agent with mental states, may be formalized:- As a map from structure in the world to relations in
  p17-l8  [52 187 545 198]  {SFTI1200 12 i}the mind, that factors through physical relations actually found in the physical world, and hence
  p17-l9  [52 201 225 213]  {SFTI1200 12 i}relies on the association map{CMSY10 12 i} A{CMMI8 8 i}P{SFTI1200 12 i} .
  p17-l10  [89 226 545 238]  {SFTI1200 12 i}On the other hand, the composition{CMSY10 12 i} R{CMMI8 8 i}Obj{CMSY10 12 i}◦A{CMMI8 8 i}M{SFTI1200 12 i} suggests how sense (as a relation between the
  p17-l11  [52 240 545 252]  {SFTI1200 12 i}physical and the mental), as perceived by the observing agent, may be formalized:- As a map from
  p17-l12  [52 255 545 266]  {SFTI1200 12 i}structure in the world to relations in the mind, that now factors through various mental models
  p17-l13  [52 269 546 281]  {SFTI1200 12 i}that the agent may have, and hence relies on the association map{CMSY10 12 i} A{CMMI8 8 i}M{SFTI1200 12 i}. However, to fully account
  p17-l14  [52 284 545 295]  {SFTI1200 12 i}for sense as this composition of maps, one has to admit a slight generalization of the diagram in
  p17-l15  [52 297 506 310]  {SFTI1200 12 i}eq.(2) such that it is commutative up to higher homotopy (in this case, a 2-morphism){SFRM0800 8}17{SFTI1200 12 i} .
  p17-l16  [89 323 545 334]  {SFTI1200 12 i}As an example, consider a physical object; say, the planet Venus. Its reference map passes
  p17-l17  [52 337 545 349]  {SFTI1200 12 i}through physical relations in the world (such as the planet between Mercury and Earth) which
  p17-l18  [52 352 545 363]  {SFTI1200 12 i}constrain its concept within the mental domain; and that yields its meaning as reference. On
  p17-l19  [52 366 545 378]  {SFTI1200 12 i}the other hand, its sense map passes through mental objects (which are mental models that the
  p17-l20  [52 380 545 392]  {SFTI1200 12 i}mind uses to know about these objects). These mental models are based on how the subject has
  p17-l21  [52 395 545 406]  {SFTI1200 12 i}experienced the planet Venus, either as the morning star, or the evening star. Hence with respect
  p17-l22  [52 410 545 421]  {SFTI1200 12 i}to sense as a relation, there are more than one possible paths that could be followed; which explains
  p17-l23  [52 424 323 435]  {SFTI1200 12 i}why there can be different senses for the same object.
  p17-l24  [52 449 545 459]  {SFBX1200 12}Remark 4.4.{SFTI1200 12 i} Based on the above, sense and reference are both crucial to a subject’s experience.
  p17-l25  [52 463 545 474]  {SFTI1200 12 i}Major dissociations or disruptions between pathways related to sense and those to reference, would
  p17-l26  [52 478 352 488]  {SFTI1200 12 i}arguably be implicated in aberrant psychological conditions.
  p17-l27  [89 502 545 513]  {SFTI1200 12 i}For example, sense without reference would suggest domination of internal biases over
  p17-l28  [52 517 545 528]  {SFTI1200 12 i}external evidence, as in cases of hallucination or delusion. Reference without sense corresponds
  p17-l29  [52 531 545 542]  {SFTI1200 12 i}to a state of being unable to contextualize what is being observed. Therefore, both sense and
  p17-l30  [52 546 386 556]  {SFTI1200 12 i}reference play a vital role in the phenomenology of consciousness.
  p17-l31  [89 570 545 582]  {SFTI1200 12 i}Also, in future technology aiming to achieve Artificial General Intelligence (AGI), we posit
  p17-l32  [52 584 546 596]  {SFTI1200 12 i}that such a corroboration between sense and reference would, at the very least, be necessary (if
  p17-l33  [52 599 127 611]  {SFTI1200 12 i}not sufficient).
  p17-l34  [62 618 545 629]  {SFRM0700 7}16{SFRM1000 10}In mathematical literature such a composition of maps is often written in right-to-left notation, but here we
  p17-l35  [52 632 349 641]  {SFRM1000 10}prefer the left-to-right presentation for the sake of better readibility.
  p17-l36  [62 642 545 653]  {SFRM0700 7}17{SFRM1000 10}We thank Giulio Katis for bringing this point to our attention. A thorough category-theoretic treatment of
  p17-l37  [52 656 466 665]  {SFRM1000 10}this diagram along with admissible higher morphisms will be reported in a forthcoming article.
  p17-l38  [293 759 305 770]  {SFRM1200 12}17
