  p18-l1  [52 72 61 87]  {SFBX1728 17.2}5
  p18-l2  [80 72 165 87]  {SFBX1728 17.2}Discussion
  p18-l3  [52 112 545 123]  {SFRM1200 12}This work posits that subjectively attributed meaning is ubiquitous in conscious experiences.
  p18-l4  [52 126 545 138]  {SFRM1200 12}Our hypothesis is that subjective meaning (or, one may also refer to it as intrinsic meaning)
  p18-l5  [52 141 545 152]  {SFRM1200 12}is intrinsic to the qualia of conscious experience, in that, the phenomenal content of conscious
  p18-l6  [52 155 545 166]  {SFRM1200 12}experience is precisely this type of meaning. In other words, we claim that the content of conscious
  p18-l7  [52 170 545 181]  {SFRM1200 12}experience is semantic. To support this proposal, we have discussed literature examples from the
  p18-l8  [52 184 545 195]  {SFRM1200 12}phenomenology of consciousness, which demonstrate the close connection between meaning and
  p18-l9  [52 199 109 210]  {SFRM1200 12}experience.
  p18-l10  [89 223 545 234]  {SFRM1200 12}Our notion of subjective meaning closely relates to what Frege has referred to as "sense".
  p18-l11  [52 238 545 249]  {SFRM1200 12}Furthermore, we have noted out how subjective meaning also parallels Peirce’s "interpretant" in
  p18-l12  [52 252 545 263]  {SFRM1200 12}semiotics. As a special case of Peirce’s theory of signs, Peirce’s object-sign-interpretant triad is
  p18-l13  [52 266 436 279]  {SFRM1200 12}captured following the sequence of maps{CMMI12 12 i} Obj{CMMI8 8 i}P{CMSY10 12 i} →{CMMI12 12 i} Obj{CMMI8 8 i}M{CMSY10 12 i} →{CMMI12 12 i} Rel{CMMI8 8 i}M{SFRM1200 12}, in eq.(2).
  p18-l14  [89 291 545 302]  {SFRM1200 12}Building upon the above, we have argued that raw feels can not be devoid of subjective
  p18-l15  [52 305 545 317]  {SFRM1200 12}meaning. It is this form of meaning of one’s mental representation (of the stimuli) that the subject
  p18-l16  [52 320 545 331]  {SFRM1200 12}is aware of during a conscious experience. The raw feel of a conscious experience has a sense to
  p18-l17  [52 334 545 346]  {SFRM1200 12}it (à{CMMI12 12 i} la{SFRM1200 12} Frege), one that comes from the mental representation of that what is being experienced.
  p18-l18  [89 360 545 371]  {SFRM1200 12}Furthermore, we have presented a formal structure of maps that elucidates the role of
  p18-l19  [52 374 545 385]  {SFRM1200 12}subjective meaning in the context of the mind-matter relation. Exploiting the mapping between
  p18-l20  [52 388 545 399]  {SFRM1200 12}the physical and mental domains, we show how syntactic and semantic structures are realized
  p18-l21  [52 403 545 414]  {SFRM1200 12}within both, the physical and the mental domain. In this sense, grammar and meaning transcend
  p18-l22  [52 417 545 428]  {SFRM1200 12}conventional written language. Meaning is realized as a relational attribute arising from a map
  p18-l23  [52 432 545 443]  {SFRM1200 12}that interprets syntactic structures of a formal system within an appropriate semantic space.
  p18-l24  [52 446 545 457]  {SFRM1200 12}Based on this, we have provided an explanation of how sense and reference may be formally
  p18-l25  [52 461 262 472]  {SFRM1200 12}realized within the mind-matter relation.
  p18-l26  [89 485 545 496]  {SFRM1200 12}Our notion of subjective meaning is the mental image of Brentano’s intentionality. It is this
  p18-l27  [52 500 545 511]  {SFRM1200 12}subjective facet of meaning that a conscious agent experiences with their mind. For this reason,
  p18-l28  [52 514 545 525]  {SFRM1200 12}we could have also referred to this notion of meaning as intrinsic meaning. The full relational
  p18-l29  [52 528 545 540]  {SFRM1200 12}map of meaning (linking the physical to the mental) is only accessible to a metaphysical observer.
  p18-l30  [52 543 545 554]  {SFRM1200 12}Working from such an intrinsic point of view is of direct relevance to any experience-based theory
  p18-l31  [52 556 146 569]  {SFRM1200 12}of consciousness{SFRM0800 8}18{SFRM1200 12} .
  p18-l32  [89 582 545 593]  {SFRM1200 12}Given that this work places meaning of an intrinsic kind at the heart of the problem of
  p18-l33  [52 597 545 608]  {SFRM1200 12}consciousness, or the qualia of it thereof, it is reasonable to ask whether or not conscious aware-
  p18-l34  [52 611 545 623]  {SFRM1200 12}ness itself is a guise of subjective meaning, as structurally defined in eq.(2) above? Though this
  p18-l35  [52 626 545 637]  {SFRM1200 12}view may appear to be in contrast to other proposals, such as consciousness being integrated
  p18-l36  [52 640 545 652]  {SFRM1200 12}information [125], or that, consciousness being identical to a specific maximally irreducible causal
  p18-l37  [52 654 545 666]  {SFRM1200 12}structure [99], or that, consciousness being a form of computation [43]; it may well be that these
  p18-l38  [52 669 545 680]  {SFRM1200 12}are different pieces of the larger puzzle, and perhaps one may find convergence of some of these
  p18-l39  [52 684 545 694]  {SFRM1200 12}different ideas in future theories of consciousness. For instance, a suggestion of how Integrated
  p18-l40  [62 705 545 716]  {SFRM0700 7}18{SFRM1000 10}Of course, one may certainly choose to consider other points of view, other than the one that is intrinsic to
  p18-l41  [52 719 545 727]  {SFRM1000 10}experience, as considered here. Each such case will have its own metaphysical considerations to take into account,
  p18-l42  [52 730 262 739]  {SFRM1000 10}as well as how that may translate to experience.
  p18-l43  [293 759 305 770]  {SFRM1200 12}18
  == page 19  595 x 842  lines 38  pictures 0  drawings 1
  p19-l1  [52 75 545 86]  {SFRM1200 12}Information Theory itself may be extended to include compositional meaning was already pro-
  p19-l2  [52 89 545 101]  {SFRM1200 12}posed{SFRM0800 8}19{SFRM1200 12} in [2]. However, note that any such synthesis of ideas does not imply that information,
  p19-l3  [52 104 545 115]  {SFRM1200 12}causality, computation or meaning can simply be used interchangeably as a theoretical basis for
  p19-l4  [52 118 545 130]  {SFRM1200 12}consciousness science. Each of these are conceptually distinct (and also differ in the way they
  p19-l5  [52 133 545 145]  {SFRM1200 12}could be used as a basis of a scientific theory). However, different ways of looking at the problem
  p19-l6  [52 147 545 159]  {SFRM1200 12}(of consciousness) may certainly be a very fruitful exercise. What this work attempts to highlight
  p19-l7  [52 162 545 173]  {SFRM1200 12}is that meaning, of the kind that is subjectively attributed, seems to account for the phenomenal
  p19-l8  [52 177 345 188]  {SFRM1200 12}and non-representational aspects of conscious experience.
  p19-l9  [89 201 545 212]  {SFRM1200 12}Taking Frege’s philosophy of language and mind seriously, we surmise that that syntax
  p19-l10  [52 216 545 227]  {SFRM1200 12}and semantics are also relevant to a theory of consciousness. In particular, mental representations
  p19-l11  [52 230 545 241]  {SFRM1200 12}are to the mind, what symbolic expressions are to language. Within such a philosophical framing,
  p19-l12  [52 245 545 256]  {SFRM1200 12}when one speaks about awareness about "something", one may then ask: precisely what attribute
  p19-l13  [52 259 545 270]  {SFRM1200 12}of the "thing" is it, that the subject is actually aware of? The answer, subjective meaning "of"
  p19-l14  [52 274 545 284]  {SFRM1200 12}the subject’s mental representation of the "thing", seems better suited than alternatives such
  p19-l15  [52 288 545 299]  {SFRM1200 12}as information, causality or computation. That is not to say that information, causality or
  p19-l16  [52 302 545 313]  {SFRM1200 12}computation are not useful to study how consciousness may work. In fact, these are absolutely
  p19-l17  [52 316 545 328]  {SFRM1200 12}indispensable for quantifying neuronal processes in brain networks (or for that matter, artificial
  p19-l18  [52 331 545 343]  {SFRM1200 12}neural networks), that may correlate to empirical states of consciousness [1, 11, 13, 14, 17–21,
  p19-l19  [52 345 545 357]  {SFRM1200 12}116]. However, if one wants to explain what may constitute the non-representational content of
  p19-l20  [52 360 545 371]  {SFRM1200 12}phenomenal experience, then, the proposal of subjective meaning seems to offer a promising new
  p19-l21  [52 375 545 386]  {SFRM1200 12}avenue for conceptualizing theories of consciousness. Such a notion of meaning is the attribute
  p19-l22  [52 389 545 401]  {SFRM1200 12}(of the thing observed) that the subject may well be aware of, or may sense (à{CMMI12 12 i} la{SFRM1200 12} Frege) during
  p19-l23  [52 404 545 414]  {SFRM1200 12}conscious experience. This hypothesis, of subjective meaning as the phenomenal constituent of
  p19-l24  [52 418 545 429]  {SFRM1200 12}qualia of conscious experience, also suggests that one take ideas from the philosophy of language
  p19-l25  [52 432 545 443]  {SFRM1200 12}and semiotics more seriously towards the advancement of a comprehensive theoretical framework
  p19-l26  [52 447 419 458]  {SFRM1200 12}that satisfactorily addresses the phenomenal character of consciousness.
  p19-l27  [52 493 197 509]  {SFBX1728 17.2}Acknowledgments
