  p1-l1  [65 54 551 64]  {CMBX12 12 b}Dominance of extrinsic scattering mechanisms in the orbital Hall effect: graphene,
  p1-l2  [115 68 501 78]  {CMBX12 12 b}transition metal dichalcogenides and topological antiferromagnets
  p1-l3  [234 91 382 102]  {CMR10 10}Hong Liu{CMR7 7}1{CMR10 10} and Dimitrie Culcer{CMR7 7}1
  p1-l4  [77 106 539 116]  {CMR6 6}1{CMTI9 9 i}School of Physics and Australian Research Council Centre of Excellence in Low-Energy Electronics Technologies,
  p1-l5  [153 118 464 126]  {CMTI9 9 i}UNSW Node, The University of New South Wales, Sydney 2052, Australia
  p1-l6  [263 128 353 137]  {CMR9 9}(Dated: April 5, 2024)
  p1-l7  [118 145 507 154]  {CMR9 9}The theory of the orbital Hall effect (OHE), a transverse flow of orbital angular momentum
  p1-l8  [109 155 507 164]  {CMR9 9}(OAM) in response to an electric field, has concentrated on intrinsic mechanisms. Here, using a
  p1-l9  [109 166 507 174]  {CMR9 9}quantum kinetic formulation, we determine the full OHE in the presence of short-range disorder
  p1-l10  [109 177 507 185]  {CMR9 9}using 2D massive Dirac fermions as a prototype. We find that, in doped systems, extrinsic effects
  p1-l11  [109 187 507 196]  {CMR9 9}associated with the Fermi surface (skew scattering and side jump) provide{CMSY9 9 i} ≈{CMR9 9} 95% of the OHE. This
  p1-l12  [109 198 482 206]  {CMR9 9}suggests that, at experimentally relevant transport densities, the OHE is primarily extrinsic.
  p1-l13  [64 228 299 237]  {CMTI10 10 i}Introduction{CMR10 10}. The electrical manipulation of magnetic
  p1-l14  [54 240 299 249]  {CMR10 10}degrees of freedom has been continuously investigated
  p1-l15  [54 251 299 260]  {CMR10 10}since Øersted discovered the deflection of a compass nee-
  p1-l16  [54 262 299 272]  {CMR10 10}dle by a current-carrying wire. Modern research has fo-
  p1-l17  [54 274 299 283]  {CMR10 10}cused on the magneto-electric coupling provided by the
  p1-l18  [54 285 299 295]  {CMR10 10}spin-orbit interaction [1–4], while an energetic recent ef-
  p1-l19  [54 297 299 306]  {CMR10 10}fort has been geared towards the electrical operation of
  p1-l20  [54 308 299 317]  {CMR10 10}orbital degrees of freedom in systems without spin-orbit
  p1-l21  [54 319 299 329]  {CMR10 10}coupling [5–12]. This effort centres on the realization
  p1-l22  [54 331 299 340]  {CMR10 10}that Bloch electrons, possess an orbital angular momen-
  p1-l23  [54 342 299 352]  {CMR10 10}tum (OAM) about their centre of mass [13], which is in
  p1-l24  [54 354 299 364]  {CMR10 10}part related to the Berry curvature [14–18]. The OAM
  p1-l25  [54 365 299 375]  {CMR10 10}affects semiclassical quantization [15, 17, 18], contributes
  p1-l26  [54 377 299 387]  {CMR10 10}to the magnetization in certain materials [19–21], affects
  p1-l27  [54 388 299 398]  {CMR10 10}the Zeeman splitting of Dirac materials [22, 23] and con-
  p1-l28  [54 400 299 409]  {CMR10 10}tributes to the non-linear magneto-resistance, valley-Hall
  p1-l29  [54 411 280 421]  {CMR10 10}effect [19, 24, 25], and anomalous Nernst effect [26].
  p1-l30  [64 429 299 439]  {CMR10 10}In a time-reversal symmetric system a finite OAM den-
  p1-l31  [54 441 299 450]  {CMR10 10}sity can be generated by an electric field via the orbital
  p1-l32  [54 452 299 462]  {CMR10 10}Edelstein effect [27–31] or by separating electrons with
  p1-l33  [54 464 299 473]  {CMR10 10}different OAMs on different sides of the sample using an
  p1-l34  [54 475 299 485]  {CMR10 10}electric current. This is the orbital Hall effect (OHE)
  p1-l35  [54 486 299 496]  {CMR10 10}[7, 10, 32–39], which has received considerable attention
  p1-l36  [54 498 299 508]  {CMR10 10}recently [11, 12, 20, 34, 40–43]. The OHE has been pro-
  p1-l37  [54 510 299 519]  {CMR10 10}posed as the mechanism behind the valley Hall effect ob-
  p1-l38  [54 521 299 530]  {CMR10 10}served in graphene and transition metal dichalcogenides
  p1-l39  [54 532 299 542]  {CMR10 10}(TMDs) [33, 44]. Injection of an orbital current into a fer-
  p1-l40  [54 544 299 553]  {CMR10 10}romagnet generates an orbital torque on local magnetic
  p1-l41  [54 555 299 565]  {CMR10 10}moments [11, 12, 20, 34, 40–43], and the OHE may like-
  p1-l42  [54 567 299 576]  {CMR10 10}wise be responsible for the large spin Hall effect observed
  p1-l43  [54 578 299 588]  {CMR10 10}recently [10, 35–39]. Remarkably, all recent theoretical
  p1-l44  [54 590 299 600]  {CMR10 10}work has focused on intrinsic OHE mechanisms [45–49],
  p1-l45  [54 602 299 611]  {CMR10 10}while neglecting extrinsic disorder contributions. This
  p1-l46  [54 613 299 622]  {CMR10 10}absence is puzzling since extrinsic scattering mechanisms
  p1-l47  [54 624 299 633]  {CMR10 10}such as skew scattering and side jump are known to con-
  p1-l48  [54 636 299 645]  {CMR10 10}tribute to the anomalous and spin-Hall effects at the same
  p1-l49  [54 647 299 656]  {CMR10 10}order in the disorder strength as the intrinsic contribu-
  p1-l50  [54 658 299 668]  {CMR10 10}tion [37, 50–57]. It is natural to expect a substantial
  p1-l51  [54 670 299 679]  {CMR10 10}disorder contribution in the OHE. This has enormous
  p1-l52  [54 682 299 691]  {CMR10 10}implications for experiment: a signal may appear to be
  p1-l53  [54 693 299 702]  {CMR10 10}intrinsic, that is, independent of the disorder strength,
  p1-l54  [54 705 299 714]  {CMR10 10}yet our work shows it can still be almost entirely due to
  p1-l55  [54 716 92 725]  {CMR10 10}disorder.
