  p1-l76  [327 474 562 483]  {CMR10 10}In this article, we calculate the full OHE, including in-
  p1-l77  [317 486 562 495]  {CMR10 10}trinsic and extrinsic contributions, for two-dimensional
  p1-l78  [317 497 562 507]  {CMR10 10}(2D) massive Dirac fermions, using graphene, TMDs,
  p1-l79  [317 509 562 518]  {CMR10 10}and topological antiferromagnets as prototype systems.
  p1-l80  [317 520 562 529]  {CMR10 10}We employ a quantum kinetic equation for the density
  p1-l81  [317 532 562 541]  {CMR10 10}matrix ˆ{CMMI10 10 i}ρ{CMR10 10}, derived from the quantum Liouville equation,
  p1-l82  [317 543 562 553]  {CMR10 10}following the blueprint of Refs. [53, 58]. Our main find-
  p1-l83  [317 555 562 564]  {CMR10 10}ing is that, remarkably, the extrinsic OH-conductance
  p1-l84  [317 566 562 575]  {CMR10 10}overwhelms the intrinsic OH-conductance in doped sys-
  p1-l85  [317 578 562 586]  {CMR10 10}tems and provides the dominant contribution to the OHE
  p1-l86  [317 589 562 598]  {CMR10 10}when the Fermi energy lies in the conduction or valence
  p1-l87  [317 600 562 610]  {CMR10 10}bands. This finding overhauls the conventional interpre-
  p1-l88  [317 612 562 621]  {CMR10 10}tation of experimental data and suggests that what is
  p1-l89  [317 623 562 632]  {CMR10 10}measured experimentally is overwhelmingly the extrin-
  p1-l90  [317 635 562 644]  {CMR10 10}sic contribution. For the model studied here of massive
  p1-l91  [317 646 562 655]  {CMR10 10}Dirac fermions, and assuming short-range impurities, the
  p1-l92  [317 658 562 667]  {CMR10 10}extrinsic contribution is 21 times of the intrinsic one.
  p1-l93  [317 669 562 678]  {CMR10 10}Our central result is summarized in Fig. 1 with the to-
  p1-l94  [317 681 562 691]  {CMR10 10}tal, intrinsic and extrinsic OH-conductance{CMMI10 10 i} σ{CMR7 7}OH{CMR10 10} vs Fermi
  p1-l95  [317 692 562 701]  {CMR10 10}energy plotted separately. The orbital Hall current op-
  p1-l96  [317 702 562 716]  {CMR10 10}erator ˆ{CMMIB10 10 i}j{CMR10 10} ={CMR7 7} 1 2{CMSY10 10 i} {{CMMIB10 10 i}L{CMR10 10}ˆ{CMMI10 10 i},{CMMIB10 10 i} v{CMR10 10}ˆ{CMSY10 10 i}}{CMR10 10} where{CMMIB10 10 i} L{CMR10 10}ˆ is the OAM operator and
