  p2-l53  [54 466 299 475]  {CMR10 10}the dominance of extrinsic mechanisms reveals the strong
  p2-l54  [54 478 299 487]  {CMR10 10}role played by disorder-induced interband coherence in
  p2-l55  [54 489 299 498]  {CMR10 10}the transport of OAM. The OAM is an interband effect,
  p2-l56  [54 501 299 510]  {CMR10 10}and its intrinsic part is large in systems exhibiting large
  p2-l57  [54 512 299 521]  {CMR10 10}Berry curvatures, in other words, strong intrinsic inter-
  p2-l58  [54 523 299 532]  {CMR10 10}band coherence. On the other hand, skew scattering and
  p2-l59  [54 535 299 544]  {CMR10 10}side jump are sources of interband coherence mediated by
  p2-l60  [54 546 299 555]  {CMR10 10}disorder. Our calculation shows that, in the case of the
  p2-l61  [54 558 299 567]  {CMR10 10}OHE, this disorder-mediated contribution is one order of
  p2-l62  [54 569 299 578]  {CMR10 10}magnitude larger than the intrinsic terms, suggesting the
  p2-l63  [54 581 299 590]  {CMR10 10}best method to maximize the orbital Hall current may be
  p2-l64  [54 592 299 601]  {CMR10 10}to harness extrinsic mechanisms. Importantly, to obtain
  p2-l65  [54 604 299 613]  {CMR10 10}a large OHE it is{CMTI10 10 i} not{CMR10 10} necessary for the Berry curvature
  p2-l66  [54 615 299 624]  {CMR10 10}to be large, a finding that has profound implications for
  p2-l67  [54 626 270 636]  {CMR10 10}the electrical manipulation of magnetic moments.
