  p3-l44  [231 273 299 284]  {CMMI7 7 i}E{CMR10 10} . This contri-
  p3-l45  [54 285 299 293]  {CMR10 10}bution is contained in vertex corrections in the diagram-
  p3-l46  [54 295 299 305]  {CMR10 10}matic formalism [53, 58]. These corrections represent
  p3-l47  [54 307 299 317]  {CMR10 10}spin/pseudospin-dependent scattering commonly termed
  p3-l48  [54 319 299 328]  {CMR10 10}skew scattering and side jump. They are associated with
  p3-l49  [54 330 299 339]  {CMR10 10}the Fermi surface, and occur because the scattering po-
  p3-l50  [54 342 299 351]  {CMR10 10}tential also contributes to band mixing and inter-band
  p3-l51  [54 353 299 362]  {CMR10 10}coherence. In this connection, an alternative interpre-
  p3-l52  [54 365 299 374]  {CMR10 10}tation is that disorder mixes the bands in the crystal,
  p3-l53  [54 376 299 385]  {CMR10 10}giving a correction to the wave function, and this results
  p3-l54  [54 388 299 396]  {CMR10 10}in a correction to the band-expectation values of certain
  p3-l55  [54 399 299 408]  {CMR10 10}physical observables, which is proportional to the disor-
  p3-l56  [54 410 299 420]  {CMR10 10}der strength. At the same time, an electric field shifts the
  p3-l57  [54 422 299 431]  {CMR10 10}Fermi surface away from equilibrium, and this shift is in-
  p3-l58  [54 433 299 442]  {CMR10 10}versely proportional to the disorder strength. The net
  p3-l59  [54 445 299 454]  {CMR10 10}result in the average of physical observables is the prod-
  p3-l60  [54 456 299 465]  {CMR10 10}uct of the correction to the band-expectation value and
  p3-l61  [54 468 299 477]  {CMR10 10}the shift in the distribution function, and this product is
  p3-l62  [54 479 255 488]  {CMR10 10}formally independent of the disorder strength.
  p3-l63  [64 491 299 500]  {CMR10 10}The intrinsic OHE arises from the intrinsic driving
