  p3-l107  [327 55 562 64]  {CMTI10 10 i}Topological antiferromagnets{CMR10 10}. These are important for
  p3-l108  [317 66 562 75]  {CMR10 10}orbital torque applications: i. an external electric field
  p3-l109  [317 78 562 87]  {CMR10 10}can interact directly with orbital degrees of freedom with-
  p3-l110  [317 89 562 98]  {CMR10 10}out requiring the spin-orbit interaction, electrical gener-
  p3-l111  [317 100 562 110]  {CMR10 10}ation of OAM can rely on light atomic elements [60–63],
  p3-l112  [317 112 562 121]  {CMR10 10}widening the material choice for the electrical control of
  p3-l113  [317 123 562 133]  {CMR10 10}magnetism [20, 64, 65]. ii. the absence of spin-orbit cou-
  p3-l114  [317 135 562 145]  {CMR10 10}pling could result in long-lived magnetic information [5],
  p3-l115  [317 147 562 156]  {CMR10 10}the orbital torque is investigated as an alternative to the
  p3-l116  [317 158 562 167]  {CMR10 10}spin torque, which has applications in data storage and
  p3-l117  [317 169 562 179]  {CMR10 10}nonvolatile logic [4, 66, 67]. Topological antiferromagnets
  p3-l118  [317 181 529 191]  {CMR10 10}are described by a tilted Dirac Hamiltonian [19]:
