  p4-l24  [64 275 299 284]  {CMR10 10}The extrinsic OHE, including skew-scattering and side-
  p4-l25  [54 287 299 296]  {CMR10 10}jump, has been considered theoretically by Bernevig{CMTI10 10 i} et al.
  p4-l26  [54 298 299 308]  {CMR10 10}in hole-doped Si [32]. The effect of impurities in the Kubo
  p4-l27  [54 309 299 319]  {CMR10 10}linear response theory of Ref. [32] is twofold: through the
  p4-l28  [54 321 299 330]  {CMR10 10}vertex correction to the current operator and the self-
  p4-l29  [54 333 299 342]  {CMR10 10}energy. Bernevig{CMTI10 10 i} et al.{CMR10 10} showed that vertex corrections
  p4-l30  [54 344 299 353]  {CMR10 10}from impurity scattering vanish for a low-energy model
  p4-l31  [54 355 299 365]  {CMR10 10}for hole-doped Si. However, a recent work [76] shows this
  p4-l32  [54 367 292 376]  {CMR10 10}is not the case in generic inversion symmetric systems.
  p4-l33  [64 387 299 396]  {CMTI10 10 i}Application of the theory.{CMR10 10} Some words are in order re-
  p4-l34  [54 399 299 408]  {CMR10 10}garding the range of applicability of our theory. Firstly,
  p4-l35  [54 410 299 419]  {CMR10 10}since most of the orbital moment contribution comes
  p4-l36  [54 421 299 431]  {CMR10 10}from the valley points [77], we considered the OHE from
  p4-l37  [54 431 299 442]  {CMR10 10}the{CMMI10 10 i} K{CMR10 10} and{CMMI10 10 i} K{CMSY7 7 i}′{CMR10 10} points, without including the contribu-
  p4-l38  [54 445 299 454]  {CMR10 10}tion from the remainder of the Brillouin zone. Secondly,
  p4-l39  [54 456 299 465]  {CMR10 10}we approximate the Fermi-Dirac function as a step func-
  p4-l40  [54 468 299 476]  {CMR10 10}tion which is the zero-temperature limit, whereas orbital
  p4-l41  [54 479 299 488]  {CMR10 10}Hall measurements are performed at room temperature
  p4-l42  [54 490 299 500]  {CMR10 10}i.e. 25 meV [7, 41]. Our main assumption is that the
  p4-l43  [54 501 299 512]  {CMR10 10}fermion system is degenerate, i.e.{CMMI10 10 i} E{CMMI7 7 i}F{CMSY10 10 i} ≫{CMMI10 10 i} k{CMMI7 7 i}B{CMMI10 10 i}T{CMR10 10}. How-
  p4-l44  [54 513 299 522]  {CMR10 10}ever, based on realistic values for{CMMI10 10 i} τ{CMR10 10} , our theory requires
  p4-l45  [54 525 299 534]  {CMR10 10}the Fermi energy to be away from the band edge by at
  p4-l46  [54 536 299 546]  {CMR10 10}least 50 meV in order to satisfy{CMMI10 10 i} E{CMMI7 7 i}F{CMMI10 10 i} τ /{MSBM10 10}ℏ{CMSY10 10 i} ≫{CMR10 10} 1. This is
  p4-l47  [54 548 299 557]  {CMR10 10}twice room temperature: whereas the assumption of de-
  p4-l48  [54 559 299 568]  {CMR10 10}generacy is not perfect, the system is still approximately
  p4-l49  [54 571 299 580]  {CMR10 10}degenerate. Thirdly, we have assumed short-range disor-
  p4-l50  [54 582 299 591]  {CMR10 10}der. For a more generic disorder model, although we do
  p4-l51  [54 593 299 603]  {CMR10 10}not expect the results to change qualitatively, the RHS of
  p4-l52  [317 55 562 65]  {CMR10 10}Eqs. (6) and (7) will depend on the angular characteris-
  p4-l53  [317 66 562 76]  {CMR10 10}tics of the scattering potential. Finally but most impor-
  p4-l54  [317 77 562 88]  {CMR10 10}tantly, the theory requires{CMMI10 10 i} E{CMMI7 7 i}F{CMMI10 10 i} τ /{MSBM10 10}ℏ{CMSY10 10 i} ≫{CMR10 10} 1, which excludes
  p4-l55  [317 89 562 98]  {CMR10 10}the low-density regions in the vicinity of the conduction
  p4-l56  [317 101 562 110]  {CMR10 10}and valence band edges. These regions are virtually im-
  p4-l57  [317 112 562 121]  {CMR10 10}possible to capture by most theoretical approaches. This
  p4-l58  [317 124 562 133]  {CMR10 10}is one of the fundamental paradoxes of transport theory:
  p4-l59  [317 135 562 144]  {CMR10 10}disorder is needed on physical grounds, but it needs to
  p4-l60  [317 146 562 157]  {CMR10 10}satisfy{CMMI10 10 i} E{CMMI7 7 i}F{CMMI10 10 i} τ{CMSY10 10 i} ≫{CMR10 10} 1 (alternatively{CMMI10 10 i} q{CMMI7 7 i}F{CMMI10 10 i} l{CMSY10 10 i} ≫{CMR10 10} 1, where{CMMI10 10 i} l{CMR10 10} is the
  p4-l61  [317 158 562 168]  {CMR10 10}mean free path) for the theory to be applicable. The
  p4-l62  [317 169 562 179]  {CMR10 10}appearance of a term{CMSY10 10 i} ∝{CMMI10 10 i} τ{CMR10 10} (i.e.{CMSY10 10 i} ∝{CMR10 10} 1{CMMI10 10 i}/n{CMMI7 7 i}i{CMR10 10}) is understood
  p4-l63  [317 181 562 190]  {CMR10 10}on physical grounds: it represents the shift in the Fermi
  p4-l64  [317 192 562 202]  {CMR10 10}surface induced by the electric field. The fact that this
  p4-l65  [317 204 562 213]  {CMR10 10}shift is{CMSY10 10 i} ∝{CMMI10 10 i} τ{CMR10 10} reflects the need for disorder to be present so
  p4-l66  [317 215 562 224]  {CMR10 10}as to keep the Fermi surface near equilibrium. If disorder
  p4-l67  [317 227 562 236]  {CMR10 10}is removed entirely and{CMMI10 10 i} τ{CMR10 10} formally tends to infinity this
  p4-l68  [317 238 562 247]  {CMR10 10}would imply that an infinitesimally small electric field
  p4-l69  [317 250 562 259]  {CMR10 10}can shift the Fermi surface infinitely far away from equi-
  p4-l70  [317 261 562 270]  {CMR10 10}librium. Such a process is unphysical, therefore the limit
  p4-l71  [317 272 562 282]  {CMMI10 10 i}τ{CMSY10 10 i} → ∞{CMR10 10} is not captured in this formulation of transport
  p4-l72  [317 284 562 293]  {CMR10 10}theory. The resolution is that the size of the sample now
  p4-l73  [317 296 562 305]  {CMR10 10}needs to be considered and it is assumed that the mean
  p4-l74  [317 307 562 316]  {CMR10 10}free path is larger than the size of the sample, so the sys-
  p4-l75  [317 319 562 328]  {CMR10 10}tem transitions into the Landauer-Buttiker regime. This
  p4-l76  [317 330 562 339]  {CMR10 10}regime can also be captured in our density matrix lan-
  p4-l77  [317 341 562 351]  {CMR10 10}guage [78], but is unlikely to be relevant to the materials
  p4-l78  [317 353 562 362]  {CMR10 10}studied in the present work, where transport is diffusive.
  p4-l79  [317 364 562 374]  {CMR10 10}The opposite limit,{CMMI10 10 i} q{CMMI7 7 i}F{CMSY10 10 i} →{CMR10 10} 0, is equivalent to{CMMI10 10 i} E{CMMI7 7 i}F{CMMI10 10 i} τ{CMSY10 10 i} ≪{CMR10 10} 1,
  p4-l80  [317 376 562 385]  {CMR10 10}which represents the transition to the localised regime.
  p4-l81  [317 387 562 396]  {CMR10 10}The transport theory first needs to be augmented with
  p4-l82  [317 398 562 408]  {CMR10 10}the Cooperon in order to describe weak localisation [79].
  p4-l83  [327 410 562 419]  {CMTI10 10 i}Conclusions{CMR10 10}. We have determined the disorder con-
  p4-l84  [317 422 562 431]  {CMR10 10}tribution to the OHE of 2D massive Dirac fermions and
  p4-l85  [317 433 562 442]  {CMR10 10}shown that it exceeds the intrinsic contribution by an
  p4-l86  [317 445 562 454]  {CMR10 10}order of magnitude, using graphene, transition metal
  p4-l87  [317 456 562 465]  {CMR10 10}dichalcogenides and topological antiferromagnets as pro-
  p4-l88  [317 467 562 477]  {CMR10 10}totypes. The calculation offers an approach to tuning
  p4-l89  [317 479 562 488]  {CMR10 10}and maximizing the orbital torque and can be extended
  p4-l90  [317 490 562 499]  {CMR10 10}to other classes of materials, such as Weyl and Dirac
  p4-l91  [317 502 562 511]  {CMR10 10}semimetals, topological insulators, and van der Waals
  p4-l92  [317 513 562 522]  {CMR10 10}heterostructures, as well as opening future perspectives
  p4-l93  [317 524 539 534]  {CMR10 10}on graphene-based orbitronics and twistronics [80].
