  p1-l21  [167 230 228 244]  {LMSans10-Bold 14.3 b}Summary
  p1-l22  [167 262 553 273]  {LMMono10-Regular 10}seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} is a library for making statistical graphics in Python. It provides a high-level interface
  p1-l23  [167 274 553 285]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}to{LMMono10-Regular 10} matplotlib{LMSans10-Regular 10} and integrates closely with{LMMono10-Regular 10} pandas{LMSans10-Regular 10} data structures. Functions in the{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn
  p1-l24  [167 286 553 296]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}library expose a declarative, dataset-oriented API that makes it easy to translate questions
  p1-l25  [167 298 553 308]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}about data into graphics that can answer them. When given a dataset and a specification
  p1-l26  [167 310 553 320]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}of the plot to make,{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} automatically maps the data values to visual attributes such
  p1-l27  [167 322 553 332]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}as color, size, or style, internally computes statistical transformations, and decorates the plot
  p1-l28  [167 334 553 344]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}with informative axis labels and a legend. Many{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} functions can generate figures with
  p1-l29  [167 346 553 356]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}multiple panels that elicit comparisons between conditional subsets of data or across different
  p1-l30  [167 358 553 368]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}pairings of variables in a dataset.{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} is designed to be useful throughout the lifecycle of
  p1-l31  [167 370 553 380]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}a scientific project. By producing complete graphics from a single function call with minimal
  p1-l32  [167 382 553 392]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}arguments,{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} facilitates rapid prototyping and exploratory data analysis. And by
  p1-l33  [167 394 553 404]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}offering extensive options for customization, along with exposing the underlying{LMMono10-Regular 10} matplotlib
  p1-l34  [167 406 454 416]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}objects, it can be used to create polished, publication-quality figures.
  p1-l35  [167 441 290 455]  {LMSans10-Bold 14.3 b}Statement of need
  p1-l36  [167 473 553 483]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}Data visualization is an indispensable part of the scientific process. Effective visualizations
  p1-l37  [167 485 553 495]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}will allow a scientist both to understand their own data and to communicate their insights to
  p1-l38  [167 497 553 507]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}others (Tukey, 1977). These goals can be furthered by tools for specifying a graph that provide
  p1-l39  [167 509 553 519]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}a good balance between efficiency and flexibility. Within the scientific Python ecosystem, the
  p1-l40  [167 521 553 531]  {LMMono10-Regular 10}matplotlib{LMSans10-Regular 10} (Hunter, 2007) project is very well established, having been under continuous
  p1-l41  [167 533 553 543]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}development for nearly two decades. It is highly flexible, offering fine-grained control over the
  p1-l42  [167 545 553 555]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}placement and visual appearance of objects in a plot. It can be used interactively through GUI
  p1-l43  [167 557 553 567]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}applications, and it can output graphics to a wide range of static formats. Yet its relatively
  p1-l44  [167 569 553 579]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}low-level API can make some common tasks cumbersome to perform. For example, creating
  p1-l45  [167 581 553 590]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}a scatter plot where the marker size represents a numeric variable and the marker shape
  p1-l46  [167 592 553 603]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}represents a categorical variable requires one to transform the size values to graphical units
  p1-l47  [167 604 553 614]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}and to loop over the categorical levels, separately invoking a plotting function for each marker
  p1-l48  [167 616 187 626]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}type.
  p1-l49  [167 634 553 644]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}The{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} library offers an interface to{LMMono10-Regular 10} matplotlib{LMSans10-Regular 10} that permits rapid data exploration
  p1-l50  [167 646 553 656]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}and prototyping of visualizations while retaining much of the flexibility and stability that are
  p1-l51  [167 658 553 668]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}necessary to produce publication-quality graphics. It is domain-general and can be used to
  p1-l52  [167 670 514 680]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}visualize a wide range of datasets that are well-represented within a tabular format.
  p1-l53  [167 704 222 719]  {LMSans10-Bold 14.3 b}Example
  p1-l54  [167 737 553 747]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}The following example demonstrates the creation of a figure with{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10}. The example
  p1-l55  [167 749 553 759]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}makes use of one of the built-in datasets that are provided for documentation and generation of
  p1-l56  [39 780 542 789]  {LMSans8-Regular 8}Waskom, M. L., (2021). seaborn: statistical data visualization.{LMSans8-Oblique 8 i} Journal of Open Source Software{LMSans8-Regular 8}, 6(60), 3021. https://doi.org/10.21105/joss.
  p1-l57  [39 790 60 798]  {LMSans8-Regular 8}03021
  p1-l58  [548 779 553 789]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}1
  p1-i1  [18 12 174 72]  picture
  == page 2  595 x 842  lines 53  pictures 1  drawings 37
  p2-l1  [167 101 553 111]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}reproducible bug reports. It illustrates several of the features described in the Overview section,
  p2-l2  [167 113 553 123]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}including the declarative API, semantic mappings, faceting across subplots, aggregation with
  p2-l3  [167 125 318 135]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}error bars, and visual theme control.
  p2-l4  [167 153 277 163]  {LMMono10-Regular 10}import seaborn as sns
  p2-l5  [167 165 324 175]  {LMMono10-Regular 10}sns.set_theme(context="paper")
  p2-l6  [167 177 329 187]  {LMMono10-Regular 10}fmri = sns.load_dataset("fmri")
  p2-l7  [167 189 251 199]  {LMMono10-Regular 10}g = sns.relplot(
  p2-l8  [188 201 308 211]  {LMMono10-Regular 10}data=fmri, kind="line",
  p2-l9  [188 213 324 223]  {LMMono10-Regular 10}x="timepoint", y="signal",
