  p2-l35  [167 521 553 530]  {LMSans10-Bold 9 b}Figure 1:{LMSans9-Regular 9} An example{LMMono9-Regular 9} seaborn{LMSans9-Regular 9} figure demonstrating some of its key features. The image was
  p2-l36  [167 532 293 541]  {LMSans9-Regular 9}generated using seaborn v0.11.1.
  p2-l37  [167 570 227 584]  {LMSans10-Bold 14.3 b}Overview
  p2-l38  [167 603 553 613]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}Users interface with{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} through a collection of plotting functions that share a common
  p2-l39  [167 615 553 625]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}API for plot specification and offer many more specific options for customization. These
  p2-l40  [167 627 553 637]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}functions range from basic plot types such as scatter and line plots to functions that apply
  p2-l41  [167 639 553 649]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}various transformations and abstractions, such as histogram binning, kernel density estimation,
  p2-l42  [167 651 553 661]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}and regression model fitting. Functions in{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} are classified as either “axes-level” or
  p2-l43  [167 663 553 673]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}“figure-level.” Axes-level functions behave like most plotting functions in the{LMMono10-Regular 10} matplotlib.
  p2-l44  [167 675 553 685]  {LMMono10-Regular 10}pyplot{LMSans10-Regular 10} namespace. By default, they hook into the state machine that tracks a “current”
  p2-l45  [167 686 553 697]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}figure and add a layer to it, but they can also accept a matplotlib axes object to control
  p2-l46  [167 698 553 709]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}where the plot is drawn, similar to using the{LMMono10-Regular 10} matplotlib{LMSans10-Regular 10} “object-oriented” interface. Figure-
  p2-l47  [167 710 553 720]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}level functions create their own figure when invoked, allowing them to “facet” the dataset
  p2-l48  [167 722 553 732]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}by creating multiple conditional subplots, along with adding conveniences such as putting
  p2-l49  [167 734 553 744]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}the legend outside the space of the plot by default. Each figure-level function corresponds
  p2-l50  [167 746 553 756]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}to several axes-level functions that serve similar purposes, with a single parameter selecting
  p2-l51  [39 780 542 789]  {LMSans8-Regular 8}Waskom, M. L., (2021). seaborn: statistical data visualization.{LMSans8-Oblique 8 i} Journal of Open Source Software{LMSans8-Regular 8}, 6(60), 3021. https://doi.org/10.21105/joss.
  p2-l52  [39 790 60 798]  {LMSans8-Regular 8}03021
  p2-l53  [548 779 553 789]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}2
  p2-i1  [18 12 174 72]  picture
  == page 3  595 x 842  lines 56  pictures 1  drawings 1
  p3-l1  [167 101 553 111]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}the kind of plot to make. For example, the{LMMono10-Regular 10} displot{LMSans10-Regular 10} function can produce several different
  p3-l2  [167 113 553 123]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}representations of a distribution, including a histogram, kernel density estimate, or empirical
  p3-l3  [167 125 553 135]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}cumulative distribution function. The figure-level functions make use of a{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} class that
  p3-l4  [167 137 553 147]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}controls the layout of the figure, mediating between the axes-level functions and matplotlib.
  p3-l5  [167 149 549 159]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}These classes are part of the public API and can be used directly for advanced applications.
  p3-l6  [167 166 553 176]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}One of the key features in{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} is that variables in a dataset can be automatically
  p3-l7  [167 178 553 188]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}“mapped” to visual attributes of the graph. These transformations are referred to as “se-
  p3-l8  [167 190 553 200]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}mantic” mappings because they endow the attributes with meaning{LMSans10-Oblique 10 i} vis a vis{LMSans10-Regular 10} the dataset. By
  p3-l9  [167 202 553 212]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}freeing the user from manually specifying the transformations – which often requires looping
  p3-l10  [167 214 553 224]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}and multiple function invocations when using{LMMono10-Regular 10} matplotlib{LMSans10-Regular 10} directly –{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} allows rapid
  p3-l11  [167 226 553 236]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}exploration of multidimensional relationships. To further aid efficiency, the default parameters
  p3-l12  [167 238 553 248]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}of the mappings are opinionated. For example, when mapping the color of the elements in a
  p3-l13  [167 250 553 260]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}plot,{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} infers whether to use a qualitative or quantitative mapping based on whether
  p3-l14  [167 262 553 272]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}the input data are categorical or numeric. This behavior can be further configured or even
  p3-l15  [167 274 456 284]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}overridden by setting additional parameters of each plotting function.
  p3-l16  [167 291 553 301]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}Several{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} functions also apply statistical transformations to the input data before
  p3-l17  [167 303 553 313]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}plotting, ranging from estimating the mean or median to fitting a general linear model. When
  p3-l18  [167 315 553 325]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}data are transformed in this way,{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} automatically computes and shows error bars to
  p3-l19  [167 327 553 337]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}provide a visual cue about the uncertainty of the estimate. Unlike many graphical libraries,
  p3-l20  [167 339 553 349]  {LMMono10-Regular 10}seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} shows 95% confidence interval error bars by default, rather than standard errors. The
  p3-l21  [167 351 553 361]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}confidence intervals are computed with a bootstrap algorithm, allowing them to generalize over
  p3-l22  [167 363 553 373]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}many different statistics, and the default level allows the user to perform “inference by eye”
  p3-l23  [167 375 553 385]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}(Cumming & Finch, 2005). Historically, error bar specification has been relatively limited, but
  p3-l24  [167 387 553 397]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}a forthcoming release (v0.12) will introduce a new configuration system that makes it possible
  p3-l25  [167 398 553 409]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}to show nonparametric percentile intervals and scaled analytic estimates of standard error or
  p3-l26  [167 410 286 420]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}standard deviation statistics.
  p3-l27  [167 428 553 438]  {LMMono10-Regular 10}seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} aims to be flexible about the format of its input data. The most convenient usage
  p3-l28  [167 440 553 450]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}pattern provides a{LMMono10-Regular 10} pandas{LMSans10-Regular 10} (McKinney, 2010) dataframe with variables encoded in a long-
  p3-l29  [167 451 553 462]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}form or “tidy” (Wickham, 2014) format. With this format, columns in the dataframe can
  p3-l30  [167 463 553 474]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}be explicitly assigned to roles in the plot, such as specifying the x and y positions of a
  p3-l31  [167 475 553 485]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}scatterplot along with size and shape semantics. Long-form data supports efficient exploration
  p3-l32  [167 487 553 497]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}and prototyping because variables can be assigned different roles in the plot without modifying
  p3-l33  [167 499 553 510]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}anything about the original dataset. But most{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} functions can also consume and
  p3-l34  [167 511 553 522]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}visualize “wide-form” data, typically producing similar output to how the analogous{LMMono10-Regular 10} matplot
  p3-l35  [167 523 553 534]  {LMMono10-Regular 10}lib{LMSans10-Regular 10} function would interpret a 2D array (e.g., producing a boxplot where each box represents a
  p3-l36  [167 535 553 546]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}column in the dataframe) while making use of the index and column names to label the graph.
  p3-l37  [167 547 553 557]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}Using the label information in a{LMMono10-Regular 10} pandas{LMSans10-Regular 10} object can help make plots that are interpretable
  p3-l38  [167 559 553 569]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}without further tweaking – reducing the chance of interpretive errors – but{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} also
  p3-l39  [167 571 553 582]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}accepts data from a variety of more basic formats, including{LMMono10-Regular 10} numpy{LMSans10-Regular 10} (Harris et al., 2020) arrays
  p3-l40  [167 583 315 593]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}and simple Python collection types.
  p3-l41  [167 600 553 610]  {LMMono10-Regular 10}seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} also offers multiple built-in themes that users can select to modify the visual appear-
  p3-l42  [167 612 553 622]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}ance of their graphs. The themes make use of the{LMMono10-Regular 10} matplotlib rcParams{LMSans10-Regular 10} system, meaning
  p3-l43  [167 624 553 634]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}that they will take effect for any figure created using{LMMono10-Regular 10} matplotlib{LMSans10-Regular 10}, not just those made by
  p3-l44  [167 636 553 646]  {LMMono10-Regular 10}seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10}. The themes are defined by two disjoint sets of parameters that separately control
  p3-l45  [167 648 553 658]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}the style of the figure and the scaling of its elements (such as line widths and font sizes). This
  p3-l46  [167 660 553 670]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}separation makes it easy to generate multiple versions of a figure that are scaled for different
  p3-l47  [167 672 553 682]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}contexts, such as written reports and slide presentations. The theming system can also be
  p3-l48  [167 684 553 694]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}used to set a default color palette. As color is particularly important in data visualization and
  p3-l49  [167 696 553 706]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}no single set of defaults is universally appropriate, every plotting function makes it easy to
  p3-l50  [167 708 553 718]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}choose an alternate categorical palette or continuous gradient mapping that is well-suited for
  p3-l51  [167 720 553 730]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}the particular dataset and plot type. The seaborn documentation contains a tutorial on the
  p3-l52  [167 732 484 742]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}use of color in data visualization to help users make this important decision.
  p3-l53  [167 749 553 759]  {LMMono10-Regular 10}seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} does not aim to completely encapsulate or replace{LMMono10-Regular 10} matplotlib{LMSans10-Regular 10}. Many useful
  p3-l54  [39 780 542 789]  {LMSans8-Regular 8}Waskom, M. L., (2021). seaborn: statistical data visualization.{LMSans8-Oblique 8 i} Journal of Open Source Software{LMSans8-Regular 8}, 6(60), 3021. https://doi.org/10.21105/joss.
  p3-l55  [39 790 60 798]  {LMSans8-Regular 8}03021
  p3-l56  [548 779 553 789]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}3
  p3-i1  [18 12 174 72]  picture
  == page 4  595 x 842  lines 48  pictures 1  drawings 1
  p4-l1  [167 101 553 111]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}graphs can be created through the{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} interface, but more advanced applications –
  p4-l2  [167 113 553 123]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}such as defining composite figures with multiple arbitrary plot types – will require importing
  p4-l3  [167 125 553 135]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}and using{LMMono10-Regular 10} matplotlib{LMSans10-Regular 10} as well. Even when calling only{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} functions, deeper cus-
  p4-l4  [167 137 553 147]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}tomization of the plot appearance is achieved by specifying parameters that are passed-
  p4-l5  [167 149 553 159]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}through to the underlying{LMMono10-Regular 10} matplotlib{LMSans10-Regular 10} functions, and tweaks to the default axis limits,
  p4-l6  [167 161 553 171]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}ticks, and labels are made by calling methods on the{LMMono10-Regular 10} matplotlib{LMSans10-Regular 10} object that axes-level
  p4-l7  [167 173 553 183]  {LMMono10-Regular 10}seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} functions return. This approach is distinct from other statistical graphing sys-
  p4-l8  [167 185 553 195]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}tems, such as{LMMono10-Regular 10} ggplot2{LMSans10-Regular 10} (Wickham, 2016). While{LMMono10-Regular 10} seaborn{LMSans10-Regular 10} offers some similar features
  p4-l9  [167 197 553 207]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}and, in some cases, uses similar terminology to{LMMono10-Regular 10} ggplot2{LMSans10-Regular 10}, it does not implement the for-
  p4-l10  [167 209 553 219]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}mal Grammar of Graphics and cannot be used to produce arbitrary visualizations. Rather, its
  p4-l11  [167 221 553 231]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}aim is to facilitate rapid exploration and prototyping through named functions and opinion-
  p4-l12  [167 233 553 243]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}ated defaults while allowing the user to leverage the considerable flexibility of{LMMono10-Regular 10} matplotlib
  p4-l13  [167 245 553 255]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}to create more domain-specific graphics and to polish figures for publication. An exam-
  p4-l14  [167 257 553 267]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}ple of a successful use of this approach to produce reproducible figures can be found at
  p4-l15  [167 269 549 279]  {LMSans10-Regular 10}https://github.com/WagnerLabPapers/Waskom_PNAS_2017 (Waskom & Wagner, 2017).
  p4-l16  [167 304 292 318]  {LMSans10-Bold 14.3 b}Acknowledgements
