  p1-l43  [167 403 223 423]  {NewCMSans10-Bold 13.9 b}Summary
  p1-l44  [167 431 553 445]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}PyMedPhys is an open-source medical physics library built for Python by a diverse community
  p1-l45  [167 443 555 457]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}that values and prioritizes code sharing, review, continuous improvement, and peer development.
  p1-l46  [167 454 553 469]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}PyMedPhys aims to simplify and enhance both research and clinical work related to medical
  p1-l47  [167 466 553 481]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}physics. It is inspired by the Astropy Project (Astropy Collaboration, 2013); a highly successful
  p1-l48  [167 478 387 493]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}collaborative work of our physics peers in astronomy.
  p1-l49  [167 507 280 527]  {NewCMSans10-Bold 13.9 b}Statement of need
  p1-l50  [167 535 553 549]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}Medical radiation applications are subject to fast-paced technological advancements. This is
  p1-l51  [167 547 553 561]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}particularly true in the field of radiation oncology, where the implementation of increasingly
  p1-l52  [167 559 553 573]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}sophisticated technologies requires increasingly complex processes to maintain the improving
  p1-l53  [167 570 553 585]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}standard of care. To help address this challenge, software tools that improve the quality, safety
  p1-l54  [167 582 554 597]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}and efficiency of clinical tasks are increasingly being developed in-house (Arumugam et al.,
  p1-l55  [167 594 554 609]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}2016; Bakhtiari et al., 2011; Bhagroo et al., 2019; Chan et al., 2015; Edvardsson et al., 2018;
  p1-l56  [167 606 553 621]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}Huang et al., 2021; Inaniwa & Kanematsu, 2018; Keall et al., 2014; Kimura et al., 2021; Kuo
  p1-l57  [167 618 555 633]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}et al., 2020; Latala et al., 2020; Li et al., 2010; Maughan et al., 2019; Skouboe et al., 2019).
  p1-l58  [167 630 553 645]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}Commercial options are often prohibitively expensive or insufficiently tailored to an individual
  p1-l59  [167 642 553 657]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}clinic’s needs. On the other hand, in-house development efforts are often limited to a single
  p1-l60  [167 654 553 669]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}institution. Similar tools that could otherwise be shared are instead “reinvented” in clinics
  p1-l61  [167 666 553 681]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}worldwide on a routine basis. Moreover, individual institutions typically lack the personnel and
  p1-l62  [167 678 553 693]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}resources to incorporate simple aspects of good development practice or to properly maintain
  p1-l63  [167 690 241 705]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}in-house software.
  p1-l64  [167 708 553 723]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}By creating and promoting an open-source repository, PyMedPhys aims to improve the quality
  p1-l65  [167 720 553 734]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}and accessibility of existing software solutions to problems faced across a range of medical
  p1-l66  [167 732 553 746]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}radiation applications, especially those traditionally within the remit of medical physicists. These
  p1-l67  [167 744 553 758]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}solutions can be broadly categorised in two areas: data extraction/conversion of proprietary
  p1-l68  [39 778 543 789]  {NewCMSans08-Book 8}Biggs et al. (2022). PyMedPhys: A community effort to develop an open, Python-based standard library for medical physics applications.{NewCMSans10-BookOblique 8 i} Journal
  p1-l69  [39 787 307 799]  {NewCMSans10-BookOblique 8 i}of Open Source Software{NewCMSans08-Book 8},{NewCMSans10-BookOblique 8 i} 7{NewCMSans08-Book 8}(78), 4555. https://doi.org/10.21105/joss.04555.
  p1-l70  [548 775 553 790]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}1
  p1-i1  [18 12 174 72]  picture
  == page 2  595 x 842  lines 48  pictures 1  drawings 1
  p2-l1  [167 98 553 112]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}formats from a variety of radiotherapy systems, and manipulation of standard radiotherapy data
  p2-l2  [167 110 553 124]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}to perform quality assurance (QA) tasks that are otherwise time-consuming or lack commercial
  p2-l3  [167 122 384 136]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}solutions with the desired flexibility or true function.
  p2-l4  [167 140 553 154]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}Data extraction and conversion currently includes: two treatment planning systems, an oncology
  p2-l5  [167 151 553 166]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}information system, and a linear accelerator vendor family of systems. Data in proprietary
  p2-l6  [167 163 553 178]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}formats from these systems are extracted and converted to allow for integration in a myriad of
  p2-l7  [167 175 553 190]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}applications. Applications that use planning system information include: electron cut-out factor
  p2-l8  [167 187 555 202]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}determination, CT extension, and extraction of dose information for patient QA purposes.
  p2-l9  [167 199 553 214]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}Applications that use the oncology information systems include: clinical dashboards that
  p2-l10  [167 211 555 226]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}summarise data, quality task tracking, and comparison of dose information to planning systems.
  p2-l11  [167 223 553 238]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}Applications that use the linear accelerator data include: patient specific QA analysis against
  p2-l12  [167 235 515 250]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}planning data, and analysis of machine performance such as the Winston-Lutz test.
  p2-l13  [167 253 553 268]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}QA tasks using standard radiotherapy data include: anonymisation, extraction of dose data
  p2-l14  [167 265 553 280]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}for analysis, manipulation of contour files to allow merging or adjustments/scaling of relative
  p2-l15  [167 277 553 292]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}electron density, modifying machine names in plans, and most frequently used, the calculation
  p2-l16  [167 289 553 304]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}of a Gamma index, a widely recognised metric in radiotherapy analysis that quantifies the
  p2-l17  [167 301 553 315]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}difference between measured and calculated dose distributions on a point-by-point basis in
  p2-l18  [167 313 440 327]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}terms of both dose and distance to agreement (DTA) differences.
  p2-l19  [167 331 553 345]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}Many of these tools are in use clinically at affiliated sites, and additionally, aspects of PyMedPhys
  p2-l20  [167 343 553 357]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}are implemented around the world for some applications. Many parties have embraced the
  p2-l21  [167 355 554 369]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}gamma analysis module (Castle et al., 2022; Cronholm et al., 2020; Gajewski et al., 2021;
  p2-l22  [167 367 554 381]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}Galić et al., 2020; Lysakovski et al., 2021; Milan et al., 2019; Pastor-Serrano & Perkó, 2021;
  p2-l23  [167 379 553 393]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}Rodrı́guez et al., 2020; Spezialetti et al., 2021; Tsuneda et al., 2021; Yang et al., 2022), while
  p2-l24  [167 391 554 405]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}implementations of the electron cutout factor module and others (Baltz & Kirsner, 2021;
  p2-l25  [167 403 553 417]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}Douglass & Keal, 2021; Rembish, 2021) have also been reported. Additionally, the work
  p2-l26  [167 414 553 429]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}has been recognized by the European Society for Radiotherapy and Oncology (ESTRO) and
  p2-l27  [167 426 553 441]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}referenced as recommended literature in their 3rd Edition of Core Curriculum for Medical
  p2-l28  [167 438 383 453]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}Physics Experts in Radiotherapy (Bert et al., 2021).
  p2-l29  [167 467 281 487]  {NewCMSans10-Bold 13.9 b}Acknowledgements
  p2-l30  [166 495 553 509]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}We acknowledge the support of all who have contributed to the development of PyMedPhys
  p2-l31  [167 507 227 521]  {NewCMSans10-Book 10}along the way.
